window.CLINICAL_ATLAS = [{"id":"mental-health","title":"Mental health","description":"Twenty-one presentations covering mood, anxiety, trauma, psychosis, dependence and complex risk. Each includes three changed circumstances.","icon":"01","reviewed":"2026-11-11","cases":[{"id":"mh-depression","title":"Depression: first presentation","summary":"Establish severity through symptoms, functioning and context","presentation":"An adult reports six weeks of loss of interest, low mood and reduced functioning. They ask whether medication is the only option.","assessment":["Establish severity through symptoms, functioning and context","Ask directly about suicide, self-harm, intent, access and previous attempts","Check previous elevated mood, psychosis, substances, medicines and physical contributors","Explore prior treatment, support and the patient’s priorities"],"management":[{"step":"1. Formulate together","detail":"Explain the working diagnosis and how it relates to symptoms; distinguish severity from a questionnaire score alone."},{"step":"2. Choose treatment","detail":"For less severe depression discuss guided self-help and psychological options; antidepressants are not routinely first-line unless preferred. More severe illness needs a discussion of psychological and medication options, including combined treatment."},{"step":"3. Prescribe thoughtfully","detail":"If medication is chosen, check suitability, interactions and overdose risk; discuss onset, adverse effects, adherence and withdrawal. Use current prescribing guidance for the actual medicine."},{"step":"4. Make the plan practical","detail":"Address work, financial stress, sleep, activity and support without implying that lifestyle replaces needed treatment. Agree a written safety plan when appropriate."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Depression can affect sleep, energy and concentration as well as mood. We can compare treatment choices and choose a first step that fits how much this is affecting you.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Current suicidal intent","change":"A plan and access to tablets emerge.","action":"Move to immediate safety assessment and emergency or crisis intervention appropriate to danger; do not wait for routine treatment to work.","phrase":"I am glad you told me. We need to arrange help to keep you safe today."},{"label":"Previous overactivity","change":"They describe several days of little sleep and disinhibition.","action":"Assess possible bipolar disorder and seek specialist assessment; do not simply increase an antidepressant.","phrase":"That earlier period changes how we should assess and treat this."},{"label":"Medication declined","change":"The patient prefers psychological treatment.","action":"Explore reasons, offer appropriate therapy and active monitoring; reassess urgency and preferences.","phrase":"We can start with an appropriate talking treatment and agree when to review it."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE CG185 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg185/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not apply an under-30 review rule or diagnose severity using PHQ-9 alone."],"followUp":"Review treatment response and safety within an agreed interval. After starting an antidepressant, usual initial review is within 2 weeks, or 1 week for ages 18–25 or particular suicide concern; urgent risk needs earlier action.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["depression","first","presentation"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Move to immediate safety assessment and emergency or crisis intervention appropriate to danger; do not wait for routine treatment to work. I am glad you told me. We need to arrange help to keep you safe today.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-minority-stress","title":"Depression with rejection and minority stress","summary":"Assess depressive symptoms, function and immediate safety","presentation":"An adult feels isolated after family rejection related to sexuality and beliefs. They worry that discussing this will lead to judgement.","assessment":["Assess depressive symptoms, function and immediate safety","Ask which relationships are safe and supportive","Explore discrimination, housing, financial dependence and coercion","Ask what support the patient wants without assumptions"],"management":[{"step":"1. Validate","detail":"Acknowledge the impact of rejection without treating identity or beliefs as an illness."},{"step":"2. Treat the clinical problem","detail":"Offer depression treatment matched to severity and preference; check prior response and adverse effects."},{"step":"3. Find safe support","detail":"Explore trusted friends and inclusive support services. Family work is optional and only appropriate if wanted and safe."},{"step":"4. Maintain privacy","detail":"Agree safe contact and consent for any information sharing; assess safeguarding when relevant."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"You deserve respectful care. I would like to understand how this has affected you and what support feels safe, rather than assume involving your family would help.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Unsafe family contact","change":"Family members monitor messages.","action":"Agree safe contact and assess coercion; do not send revealing information.","phrase":"How can we contact you without putting you at risk?"},{"label":"Existing medicine ineffective","change":"Treatment has not helped.","action":"Review adherence, duration, diagnosis and comorbidity before changing treatment.","phrase":"Let us check what has and has not changed before choosing the next step."},{"label":"Support desired","change":"They want to repair a relationship.","action":"Explore patient-led support, boundaries and counselling without promising reconciliation.","phrase":"We can discuss support for that conversation if it is safe and something you want."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not assume that sexuality, religion or lack of religion determines symptoms, preferences or available support."],"followUp":"Agree early review of mood, safety and access to therapy; use antidepressant-specific monitoring when medication changes.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["depression","with","rejection","and"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Agree safe contact and assess coercion; do not send revealing information. How can we contact you without putting you at risk?","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-sleep","title":"Low mood and a sleeping-tablet request","summary":"Distinguish sleep-onset and maintenance problems and duration","presentation":"A worker reports persistent insomnia, stress and declining mood. They ask for a quick prescription and operate machinery.","assessment":["Distinguish sleep-onset and maintenance problems and duration","Assess mood, suicide risk, mania, alcohol, drugs and sedating medicines","Ask about shift work, caffeine, sleep apnoea symptoms and restless legs","Explore what relief the patient hopes to gain"],"management":[{"step":"1. Identify causes","detail":"Assess depression, anxiety, substance effects and physical contributors rather than treating insomnia in isolation."},{"step":"2. Compare options","detail":"Discuss appropriate psychological treatment, including CBT-based approaches to insomnia and mood where indicated; agree manageable sleep routines."},{"step":"3. Make prescribing safe","detail":"Do not use a hypnotic as an automatic response. Check interactions, dependence, alertness, driving and work risks before any individualised medicine decision."},{"step":"4. Address stress","detail":"Offer practical support for work or caring pressures and arrange follow-up."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"I want to help you sleep, and we also need to understand what is keeping the problem going. Any medicine needs to be safe with your work and anything else you take.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Heavy alcohol use","change":"Morning shaking and regular alcohol emerge.","action":"Assess dependence and withdrawal; avoid adding sedation and arrange supported care.","phrase":"Combining alcohol and sleeping tablets could be dangerous."},{"label":"Little need for sleep","change":"They feel energised after very little sleep.","action":"Assess possible mania and arrange urgent specialist review if suspected.","phrase":"Feeling unusually energised changes what we need to check."},{"label":"Safety-critical work","change":"They drive professionally.","action":"Discuss alertness and current fitness-to-drive requirements; arrange practical occupational support.","phrase":"We need a plan that also keeps you safe at work."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE CG113 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg113/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG215 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng215/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not prescribe mirtazapine while concealing that it is an antidepressant, or promise a sedative is safe because the course is short."],"followUp":"Agree a specific review of sleep, mood, functioning and adverse effects; assess urgent risk immediately.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["low","mood","and","a"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Assess dependence and withdrawal; avoid adding sedation and arrange supported care. Combining alcohol and sleeping tablets could be dangerous.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-palliative","title":"Depression and distress in advanced illness","summary":"Clarify pain, delirium, breathlessness and other distressing symptoms","presentation":"A person with advanced cancer asks for extra sedating medication and says they no longer see a point in living. Pain and loneliness may be contributing.","assessment":["Clarify pain, delirium, breathlessness and other distressing symptoms","Ask directly about suicidal intent, plans, access and any ingestion","Explore goals, understanding of illness, bereavement and support","Review actual medicines, supply, renal function and care plan"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess safety today","detail":"A request for extra medicine with hopelessness needs direct assessment; emergency care is required for overdose or immediate danger."},{"step":"2. Relieve suffering","detail":"Coordinate symptom assessment and treatment with palliative clinicians. Do not withhold appropriate analgesia because suicide is being assessed."},{"step":"3. Coordinate support","detail":"Involve community nursing, palliative care and mental health services according to need; agree safe prescribing and storage collaboratively."},{"step":"4. Plan continuity","detail":"Address loneliness, carer burden and preferred care setting. Discuss future-care planning separately from immediate crisis management."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Your pain and distress both deserve help. I want to understand what you mean about not wanting to continue, and make sure we treat your symptoms safely.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Active plan","change":"They intend to take the available medicine.","action":"Arrange immediate emergency/crisis assessment and safe support; address means access without abandoning symptom relief.","phrase":"We need help now, and I will stay involved while it is arranged."},{"label":"New confusion","change":"The change is sudden and fluctuating.","action":"Assess possible delirium and reversible causes urgently rather than labelling it depression.","phrase":"This sudden change may have a physical cause we need to assess."},{"label":"Loneliness without intent","change":"They deny intent but feel unsupported.","action":"Assess the full picture and arrange active psychosocial and palliative follow-up; do not declare safety from denial alone.","phrase":"Let us make a practical plan for support as well as symptoms."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG225 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng225/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not treat a DNACPR discussion as the response to current suicidal intent."],"followUp":"Match follow-up to immediate risk and symptom severity; confirm who will contact the patient today and out of hours.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["depression","and","distress","in"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange immediate emergency/crisis assessment and safe support; address means access without abandoning symptom relief. We need help now, and I will stay involved while it is arranged.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-adjustment","title":"Low mood after relationship breakdown","summary":"Assess duration, severity, function and suicide risk","presentation":"An adult has become tearful and unable to concentrate after separation. They request a letter for work.","assessment":["Assess duration, severity, function and suicide risk","Distinguish an understandable stress response from depressive illness without minimising either","Explore alcohol, sleep, housing, finances and dependants","Check domestic abuse or coercion when indicated"],"management":[{"step":"1. Validate","detail":"Acknowledge the loss and disruption, and ask what is hardest now."},{"step":"2. Formulate","detail":"Avoid diagnosing major depression solely because a stressor is present, or excluding it because there is a stressor."},{"step":"3. Support","detail":"Agree practical help, structured routine, trusted support and appropriate psychological care; consider a fit note based on functional assessment."},{"step":"4. Review","detail":"Arrange active monitoring and reassess if symptoms persist, worsen or meet criteria for depression."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"What has happened sounds painful. We can support you with the practical pressures and assess whether there is a depression that needs specific treatment.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Immediate self-harm risk","change":"The patient has made preparations.","action":"Arrange urgent or emergency assessment according to danger.","phrase":"Your safety needs our attention today."},{"label":"Coercive relationship","change":"They fear a partner’s reaction.","action":"Check safe communication and safeguarding needs; avoid unsafe joint counselling.","phrase":"Is it safe for us to discuss this and contact you?"},{"label":"Symptoms persistent","change":"Function and mood continue to deteriorate.","action":"Reassess diagnosis, severity and treatment needs rather than repeatedly attributing symptoms to stress.","phrase":"The persistence means we should review the treatment plan."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG225 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng225/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not use a recent life event as proof that depression is absent."],"followUp":"Agree a short, explicit review interval based on function and safety; provide an earlier deterioration route.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["low","mood","after","relationship"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange urgent or emergency assessment according to danger. Your safety needs our attention today.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-antenatal","title":"Depression during pregnancy","summary":"Assess mood, safety, prior severe episodes and bipolar symptoms","presentation":"A pregnant patient has stopped an effective antidepressant after discovering the pregnancy and is becoming unwell.","assessment":["Assess mood, safety, prior severe episodes and bipolar symptoms","Establish pregnancy stage, medication, dose and timing of cessation","Explore fears and previous treatment response","Assess obstetric, social and safeguarding context"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess relapse and withdrawal","detail":"Clarify the course since stopping; significant deterioration requires prompt specialist support."},{"step":"2. Discuss balanced risks","detail":"Compare untreated illness, relapse and medication exposure using current perinatal prescribing advice; avoid blanket reassurance or blanket stopping."},{"step":"3. Coordinate","detail":"Seek specialist perinatal input where indicated and involve maternity care with consent; agree a named coordinating clinician."},{"step":"4. Offer treatment","detail":"Discuss suitable psychological and medication options according to severity, history and preferences; plan postpartum care too."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"We need to weigh the effects of the illness and stopping treatment as well as the medicine itself. Let us make that decision with the right pregnancy-specific advice.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Severe illness","change":"Psychosis or inability to remain safe emerges.","action":"Arrange immediate specialist or emergency help; routine referral is insufficient.","phrase":"We need urgent specialist assessment today."},{"label":"Stable on treatment","change":"The medicine is working and no acute issue is present.","action":"Review benefits and pregnancy-specific risks; do not abruptly stop solely because of pregnancy.","phrase":"We can make an informed plan rather than stop automatically."},{"label":"Breastfeeding planned","change":"They ask about feeding and treatment.","action":"Use medicine-specific lactation advice and develop a postpartum monitoring plan.","phrase":"The choice can take your feeding wishes into account."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG192 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not describe any antidepressant as universally safe or universally prohibited in pregnancy."],"followUp":"Agree risk-adjusted perinatal follow-up and a shared antenatal/postnatal care plan, including who monitors medication changes.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["depression","during","pregnancy"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange immediate specialist or emergency help; routine referral is insufficient. We need urgent specialist assessment today.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-postnatal","title":"Postnatal depression and isolation","summary":"Assess symptoms, duration, functioning and ability to care","presentation":"A new parent has persistent low mood, guilt and poor sleep, with limited family support. They worry their baby will be removed if they disclose symptoms.","assessment":["Assess symptoms, duration, functioning and ability to care","Ask directly about self-harm, infant safety, intrusive thoughts and intent","Screen for mania, delusions, hallucinations and confusion","Assess birth trauma, feeding, support, domestic safety and physical illness"],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain","detail":"Differentiate transient early emotional changes from persistent depression requiring assessment."},{"step":"2. Treat","detail":"Offer appropriate psychological and/or medication treatment matched to severity and feeding considerations; use perinatal advice where needed."},{"step":"3. Support the family","detail":"Coordinate health visitor, maternity and perinatal services; assess practical help and the parent–infant relationship sensitively."},{"step":"4. Plan safety","detail":"Explain confidentiality honestly, identify urgent warning signs and arrange a named review."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Telling me is a way of getting support. I want to understand how you and your baby are doing, including whether the thoughts feel unwanted or whether you feel you might act on them.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Psychotic symptoms","change":"Delusions, confusion or unusually elevated mood appear.","action":"Suspected postpartum psychosis needs immediate specialist assessment within 4 hours of referral; emergency help if immediate danger.","phrase":"This needs urgent specialist help now."},{"label":"Intrusive unwanted thoughts","change":"They are distressed by thoughts with no desire to act.","action":"Assess carefully rather than equating intrusive thoughts automatically with intent; arrange appropriate treatment and support.","phrase":"Help me understand what the thought means to you."},{"label":"No practical support","change":"They cannot rest or access appointments.","action":"Arrange feasible support and accessible follow-up; do not make advice depend on unavailable help.","phrase":"Let us identify support you can actually use."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG192 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not assume every tired new parent has depression or that intrusive thoughts automatically mean intent to harm."],"followUp":"Agree early review matched to safety and functioning, coordinated with perinatal and health-visiting teams; escalate new psychotic symptoms immediately.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["postnatal","depression","and","isolation"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Suspected postpartum psychosis needs immediate specialist assessment within 4 hours of referral; emergency help if immediate danger. This needs urgent specialist help now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-postpartum-psychosis","title":"Postpartum psychosis: recognising an emergency","summary":"Assess immediate danger to parent and baby","presentation":"Ten days after delivery, a parent has hardly slept, appears confused and believes the baby has special powers. A partner requests sleeping tablets.","assessment":["Assess immediate danger to parent and baby","Establish onset, sleep, mood, psychotic symptoms and capacity to engage","Check medical causes, medicines and substances without delaying referral","Confirm location, support and safe supervision"],"management":[{"step":"1. Escalate immediately","detail":"Refer for immediate specialist mental health assessment, preferably perinatal, within 4 hours of referral. If there is immediate danger, use emergency services now."},{"step":"2. Protect safely","detail":"Arrange safe support for parent and baby while help is mobilised; do not leave the family to manage an unsafe situation alone."},{"step":"3. Assess physical needs","detail":"Coordinate urgent medical assessment for confusion or possible physical illness as needed."},{"step":"4. Handover","detail":"Share clinical facts, timing, safety concerns and relevant history directly with receiving services; confirm the plan has been accepted."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"This sudden change is a medical emergency. It is treatable, but sleeping tablets alone are not the right response. We need specialist assessment now.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Immediate threat","change":"The parent threatens harm.","action":"Activate emergency help immediately; do not wait for the 4-hour maximum.","phrase":"We need emergency help at your location now."},{"label":"No previous illness","change":"There is no psychiatric history.","action":"Do not downgrade urgency; postpartum psychosis can be a first episode.","phrase":"This can happen even without previous mental illness."},{"label":"Physical instability","change":"Fever or reduced consciousness appears.","action":"Arrange emergency medical assessment alongside psychiatric care.","phrase":"We need to assess physical causes urgently as well."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG192 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not call this routine postnatal depression or wait for a routine appointment."],"followUp":"Confirm immediate handover and supervision; subsequent treatment and follow-up are specialist-led with coordinated family and primary-care support.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["postpartum","psychosis","recognising","an"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Activate emergency help immediately; do not wait for the 4-hour maximum. We need emergency help at your location now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-gad","title":"Generalised anxiety: persistent worry","summary":"Assess duration, breadth of worry and functional impairment","presentation":"An adult describes months of hard-to-control worry, tension and poor sleep affecting work. They request diazepam.","assessment":["Assess duration, breadth of worry and functional impairment","Assess depression, self-harm, substances and physical contributors","Review previous therapy and medication response","Explore barriers and preferences"],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain","detail":"Discuss the working diagnosis and the worry–avoidance cycle in plain language."},{"step":"2. Use stepped care","detail":"Begin with education and appropriate low-intensity interventions when suitable; marked impairment or inadequate response warrants high-intensity CBT, applied relaxation or medication according to preference."},{"step":"3. Prescribe safely","detail":"If using an SSRI, check contraindications, interactions, licensing, activation and withdrawal; explain monitoring."},{"step":"4. Avoid default sedation","detail":"Benzodiazepines are not routine GAD treatment; exceptional short crisis use requires specific assessment and a clear plan."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"We can treat the worry rather than just sedate you. Let us compare therapy and medicine options and choose something you can sustain.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Marked impairment","change":"They can no longer work or manage daily tasks.","action":"Offer higher-intensity treatment and consider specialist involvement according to complexity.","phrase":"The impact means a more active treatment plan is needed."},{"label":"Alcohol coping","change":"They use alcohol to manage anxiety.","action":"Assess dependence and avoid unsafe sedative combinations; coordinate treatment.","phrase":"We need a plan that addresses both problems safely."},{"label":"First treatment fails","change":"Symptoms persist despite an adequate attempt.","action":"Review diagnosis, engagement and adverse effects, then discuss another recommended option or specialist input.","phrase":"Let us work out why the first approach did not help."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG113 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg113/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not make diazepam the routine quick fix for chronic worry."],"followUp":"Review medication efficacy and adverse effects regularly, particularly early in treatment; agree therapy access and risk-adjusted follow-up.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["generalised","anxiety","persistent","worry"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Offer higher-intensity treatment and consider specialist involvement according to complexity. The impact means a more active treatment plan is needed.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-panic","title":"Panic attacks with physical symptoms","summary":"Assess the current episode for cardiac, respiratory and other acute causes","presentation":"An adult reports episodes of racing heart, breathlessness and fear. They believe anxiety explains a new episode of chest pain.","assessment":["Assess the current episode for cardiac, respiratory and other acute causes","Clarify triggers, onset, duration, avoidance and anticipatory anxiety","Review caffeine, substances, medicines and physical history","Assess depression and safety"],"management":[{"step":"1. Triage first","detail":"Do not use a previous anxiety diagnosis to exclude physical illness; urgent symptoms need urgent assessment."},{"step":"2. Explain after assessment","detail":"When appropriate, explain panic symptoms and the cycle of fear without dismissing the physical experience."},{"step":"3. Treat recurrent panic","detail":"Discuss CBT and appropriate antidepressant options according to the guideline and patient preference."},{"step":"4. Plan coping and review","detail":"Agree non-harmful coping methods, reduced stimulant exposure, access to therapy and a clear plan for changed symptoms."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Panic can cause powerful physical symptoms. I also need to assess anything new or different so we do not assume every episode is anxiety.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Persistent chest pressure","change":"Sweating and ongoing chest discomfort occur.","action":"Arrange emergency assessment for a possible acute cardiac problem.","phrase":"This pattern needs emergency help now."},{"label":"Avoidance","change":"They have stopped leaving home.","action":"Assess agoraphobia and functional impact; arrange accessible CBT and treatment.","phrase":"We can make the treatment accessible while working on avoidance."},{"label":"Sedative request","change":"They request a benzodiazepine for every attack.","action":"Explain that benzodiazepines are not recommended treatment for panic disorder and offer evidence-based alternatives.","phrase":"I want an approach that improves the attacks over time."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG113 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg113/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not advise paper-bag rebreathing or use benzodiazepines as standard panic treatment."],"followUp":"Agree review of attack frequency, avoidance and treatment effects; specify reassessment for new or different physical symptoms.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["panic","attacks","with","physical"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange emergency assessment for a possible acute cardiac problem. This pattern needs emergency help now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-young-person","title":"Young person with anxiety, low mood and school avoidance","summary":"Hear the young person directly and offer private discussion","presentation":"A parent requests antidepressants for a 15-year-old who is not attending school. The young person has not yet spoken privately.","assessment":["Hear the young person directly and offer private discussion","Assess self-harm, suicide, bullying, abuse and exploitation","Explore development, family, school, substances and physical health","Assess function, eating, sleep and existing supports"],"management":[{"step":"1. Engage","detail":"Explain confidentiality and its limits in age-appropriate language; include the young person in decisions."},{"step":"2. Assess needs","detail":"Distinguish anxiety, depression and other causes; assess safeguarding and immediate risk."},{"step":"3. Coordinate age-appropriate care","detail":"Use appropriate child mental-health pathways and psychological interventions. Do not apply adult antidepressant prescribing pathways automatically."},{"step":"4. Support function","detail":"Agree school and family support with consent and safety considerations; arrange active follow-up during waits."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"I would like to hear how things feel for you, and give you some time to talk privately. We can then agree what support would help at home and school.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Self-harm disclosure","change":"They have recently taken tablets.","action":"Arrange emergency medical care and age-appropriate psychosocial assessment; do not wait for routine CAMHS.","phrase":"We need to make sure you are medically safe now."},{"label":"Parent dominates","change":"The parent answers every question.","action":"Create a safe opportunity for direct and private discussion while respecting relevant consent frameworks.","phrase":"I ask all young people some questions on their own."},{"label":"Medication requested","change":"The parent wants an adult relative’s SSRI.","action":"Do not share or copy another person’s prescription; seek appropriate specialist assessment for depression treatment.","phrase":"The right treatment needs to be chosen specifically for them."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG134 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng134/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG225 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng225/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not let a long waiting list substitute for a safety plan and ongoing clinical review."],"followUp":"Name a clinician for interim review and escalation; frequency depends on safety, functioning and treatment, with close monitoring if specialist medication is initiated.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["young","person","with","anxiety,"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange emergency medical care and age-appropriate psychosocial assessment; do not wait for routine CAMHS. We need to make sure you are medically safe now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-psychosis","title":"First-episode psychosis and a request for sleep medication","summary":"Assess command hallucinations, risk, self-neglect and vulnerability","presentation":"A young adult hears voices, suspects neighbours are watching them and has slept poorly. They ask for sleeping tablets and plan to travel.","assessment":["Assess command hallucinations, risk, self-neglect and vulnerability","Clarify symptoms, onset, insight, function and mood","Assess substances, medicines and physical causes","Identify support, location and ability to engage"],"management":[{"step":"1. Refer promptly","detail":"Refer without delay to early intervention in psychosis; use crisis or emergency services when risk or severity requires."},{"step":"2. Assess broadly","detail":"Arrange physical and mental-state assessment and targeted tests without delaying specialist involvement."},{"step":"3. Avoid unsupervised initiation","detail":"Do not start an antipsychotic for a first sustained presentation in primary care without consultation with a consultant psychiatrist."},{"step":"4. Support safely","detail":"Discuss trusted support, practical needs and travel risks; use clear, non-confrontational language and arrange direct handover."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"I can see these experiences feel real and frightening. I do not share your explanation yet, but I do want to help you feel safe and arrange a specialist assessment promptly.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Command voices","change":"Voices urge harm and the patient feels compelled.","action":"Assess immediate danger and arrange urgent crisis or emergency help.","phrase":"We need help now to keep you and others safe."},{"label":"Substance use","change":"Cannabis or stimulants are involved.","action":"Assess intoxication and physical risk; do not assume substance involvement removes the need for psychosis assessment.","phrase":"We should assess both the substance effects and these experiences."},{"label":"Travel imminent","change":"They intend to fly alone tomorrow.","action":"Discuss postponement and safe continuity; urgency is determined by current illness and risk.","phrase":"Travelling could make getting help much harder just now."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG178 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg178/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not agree with delusions, argue aggressively, or treat sleep alone while delaying referral."],"followUp":"Confirm specialist acceptance, immediate safety arrangements and primary-care contact; subsequent follow-up is coordinated with the treating team.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["first-episode","psychosis","and","a"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Assess immediate danger and arrange urgent crisis or emergency help. We need help now to keep you and others safe.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-relapse","title":"Schizophrenia: stopping medication because of adverse effects","summary":"Assess relapse symptoms, risk and ability to care for self","presentation":"An adult with schizophrenia stopped treatment because of distressing side effects and is becoming suspicious again.","assessment":["Assess relapse symptoms, risk and ability to care for self","Establish medicine, last dose, reasons for stopping and previous relapse","Check substances, physical illness and adverse-effect severity","Review the crisis plan, team contacts and support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Respond to the reason","detail":"Acknowledge adverse effects and assess whether urgent medical treatment is needed."},{"step":"2. Contact the team","detail":"Use the existing crisis plan and seek prompt specialist advice for relapse; escalate according to severity."},{"step":"3. Restart safely","detail":"Do not automatically restart an old full dose. Some medicines require special restart procedures and monitoring; coordinate with the treating specialist."},{"step":"4. Plan ongoing care","detail":"Agree medicine review, physical-health monitoring, support and a reliable contact route."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"The side effects matter, and stopping has also left you unwell. Let us involve your team so we can address both safely rather than simply tell you to take the same tablets.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Clozapine interruption","change":"The stopped medicine is clozapine.","action":"Seek urgent specialist advice on restart and monitoring; do not resume the previous dose without instruction.","phrase":"This medicine needs a specific safe restart plan."},{"label":"Severe self-neglect","change":"They are not eating or maintaining safety.","action":"Arrange urgent crisis assessment and physical care as indicated.","phrase":"The immediate priority is getting you assessed and supported."},{"label":"Stable but unhappy","change":"There are no relapse signs yet.","action":"Arrange a collaborative specialist medication review before deterioration occurs.","phrase":"We can review the side effects while protecting your stability."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG178 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg178/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not assume every antipsychotic can be restarted at its previous dose after an interruption."],"followUp":"Confirm team communication and an early review of symptoms, adverse effects and the agreed prescribing plan.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["schizophrenia","stopping","medication","because"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Seek urgent specialist advice on restart and monitoring; do not resume the previous dose without instruction. This medicine needs a specific safe restart plan.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-mania","title":"Possible mania behind reduced sleep","summary":"Assess mood elevation, disinhibition, psychosis and function","presentation":"An adult requests help for sleep but is unusually energised, spending excessively and talking rapidly after several nights with little sleep.","assessment":["Assess mood elevation, disinhibition, psychosis and function","Ask about danger, exploitation, spending, driving and dependants","Check medicines, antidepressants, substances and physical causes","Establish previous episodes and collateral history with appropriate consent"],"management":[{"step":"1. Recognise","detail":"Distinguish reduced need for sleep from exhausting insomnia; consider mania or hypomania."},{"step":"2. Escalate","detail":"Suspected mania requires urgent specialist assessment, with emergency care for immediate danger or severe physical compromise."},{"step":"3. Reduce harm","detail":"Address driving, finances, exploitation and safe support without confrontation or unsupported guarantees."},{"step":"4. Coordinate treatment","detail":"Medication changes should follow urgent specialist advice and current safety requirements; do not simply increase an antidepressant."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"The unusually high energy and reduced need for sleep make me concerned about a change in mood that needs urgent specialist assessment.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Psychosis","change":"Grandiose beliefs and unsafe behaviour emerge.","action":"Increase urgency and arrange crisis or emergency assessment according to immediate danger.","phrase":"We need help today to keep things safe."},{"label":"Antidepressant started","change":"Symptoms followed a prescription change.","action":"Review urgently with specialist input; assess possible treatment-associated mania.","phrase":"That medicine change is important to the assessment."},{"label":"Past brief episodes","change":"They report earlier periods of overactivity.","action":"Arrange specialist diagnostic assessment even if currently settled, with urgency matched to risk.","phrase":"Your previous experiences help us choose safer treatment."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG185 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg185/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not diagnose bipolar disorder from a questionnaire alone or treat possible mania as ordinary anxiety."],"followUp":"Arrange direct handover and confirm an immediate safety plan; ongoing monitoring and diagnosis are specialist-led.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["possible","mania","behind","reduced"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Increase urgency and arrange crisis or emergency assessment according to immediate danger. We need help today to keep things safe.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-ptsd","title":"PTSD after trauma","summary":"Ask permission and assess symptoms without requiring a detailed trauma narrative","presentation":"An adult has nightmares, avoidance and intrusive memories after an assault. They fear being forced to retell every detail at the first appointment.","assessment":["Ask permission and assess symptoms without requiring a detailed trauma narrative","Assess current safety, ongoing threat and self-harm","Explore dissociation, substances, mood and function","Consider physical injury, social needs and previous treatment"],"management":[{"step":"1. Establish safety","detail":"Address ongoing danger, urgent risk and practical instability."},{"step":"2. Explain","detail":"Describe trauma-related symptoms sensitively and let the person control the pace of discussion."},{"step":"3. Offer treatment","detail":"Discuss appropriate trauma-focused CBT or EMDR according to timing, presentation and preference; medication can be considered for adults preferring it after discussion."},{"step":"4. Enable access","detail":"Adapt the setting and communication, address barriers and arrange a follow-up route."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"You do not have to give me every detail today. I want to understand the effects on you and help you access treatment at a pace that feels manageable.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Alcohol use","change":"Alcohol is used to suppress memories.","action":"Assess dependence and withdrawal and coordinate support; do not exclude PTSD treatment solely for substance misuse.","phrase":"We can address both together."},{"label":"Ongoing threat","change":"The perpetrator still has access.","action":"Prioritise safety and safeguarding alongside treatment planning.","phrase":"Your present safety comes first."},{"label":"Traumatic birth","change":"Symptoms follow a difficult delivery.","action":"Use perinatal and trauma pathways and assess parent–infant needs.","phrase":"We can arrange support that understands both the trauma and new parenthood."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG116 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng116/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE CG192 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not require detailed retelling or offer compulsory single-session psychological debriefing."],"followUp":"Agree review of safety, access to therapy and functioning; coordinate care where substance use, severe symptoms or perinatal needs complicate treatment.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["ptsd","after","trauma"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Assess dependence and withdrawal and coordinate support; do not exclude PTSD treatment solely for substance misuse. We can address both together.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-bullying","title":"Bullying, disability and deteriorating mental health","summary":"Assess mood, self-harm, sleep and function","presentation":"An adult with a long-term condition describes workplace humiliation and worsening mood. They ask whether the GP can solve the dispute.","assessment":["Assess mood, self-harm, sleep and function","Clarify specific events, current threat and desired outcome","Explore disability-related needs and workplace support","Assess financial pressure and safe social support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Treat health needs","detail":"Address the mental and physical health effects and immediate safety."},{"step":"2. Support function","detail":"Consider a fit note based on clinical function and discuss possible occupational-health input and reasonable adjustments."},{"step":"3. Explain boundaries","detail":"Provide factual medical information with consent; do not promise a legal finding or outcome."},{"step":"4. Agree practical steps","detail":"Discuss appropriate workplace, union or advice routes and psychological care according to need."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"What has happened is affecting your health. I can help with treatment and the impact on work, and provide relevant medical information with your consent.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Self-harm","change":"They disclose current suicidal intent.","action":"Arrange immediate safety assessment and escalation; the workplace dispute becomes secondary today.","phrase":"We need to focus on keeping you safe now."},{"label":"Employer asks for records","change":"A manager requests the whole record.","action":"Clarify consent, purpose and proportionate disclosure under professional guidance.","phrase":"We should agree exactly what information is relevant."},{"label":"Financial crisis","change":"They feel unable to take time off.","action":"Discuss feasible workplace changes and practical support rather than assume absence is possible.","phrase":"Let us make a plan you can realistically use."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG222 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng222/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"},{"label":"NICE NG225 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng225/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not promise that a GP letter proves discrimination or guarantees adjustments."],"followUp":"Arrange review of health, functioning, treatment and practical support; reassess risk if work circumstances worsen.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["bullying,","disability","and","deteriorating"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange immediate safety assessment and escalation; the workplace dispute becomes secondary today. We need to focus on keeping you safe now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-dependence","title":"Long-term benzodiazepine use and an early refill","summary":"Establish actual dose, last use, remaining supply and withdrawal symptoms","presentation":"An adult taking diazepam regularly has used extra tablets during stress and fears being cut off.","assessment":["Establish actual dose, last use, remaining supply and withdrawal symptoms","Assess sedation, alcohol, opioids and other medicines","Ask about overdose, self-harm and underlying anxiety","Review previous reduction attempts and prescriber records"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess immediate danger","detail":"Severe withdrawal, seizures, respiratory depression or overdose require urgent or emergency care."},{"step":"2. Verify","detail":"Reconcile prescribing and actual use with the usual clinician and pharmacy; prevent conflicting supplies."},{"step":"3. Plan collaboratively","detail":"Avoid punitive language and abrupt discontinuation. If reducing, use an individualised gradual process adjusted to symptoms and clinical risk."},{"step":"4. Treat underlying needs","detail":"Offer appropriate anxiety or insomnia care and agree clear monitoring and prescribing responsibility."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"I will not leave you without a plan. We need to check what you have taken and how you feel, then agree a safe way forward with your regular prescriber.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Seizure","change":"They have had a seizure after running out.","action":"Arrange emergency assessment; a routine taper discussion is insufficient.","phrase":"You need urgent medical assessment now."},{"label":"Alcohol co-use","change":"They drink heavily with the medicine.","action":"Assess respiratory and withdrawal risks and coordinate supported management.","phrase":"The combination changes what is safe today."},{"label":"Withdrawal symptoms","change":"Symptoms worsen after a reduction.","action":"Review whether symptoms reflect withdrawal, relapse or another illness and adapt the reduction plan.","phrase":"We can review the pace rather than push through without assessment."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG215 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng215/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not give a universal seven-day taper or stop treatment as punishment for early use."],"followUp":"Agree a named prescriber, next review and a specific route for withdrawal symptoms or supply problems; monitor at each reduction.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["long-term","benzodiazepine","use","and"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange emergency assessment; a routine taper discussion is insufficient. You need urgent medical assessment now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-opioid","title":"Codeine use, persistent pain and dependence","summary":"Assess pain diagnosis, new red flags and functional goals","presentation":"An adult requests more codeine for longstanding non-cancer pain. The benefit is decreasing and they feel unwell when doses are missed.","assessment":["Assess pain diagnosis, new red flags and functional goals","Establish actual opioid use and other sedating drugs","Assess constipation, sedation, overdose and withdrawal","Explore mood, sleep, previous treatment and daily function"],"management":[{"step":"1. Reassess","detail":"Do not assume persistent pain has one cause or that increasing opioid dose is the answer."},{"step":"2. Review balance","detail":"Discuss actual benefit, harms and dependence without blame; verify medicines and prescribing history."},{"step":"3. Agree change","detail":"When reduction is appropriate, use an individualised supported plan; avoid abrupt cessation except exceptional medical circumstances requiring urgent management."},{"step":"4. Support function","detail":"Offer condition-appropriate alternatives, rehabilitation and psychological support, with specialist input where needed."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Your pain is real. I want us to review whether this medicine is helping enough and how to improve function without leaving you to manage withdrawal alone.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Overdose signs","change":"They are very drowsy with slow breathing.","action":"Activate emergency care immediately.","phrase":"This needs emergency help now."},{"label":"New neurological symptoms","change":"Back pain now includes bladder changes.","action":"Assess for a spinal emergency rather than simply increasing analgesia.","phrase":"The new symptoms need urgent assessment."},{"label":"Cancer pain","change":"Pain is due to advanced cancer.","action":"Use the appropriate cancer/palliative pathway; do not apply this chronic non-cancer withdrawal framework automatically.","phrase":"Your situation needs a pain plan specific to the cancer."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG215 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng215/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not apply this dependence guideline as a blanket opioid withdrawal protocol for acute pain, cancer pain or end-of-life care."],"followUp":"Review function, adverse effects and the reduction plan regularly, with a clear prescribing owner and escalation route.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["codeine","use,","persistent","pain"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Activate emergency care immediately. This needs emergency help now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-gambling","title":"Gambling-related harms and hidden suicidal risk","summary":"Ask about gambling patterns and impacts without judgement","presentation":"An adult presents with insomnia and irritability after escalating online gambling losses. They are reluctant to discuss debt.","assessment":["Ask about gambling patterns and impacts without judgement","Assess suicide directly, including acute risk after losses or disclosure","Explore debt, relationships, safeguarding and substances","Review medicines associated with impulse-control problems"],"management":[{"step":"1. Prioritise safety","detail":"Gambling harm can drive suicide risk even without another diagnosis; act on current danger."},{"step":"2. Offer treatment","detail":"Discuss specialist gambling services and gambling-specific CBT; group CBT is a recommended option, with alternatives when unsuitable."},{"step":"3. Reduce harm","detail":"Discuss practical barriers to gambling, financial safeguards and support for affected others with appropriate consent."},{"step":"4. Use specialist medicines appropriately","detail":"Medication is not the automatic first step; specialist consideration of naltrexone may be appropriate in selected circumstances with monitoring and contraindication checks."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"You do not have to solve this alone. I want to understand the harm and check your safety, then help you access treatment specifically for gambling.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Major loss tonight","change":"They intend to harm themselves after a loss.","action":"Arrange immediate emergency or crisis help based on danger.","phrase":"Your safety needs help now."},{"label":"Partner affected","change":"Family finances and children are at risk.","action":"Assess safeguarding and practical support, involve others with consent where appropriate.","phrase":"We should also think about who else is affected."},{"label":"Impulse-control medicine","change":"The problem began after a medication change.","action":"Coordinate urgent review with the treating team; do not stop essential treatment without a safe plan.","phrase":"That timing is important and needs a medication review."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG248 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng248/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not substitute an SSRI or sleeping tablet for gambling-specific assessment and treatment."],"followUp":"Arrange active follow-up for safety, treatment engagement and financial/social harm; check risk again after relapse, losses or disclosure.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["gambling-related","harms","and","hidden"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange immediate emergency or crisis help based on danger. Your safety needs help now.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-eating","title":"Adolescent eating disorder and physical risk","summary":"Assess weight trajectory, restriction, purging, exercise and intake","presentation":"A teenager has restricted intake, lost weight quickly and feels faint. Their BMI is not in an underweight range and the family asks to wait.","assessment":["Assess weight trajectory, restriction, purging, exercise and intake","Check observations, hydration, syncope and physical symptoms promptly","Assess electrolytes, glucose and ECG need according to risk","Ask about self-harm, comorbidity, family context and safeguarding"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess in person","detail":"Rapid weight loss and faintness warrant timely physical assessment; do not use normal appearance or BMI to dismiss risk."},{"step":"2. Escalate instability","detail":"Severe dehydration, electrolyte disturbance, malnutrition or organ compromise requires acute medical care, potentially emergency admission."},{"step":"3. Refer","detail":"Arrange prompt age-appropriate specialist eating-disorder assessment; do not wait for a particular BMI threshold."},{"step":"4. Coordinate","detail":"Agree monitoring, family involvement, nutrition support and safe follow-up with specialists; refeeding risk needs appropriate expertise."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"The speed of weight loss and faintness matter even if the BMI is not low. We need to check the physical effects and arrange specialist support.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Collapse","change":"They collapse or have severe physical symptoms.","action":"Arrange emergency care now.","phrase":"This needs immediate medical assessment."},{"label":"Purging","change":"Vomiting and laxative misuse emerge.","action":"Assess electrolyte and cardiac risk and increase urgency according to findings.","phrase":"These behaviours can affect salts and heart rhythm."},{"label":"Referral declined","change":"The teenager fears weight restoration.","action":"Explore fears, support decision-making and assess capacity/competence and safety; seek specialist advice promptly.","phrase":"I hear the fear, and we still need to address the risks together."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG69 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng69/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not decide admission using an absolute BMI threshold or treat a screening score as a complete assessment."],"followUp":"Name the clinician monitoring physical status and referral progress; frequency follows clinical risk and specialist advice, not a standard distant review.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["adolescent","eating","disorder","and"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"Arrange emergency care now. This needs immediate medical assessment.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}},{"id":"mh-alcohol","title":"Alcohol dependence, withdrawal and change","summary":"Establish quantity, strength, pattern, last drink and current withdrawal","presentation":"An adult wants to stop heavy daily alcohol use immediately. They describe morning tremor and poor nutrition.","assessment":["Establish quantity, strength, pattern, last drink and current withdrawal","Ask about past seizures, delirium, detoxification and other substances","Assess nutrition, physical illness, injury and cognition","Assess self-harm, driving, dependants and available support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess urgency","detail":"Severe withdrawal, seizures, confusion or suspected Wernicke encephalopathy require emergency hospital assessment."},{"step":"2. Plan supported withdrawal","detail":"Explain that sudden stopping or a large reduction may be dangerous in dependence; arrange skilled assessment for the appropriate setting."},{"step":"3. Treat nutritional risk","detail":"Assess need for oral or parenteral thiamine according to risk and presentation, using current guidance; do not delay emergency care."},{"step":"4. Continue recovery care","detail":"Coordinate alcohol treatment, psychological support, relapse prevention and physical-health monitoring with named follow-up."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does this mean for me?","response":"Wanting to change is an important step. The shaking means stopping suddenly could be unsafe, so let us arrange a supported assessment and plan.","why":"Connect the explanation to this person’s concern, then check their understanding and preferences."}],"variations":[{"label":"Previous withdrawal seizure","change":"They have a history of severe withdrawal.","action":"This increases risk and may require inpatient medically assisted withdrawal; seek skilled urgent assessment.","phrase":"Your previous seizure affects where withdrawal should be managed."},{"label":"Confusion and poor balance","change":"New neurological features develop.","action":"Treat as possible serious alcohol-related complication and arrange emergency care.","phrase":"These symptoms need hospital assessment now."},{"label":"Not ready to stop","change":"They decline referral.","action":"Explore concerns, offer harm-reduction engagement and continued review; still assess immediate risks and withdrawal.","phrase":"We can keep working together without forcing a decision today."}],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG100 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg100/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment and escalation for this presentation"}],"pitfalls":["Do not advise abrupt cessation in a dependent patient or provide a generic unsupervised detox regimen."],"followUp":"Confirm service handover and early follow-up matched to physical and withdrawal risk; arrange ongoing relapse-prevention and nutritional review.","reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["alcohol","dependence,","withdrawal","and"],"priorities":["Identify current danger and the appropriate assessment setting.","Connect the patient’s goal with a feasible clinical plan."],"safetyNet":["Suspected overdose, seizure, collapse, severe breathing difficulty or immediate danger: arrange emergency help now.","New intent to act on suicidal thoughts, rapidly worsening mental state or inability to stay safe: seek urgent same-day crisis assessment; use emergency care for immediate danger.","If the agreed referral or review cannot happen, contact the named clinical team rather than wait without a plan."],"retrieval":{"question":"How would your management change in the first variation, and how would you explain the reason?","answer":"This increases risk and may require inpatient medically assisted withdrawal; seek skilled urgent assessment. Your previous seizure affects where withdrawal should be managed.","checks":["Named the changed risk or need","Adapted management and urgency","Explained the plan without blame and checked feasibility"]}}]},{"id":"paediatrics","title":"Paediatrics","description":"Children, young people and families: urgent assessment, development, common symptoms and practical treatment plans.","icon":"02","reviewed":"2026-11-11","cases":[{"id":"pd-fever","title":"Fever in a young child: assess before reassuring","summary":"A parent phones about a 2-year-old with fever and reduced drinking.","presentation":"A parent phones about a 2-year-old with fever and reduced drinking. They want antibiotics because a relative previously had sepsis. You have not examined the child.","priorities":["Establish age, duration, current alertness, breathing, colour and rash","Assess hydration, urine output, intake and ability to keep fluids down"],"assessment":["Establish age, duration, current alertness, breathing, colour and rash","Assess hydration, urine output, intake and ability to keep fluids down","Ask about focal symptoms, medical conditions, immunisation and parental concern","Determine whether emergency or face-to-face assessment is required; do not invent normal observations"],"management":[{"step":"1. Triage first","detail":"Use age-appropriate fever and sepsis assessment. Emergency features need immediate help; arrange timely examination when remote assessment cannot establish severity."},{"step":"2. Make a reasoned decision","detail":"Antibiotics depend on the likely cause and clinical findings. Explain the working assessment and uncertainty rather than prescribe solely to reduce anxiety."},{"step":"3. Support comfort","detail":"Discuss fluids and appropriate comfort measures. If antipyretics are used, follow age/weight and product guidance; they do not prove an illness is harmless when the temperature falls."},{"step":"4. Close the loop","detail":"Give selected warning signs, a route to urgent help and a review matched to illness severity and persistence. Confirm the parent can carry out the plan."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Last time we waited and it got worse.","response":"I can understand why that experience worries you. First I need to check how your child is now, so we choose the right assessment and treatment.","why":"Recognise the reason behind the antibiotic request."},{"cue":"Can you promise it is only a virus?","response":"I cannot promise that by phone. I will explain what we can assess now and whether your child needs an examination.","why":"Avoid false certainty."},{"cue":"What should I watch for?","response":"Let us go through the specific warning symptoms and exactly where to get help, rather than just say to come back if worse.","why":"Introduce, then provide the actual safety net."}],"variations":[{"label":"Young infant","change":"The child is 6 weeks old with a temperature of 38°C.","action":"This is a high-risk age/temperature combination requiring urgent paediatric assessment; use emergency care if seriously unwell.","phrase":"At this age, that temperature needs urgent assessment."},{"label":"Non-blanching rash and illness","change":"A new rash does not fade under pressure and the child appears unwell.","action":"Treat as a possible serious infection and arrange emergency assessment; do not delay to complete routine questions.","phrase":"We need emergency help now."},{"label":"Persistent fever","change":"The fever reaches 5 days.","action":"Reassess and consider causes such as Kawasaki disease and other significant illness, using the clinical picture.","phrase":"The duration means we need to assess again."}],"safetyNet":["Severe breathing difficulty, markedly reduced responsiveness, blue/mottled appearance or rapid deterioration: arrange emergency help immediately.","Poor drinking, markedly fewer wet nappies or worsening parental concern: urgent reassessment according to severity.","Explain what to do if the planned assessment or review cannot be accessed."],"followUp":"Specify the reassessment interval from current risk and the expected course; do not simply wait for a fixed week if deteriorating.","pitfalls":["Do not use response to antipyretics to distinguish serious from non-serious illness.","Do not reassure on a remote impression without acknowledging missing observations."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG143 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng143/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["fever","in","a","young","child:"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"This is a high-risk age/temperature combination requiring urgent paediatric assessment; use emergency care if seriously unwell. At this age, that temperature needs urgent assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-gastroenteritis","title":"Diarrhoea and vomiting: dehydration and home care","summary":"A toddler has diarrhoea and vomiting and is drinking less.","presentation":"A toddler has diarrhoea and vomiting and is drinking less. The parent cannot tell whether wet nappies are reduced because every nappy contains diarrhoea.","priorities":["Assess responsiveness, breathing, perfusion and dehydration","Clarify age, duration, vomiting colour, blood, pain, intake and urine"],"assessment":["Assess responsiveness, breathing, perfusion and dehydration","Clarify age, duration, vomiting colour, blood, pain, intake and urine","Consider travel, contacts, underlying illness and diagnoses other than gastroenteritis","Assess whether the family can give fluids and access help"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess hydration","detail":"Arrange examination when hydration or severity cannot be established remotely. Shock or severe illness needs emergency transfer."},{"step":"2. Use appropriate fluids","detail":"For children suitable for oral treatment, explain oral rehydration solution in small frequent amounts according to the assessed rehydration plan. Continue breastfeeding; follow guidance on feeding during and after rehydration."},{"step":"3. Avoid unnecessary treatment","detail":"Antibiotics are not routine, and antidiarrhoeal medicines are not an appropriate default in young children. Investigations depend on clinical and epidemiological indications."},{"step":"4. Explain practical care","detail":"Discuss hygiene, avoiding fruit juice/fizzy drinks until diarrhoea stops, and the usual 48-hour exclusion after the last vomiting or diarrhoea episode, subject to specific infection/public-health advice."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"How do I know if they are dehydrated?","response":"I want to assess the drinking, urine, behaviour and physical signs together. Because the nappies are difficult to interpret, an examination may be needed.","why":"Explain why one sign is insufficient."},{"cue":"Should I stop breastfeeding?","response":"Keep breastfeeding. We can discuss additional oral rehydration if needed and explain the feeding plan for your child.","why":"Give a practical answer."},{"cue":"Can you give something to stop it?","response":"The main treatment is replacing fluid safely. We also need to check that nothing suggests another cause.","why":"Reconnect the request with the actual need."}],"variations":[{"label":"Bilious vomiting","change":"The vomit is green.","action":"Arrange urgent emergency assessment for possible obstruction or another serious cause; do not label routine gastroenteritis.","phrase":"Green vomiting needs urgent assessment."},{"label":"Oral treatment fails","change":"The child cannot keep oral rehydration down and is worsening.","action":"Arrange urgent reassessment; enteral or intravenous rehydration may be needed in a supervised setting.","phrase":"The home plan is not working safely now."},{"label":"Blood in stool","change":"Bloody diarrhoea develops.","action":"Reassess and use indicated stool testing and specialist advice; avoid assuming all infection needs the same antibiotic.","phrase":"This change needs a fresh assessment."}],"safetyNet":["Collapse, poor responsiveness or signs of shock: emergency help now.","Persistent vomiting preventing fluids, substantially reduced urine or increasing drowsiness: urgent assessment.","Severe localised pain, green vomiting or blood in stool: prompt escalation appropriate to severity."],"followUp":"Agree review of intake, hydration and symptoms, with earlier assessment for deterioration; confirm the family can obtain and give the recommended fluids.","pitfalls":["Do not assume diarrhoea excludes a surgical cause.","Do not offer a generic rehydration volume without weight and clinical assessment."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG84 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg84/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["diarrhoea","and","vomiting:","dehydration","and"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent emergency assessment for possible obstruction or another serious cause; do not label routine gastroenteritis. Green vomiting needs urgent assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-constipation","title":"Constipation, overflow soiling and family strain","summary":"A school-aged child soils underwear and avoids the toilet because passing stool hurts.","presentation":"A school-aged child soils underwear and avoids the toilet because passing stool hurts. The parent thinks the child is doing it deliberately and has limited money for food.","priorities":["Check onset, stool pattern, pain, withholding, soiling and growth","Look for neonatal onset, delayed meconium, neurological signs, distension with vomiting or other red flags"],"assessment":["Check onset, stool pattern, pain, withholding, soiling and growth","Look for neonatal onset, delayed meconium, neurological signs, distension with vomiting or other red flags","Assess faecal impaction and examination needs","Explore toileting access, school, finances, treatment use and family distress"],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain the mechanism","detail":"Overflow soiling may occur around retained stool; it is not wilful misbehaviour. Avoid blame and explain the pain–withholding cycle."},{"step":"2. Treat appropriately","detail":"If idiopathic constipation is established, assess impaction and use age-appropriate disimpaction when indicated, commonly with a macrogol regimen checked against current prescribing guidance."},{"step":"3. Maintain improvement","detail":"Maintenance laxatives often continue beyond the first improvement, with gradual adjustment. Combine with regular supported toileting, rewards for behaviours rather than dry pants, and suitable diet/fluids."},{"step":"4. Make it feasible","detail":"Provide a written regimen and accessible toilet/school plan, address cost and supply, and review response rather than rely on dietary advice alone."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"They must be doing it on purpose.","response":"The leakage may happen because stool has built up and softer stool leaks around it. Blaming them will not solve that; we need to make passing stool comfortable again.","why":"Remove shame."},{"cue":"Can we just change the diet?","response":"Healthy food and fluids help, but established constipation often needs medicine as well. I will explain the actual regimen and review it with you.","why":"Set realistic expectations."},{"cue":"Will this be fixed tomorrow?","response":"It can take time for the bowel to recover. We will check the response and adjust the plan rather than stop as soon as one stool passes.","why":"Explain continuity."}],"variations":[{"label":"Red flags","change":"Constipation began in the neonatal period with delayed meconium.","action":"Arrange specialist assessment for an underlying cause rather than label idiopathic constipation.","phrase":"The early onset changes what we need to investigate."},{"label":"Disimpaction started","change":"Soiling initially increases during treatment.","action":"Explain that this can occur, check the prescribed regimen and symptoms, and review for completion or complications.","phrase":"Some leakage can increase while retained stool clears."},{"label":"Treatment not accessible","change":"The family cannot obtain or administer the medicine.","action":"Resolve supply, formulation, language and school barriers before calling the treatment ineffective.","phrase":"Let us make the plan possible to follow."}],"safetyNet":["Vomiting with marked distension, severe pain or a very unwell child: urgent assessment.","New neurological symptoms, faltering growth or unexplained bleeding: reassess and investigate appropriately.","Contact the treating team if the prescribed regimen cannot be given or symptoms worsen."],"followUp":"During disimpaction, review within 1 week; maintenance requires planned continuing review and a supported reduction plan when appropriate.","pitfalls":["Do not blame the child or treat established constipation with diet alone.","Do not abruptly stop maintenance treatment after the first improvement."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG99 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg99/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["constipation,","overflow","soiling","and","family"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange specialist assessment for an underlying cause rather than label idiopathic constipation. The early onset changes what we need to investigate.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-newborn-weight","title":"Newborn weight loss and feeding concerns","summary":"A newborn has lost more than 10% of birth weight.","presentation":"A newborn has lost more than 10% of birth weight. The parent is exhausted and worried that asking for help means breastfeeding has failed.","priorities":["Assess current illness, hydration, urine, stool, alertness and jaundice","Confirm weight measurements and feeding history, including observed feeding"],"assessment":["Assess current illness, hydration, urine, stool, alertness and jaundice","Confirm weight measurements and feeding history, including observed feeding","Ask about birth history, milk transfer and maternal health","Identify urgent paediatric needs and skilled feeding support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess promptly","detail":"Weight loss over 10% warrants clinical assessment for dehydration or illness, a detailed feeding history and observation by someone with appropriate expertise."},{"step":"2. Support feeding","detail":"Arrange skilled breastfeeding or formula-feeding support matched to the situation. Discuss supplements when clinically indicated, supporting breastmilk supply and the parent’s informed preference."},{"step":"3. Escalate when indicated","detail":"Marked loss, illness, dehydration or failure to respond to feeding support requires paediatric input; a routine reweigh alone may be unsafe."},{"step":"4. Agree monitoring","detail":"Use a coordinated feeding and weight plan, avoiding unnecessary repeated weighing and conflicting advice. Check the parent understands what to do tonight."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Have I failed at feeding?","response":"You have done the right thing by asking for help. We need to check the baby and observe a feed so we can make a plan that works for both of you.","why":"Reduce blame while retaining assessment."},{"cue":"Can we wait for another weight?","response":"I want to assess the baby’s hydration and feeding now. The next weight is one part of monitoring, not a replacement for that assessment.","why":"Explain urgency."},{"cue":"Will I have to stop breastfeeding?","response":"Not automatically. If extra feeds are needed, we can discuss how to give them while supporting your feeding goals and milk supply.","why":"Avoid a forced either/or choice."}],"variations":[{"label":"Lethargy","change":"The baby is difficult to wake and feeding poorly.","action":"Arrange emergency or urgent paediatric care according to severity; do not wait for feeding support alone.","phrase":"The baby needs urgent medical assessment."},{"label":"Ongoing loss","change":"Weight continues to fall despite support.","action":"Seek paediatric assessment and review the actual feeding plan and possible underlying illness.","phrase":"The response tells us we need more assessment."},{"label":"Parent overwhelmed","change":"The parent cannot manage the proposed schedule.","action":"Simplify and coordinate practical support while protecting adequate intake and parental wellbeing.","phrase":"Let us agree a safe plan you can actually carry out."}],"safetyNet":["Poor responsiveness, breathing difficulty or severe illness: emergency help.","Very poor intake, reduced urine or worsening jaundice: urgent assessment.","Explain who to contact if feeding support or weight checks do not happen."],"followUp":"Set a named feeding/weight review according to clinical concern. NICE advises weighing no more frequently than daily for concern about early weight loss; urgency is determined by the baby’s condition.","pitfalls":["Do not automatically defer all assessment for 48 hours.","Do not infer adequate intake from the duration of feeds alone."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG75 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng75/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["newborn","weight","loss","and","feeding"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange emergency or urgent paediatric care according to severity; do not wait for feeding support alone. The baby needs urgent medical assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-reflux","title":"Infant reflux: reassurance, feeding and red flags","summary":"A thriving young infant brings up milk after feeds.","presentation":"A thriving young infant brings up milk after feeds. The parent asks for acid-suppressing medicine because the washing and crying are difficult.","priorities":["Distinguish effortless regurgitation from forceful or bilious vomiting","Assess feeding, growth, distress, stool, blood and systemic illness"],"assessment":["Distinguish effortless regurgitation from forceful or bilious vomiting","Assess feeding, growth, distress, stool, blood and systemic illness","Ask about choking, respiratory events and feeding technique","Explore the family’s concern and what has already been tried"],"management":[{"step":"1. Recognise physiological reflux","detail":"Explain that uncomplicated regurgitation is common and often improves with time. A thriving infant without concerning features may not need medicine."},{"step":"2. Optimise feeding","detail":"Offer skilled feeding assessment; in formula-fed infants review volumes and feeding pattern. Use a guideline-based stepped approach if regurgitation causes marked distress."},{"step":"3. Use medicines selectively","detail":"Do not offer acid suppression solely for isolated visible regurgitation. Alginate or other treatment requires the relevant indication and review; check compatibility with thickened feeds."},{"step":"4. Protect safe sleep","detail":"Keep the infant sleeping on their back on a flat, firm sleep surface. Do not advise prone sleep or raising the cot as reflux treatment."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Is acid burning the baby?","response":"Bringing up milk does not by itself tell us there is damaging acid disease. Let us check growth, feeding and any warning signs before deciding on treatment.","why":"Explain without dismissal."},{"cue":"Could I raise the cot?","response":"For sleep, keep the baby flat on their back. We can work on feeding safely without changing that sleep advice.","why":"Give a clear safety instruction."},{"cue":"So you are doing nothing?","response":"We can assess the feeds, check growth and agree what changes would need reassessment. Medicine is only useful when it fits the problem.","why":"Describe active care."}],"variations":[{"label":"Projectile vomiting","change":"A young infant develops persistent forceful vomiting.","action":"Arrange prompt assessment for causes such as pyloric stenosis, with urgency based on hydration and illness.","phrase":"This is different from ordinary posseting."},{"label":"Green vomiting","change":"Bilious vomiting occurs.","action":"Emergency assessment is needed for possible obstruction.","phrase":"Green vomit needs urgent medical assessment."},{"label":"Poor growth","change":"Weight gain falters or feeding becomes difficult.","action":"Reassess for pathological reflux and other causes and seek indicated specialist input.","phrase":"The growth change means we need to look further."}],"safetyNet":["Green or bloody vomiting, marked distension, severe illness or poor responsiveness: urgent/emergency assessment.","Persistent projectile vomiting, dehydration or faltering growth: prompt review.","Reassess if symptoms begin late or persist beyond the usual infant course."],"followUp":"Agree review of feeding, growth and distress; any medication trial needs an explicit duration and review of benefit.","pitfalls":["Do not prescribe a PPI for simple regurgitation alone.","Do not compromise safe-sleep advice to reduce reflux."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG1 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng1/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["infant","reflux:","reassurance,","feeding","and"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange prompt assessment for causes such as pyloric stenosis, with urgency based on hydration and illness. This is different from ordinary posseting.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-colic","title":"Crying infant and an exhausted parent","summary":"A parent describes prolonged evening crying in a young infant.","presentation":"A parent describes prolonged evening crying in a young infant. They say they are close to losing control and ask for stronger colic drops.","priorities":["Assess the baby for illness, feeding difficulty, injury and abnormal crying","Check growth, vomiting, stools and pattern between episodes"],"assessment":["Assess the baby for illness, feeding difficulty, injury and abnormal crying","Check growth, vomiting, stools and pattern between episodes","Ask directly how the parent is coping and whether the baby is safe","Explore support, sleep and parental mental health"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess before labelling","detail":"Colic describes crying in an otherwise healthy infant; review concerning features and examine when needed."},{"step":"2. Offer practical comfort","detail":"Discuss feeding support, holding, soothing and a calm environment. Avoid promising benefit from drops, herbal remedies or routine dietary restriction."},{"step":"3. Protect both parent and baby","detail":"If overwhelmed, the parent can place the baby safely in the cot, step away briefly to calm down and obtain help. Never shake a baby."},{"step":"4. Arrange support","detail":"Identify a trusted person and health-visiting support; act on immediate safeguarding or parental mental-health risk."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"I cannot take another night.","response":"This sounds exhausting. First, are you and the baby safe right now? Let us arrange some practical support as well as check the baby.","why":"Take distress seriously."},{"cue":"Which drops will cure it?","response":"There is not a guaranteed medicine for colic. We should check that the baby is well and focus on safe soothing and support.","why":"Avoid an unsupported cure."},{"cue":"I feel angry when the crying starts.","response":"Thank you for telling me. Put the baby safely in the cot if you need a short break, and let us work out who can help you now.","why":"Respond without shame and with action."}],"variations":[{"label":"Unwell baby","change":"Fever, poor feeding or a weak unusual cry appears.","action":"Reassess urgently rather than assume colic.","phrase":"This change needs a medical check."},{"label":"Immediate safety concern","change":"The parent fears they may hurt the baby.","action":"Arrange immediate safe support and safeguarding/emergency help appropriate to danger.","phrase":"We need someone to help keep you both safe now."},{"label":"Persistent symptoms","change":"Crying remains atypical or growth is affected.","action":"Reassess the diagnosis and feeding and arrange further assessment.","phrase":"We should review what is causing this rather than keep changing drops."}],"safetyNet":["An infant who is difficult to wake, struggling to breathe or seriously unwell needs emergency help.","Fever in a young infant, poor feeding or a changed cry needs urgent assessment.","Immediate fear of harm to the baby requires urgent safety support."],"followUp":"Agree health visitor/GP follow-up and a clear plan for the next difficult period; earlier review for illness or worsening parental coping.","pitfalls":["Do not assume all crying is benign.","Do not leave a parent describing loss of control with only a leaflet."],"links":[{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/colic/","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"},{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/baby/caring-for-a-newborn/soothing-a-crying-baby/","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["crying","infant","and","an","exhausted"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Reassess urgently rather than assume colic. This change needs a medical check.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-autism","title":"Developmental differences and possible autism","summary":"A parent is concerned about their toddler’s communication and play.","presentation":"A parent is concerned about their toddler’s communication and play. They fear that an assessment will label the child and limit schooling.","priorities":["Explore developmental profile, communication, play, sensory needs and repetitive patterns","Ask about loss of acquired skills, hearing, vision and other health concerns"],"assessment":["Explore developmental profile, communication, play, sensory needs and repetitive patterns","Ask about loss of acquired skills, hearing, vision and other health concerns","Gather information across home and nursery, including strengths and support needs","Explore family priorities, access barriers and the child’s own communication"],"management":[{"step":"1. Listen and explain","detail":"Describe a possible neurodevelopmental difference without diagnosing from a brief checklist or making predictions about an individual child’s future."},{"step":"2. Refer appropriately","detail":"Use the local autism diagnostic pathway when indicated. Loss of language or social skills under age 3 warrants referral to the autism team; other regression patterns may need paediatric/neurological assessment."},{"step":"3. Support now","detail":"Offer appropriate hearing/developmental assessment and speech, education or family support according to need; support should not wait solely for a diagnostic label."},{"step":"4. Coordinate","detail":"Agree consent for sharing relevant nursery/school information and name who will follow the referral and current needs."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Will this ruin their future?","response":"An assessment is about understanding their needs and strengths so we can offer useful support. It cannot predict their whole future.","why":"Avoid deficit-only or falsely certain language."},{"cue":"Must we wait for a diagnosis?","response":"We can look at communication, hearing and practical support while the assessment is arranged.","why":"Offer action now."},{"cue":"They make eye contact, so it cannot be autism?","response":"One behaviour cannot confirm or rule it out. We need to understand the whole developmental pattern.","why":"Correct a common misconception."}],"variations":[{"label":"Regression","change":"A 2-year-old loses words and social skills.","action":"Refer to the autism team and assess wider developmental/medical needs promptly.","phrase":"Losing skills changes the assessment we need."},{"label":"Motor regression","change":"Previously acquired motor skills are lost.","action":"Arrange paediatric/neurological assessment rather than assume autism explains it.","phrase":"This needs a broader medical assessment."},{"label":"Family reluctant","change":"The parent declines referral.","action":"Explore the fear, explain benefits and limits and arrange review of support and development.","phrase":"We can discuss your concerns and keep the support needs under review."}],"safetyNet":["Loss of skills, seizures or new neurological symptoms needs prompt medical review; acute severe symptoms require emergency care.","Family exhaustion or unsafe behaviour warrants additional support and risk assessment.","Provide a contact route if the referral is not progressing."],"followUp":"Agree a named follow-up for referral progress and the child’s functioning, with earlier reassessment for regression or new concerns.","pitfalls":["Do not rule out autism from eye contact, affection or apparently good skills in one area.","Do not promise that all children will develop in the same way."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG128 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg128/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["developmental","differences","and","possible","autism"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Refer to the autism team and assess wider developmental/medical needs promptly. Losing skills changes the assessment we need.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-adhd","title":"Attention and activity differences: possible ADHD","summary":"A parent requests ADHD medication for a child who struggles with concentration.","presentation":"A parent requests ADHD medication for a child who struggles with concentration. School reports are incomplete, and home life is under strain.","priorities":["Assess attention, impulsivity, activity, duration and impairment across settings","Explore sleep, learning, anxiety, trauma, hearing and other explanations"],"assessment":["Assess attention, impulsivity, activity, duration and impairment across settings","Explore sleep, learning, anxiety, trauma, hearing and other explanations","Hear the child’s perspective and gather school information appropriately","Assess severity, safety, family needs and existing support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess context","detail":"Describe the observed difficulties without calling the child naughty or attention-seeking. Academic success does not by itself exclude difficulties."},{"step":"2. Match referral to severity","detail":"Refer directly for specialist assessment when impairment is severe. In other presentations, provide appropriate support and review/referral; questionnaires assist but do not independently diagnose."},{"step":"3. Support function","detail":"Discuss environmental adjustments, predictable routines and appropriate parenting/education support without blaming parents."},{"step":"4. Use specialist prescribing","detail":"Initial ADHD medication decisions require relevant specialist expertise. Baseline physical assessment is needed; an ECG is not universal and depends on cardiac features/risks."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Are they just being naughty?","response":"There may be several reasons why attention or behaviour is difficult. I would like to understand what is happening in different settings before judging the cause.","why":"Avoid labels."},{"cue":"Can you start tablets today?","response":"Medication needs a specialist assessment and safety checks. We can start addressing the difficulties and arrange the right pathway now.","why":"Explain action and boundaries."},{"cue":"School says the grades are fine.","response":"Good grades are useful information, but we also need to know about effort, distress, attention and daily functioning.","why":"Look beyond one measure."}],"variations":[{"label":"Severe impairment","change":"The child is at substantial risk of exclusion and daily functioning is severely affected.","action":"Refer directly for specialist assessment rather than impose a routine observation delay.","phrase":"The level of difficulty warrants specialist assessment now."},{"label":"Cardiac history","change":"Syncope or relevant family cardiac history emerges before proposed medication.","action":"Follow specialist baseline assessment and indicated cardiology advice; do not rely on a routine ECG alone.","phrase":"This history changes the safety checks needed."},{"label":"Symptoms in one setting","change":"Only home difficulties are currently reported.","action":"Explore contextual and alternative explanations and gather fuller information; do not declare a diagnosis or dismiss distress.","phrase":"We need more information to understand the pattern."}],"safetyNet":["Unsafe impulsivity, self-harm or severe mental-health deterioration needs urgent assessment.","New neurological or physical symptoms require appropriate medical review.","Give a route for escalating support if school or family function deteriorates."],"followUp":"Agree review of functioning and support while awaiting assessment. Specialist medication follow-up should include the recommended growth, pulse/BP and adverse-effect monitoring for the medicine and age.","pitfalls":["Do not delay severe impairment until questionnaires are complete.","Do not state that every child needs an ECG before ADHD medication."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG87 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng87/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["attention","and","activity","differences:","possible"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Refer directly for specialist assessment rather than impose a routine observation delay. The level of difficulty warrants specialist assessment now.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-preschool-wheeze","title":"Preschool wheeze and suspected asthma","summary":"A 3-year-old has recurrent wheeze and interval night symptoms.","presentation":"A 3-year-old has recurrent wheeze and interval night symptoms. The parent wants a diagnosis and is worried about inhaled steroids.","priorities":["Assess current breathing and attack severity before chronic management","Clarify recurrent episodes, interval symptoms, atopy and previous severe attacks"],"assessment":["Assess current breathing and attack severity before chronic management","Clarify recurrent episodes, interval symptoms, atopy and previous severe attacks","Review existing inhalers, spacer technique, adherence and smoke exposure","Consider alternative causes and whether symptoms fit the under-5 pathway"],"management":[{"step":"1. Address acute illness","detail":"Use acute assessment and escalation if an attack is present; the chronic pathway is not an emergency treatment protocol."},{"step":"2. Explain diagnostic uncertainty","detail":"Asthma may be difficult to confirm objectively at this age. Explain the role of a planned treatment trial and review."},{"step":"3. Use the age-specific pathway","detail":"For appropriate suspected asthma under 5, consider an 8–12-week trial of twice-daily paediatric low-dose ICS with SABA reliever, following current prescribing guidance and clinical criteria."},{"step":"4. Teach and review","detail":"Demonstrate device/spacer technique, check the parent can use it and provide a written action plan. Review response and diagnosis rather than continue indefinitely without assessment."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does a steroid inhaler mean severe asthma?","response":"It treats inflammation in the airways. The choice and dose depend on your child’s symptoms, and we will review whether it helps.","why":"Explain purpose without minimising harms."},{"cue":"Why not just the blue inhaler?","response":"Relief of tightness is only part of the plan. We also need to consider treatment for the inflammation and how often symptoms occur.","why":"Avoid reliever-only thinking."},{"cue":"How will we know it works?","response":"We will agree which symptoms to track and when to review the trial, including technique and any side effects.","why":"Make the trial assessable."}],"variations":[{"label":"Acute distress","change":"The child is struggling to breathe or reliever treatment is not helping.","action":"Arrange urgent or emergency care according to severity; do not wait for the trial to work.","phrase":"We need to deal with the breathing difficulty now."},{"label":"No response","change":"Symptoms persist despite the trial.","action":"Check technique, adherence, environment and alternative diagnoses, then seek specialist assessment as indicated.","phrase":"We should review why it has not helped before adding more treatment."},{"label":"Symptoms resolve","change":"The trial resolves symptoms.","action":"Consider guideline-based stopping/reassessment and ongoing review rather than assume permanent treatment is automatically needed.","phrase":"We can review whether the treatment is still needed."}],"safetyNet":["Severe breathing difficulty, exhaustion, blue colour or reduced responsiveness: emergency help.","Worsening wheeze or poor response to the agreed action plan: urgent reassessment.","Give device-specific reliever instructions and contacts in the written plan, not a generic instruction to double inhalers."],"followUp":"Review the trial at 8–12 weeks and sooner for deterioration or adverse effects; confirm the ongoing or stopping plan.","pitfalls":["Do not copy the adult AIR/MART pathway into every preschool case.","Do not let chronic management delay acute attack treatment."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG245 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng245/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["preschool","wheeze","and","suspected","asthma"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent or emergency care according to severity; do not wait for the trial to work. We need to deal with the breathing difficulty now.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-uti","title":"Childhood UTI and recurrent infection","summary":"A child has painful urination and a previous urinary infection.","presentation":"A child has painful urination and a previous urinary infection. The parent asks for the same antibiotic and an automatic preventive prescription.","priorities":["Establish age, systemic illness, fever, loin pain, vomiting and hydration","Obtain appropriate urine information and prior culture results"],"assessment":["Establish age, systemic illness, fever, loin pain, vomiting and hydration","Obtain appropriate urine information and prior culture results","Ask about recurrence, constipation, voiding and structural history","Assess sample collection and the need for urgent specialist care"],"management":[{"step":"1. Triage by age and illness","detail":"A suspected UTI in an infant under 3 months needs immediate paediatric specialist referral; seriously unwell children need urgent/emergency care."},{"step":"2. Collect a useful sample","detail":"Use age-appropriate collection and testing, ideally before antibiotics, without delaying needed treatment for a seriously ill child."},{"step":"3. Treat according to presentation","detail":"Choose treatment using the lower/upper UTI pathway, age, allergy and culture history. Do not simply repeat a previous antibiotic without review."},{"step":"4. Plan recurrence care","detail":"Use the age- and episode-specific imaging tables. Recurrent or atypical infection may need specialist referral and imaging; prophylaxis is a selected specialist decision, not routine after a first infection."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Why do we need another urine sample?","response":"The sample helps us check whether this is an infection and which treatment is likely to work. We also need to assess how unwell your child is.","why":"Connect testing with decisions."},{"cue":"Will every child need a scan?","response":"Not every child. The need and timing depend on age, the type of infection, the response and whether infections recur.","why":"Avoid a universal scan rule."},{"cue":"Can daily antibiotics prevent this?","response":"Sometimes a specialist may consider prevention, but first we need to review the infections and possible contributing problems.","why":"Keep prevention individualised."}],"variations":[{"label":"Under 3 months","change":"The patient is a 7-week-old baby.","action":"Refer immediately to paediatrics for assessment and treatment; do not use a routine community oral-antibiotic plan.","phrase":"At this age, suspected infection needs specialist assessment."},{"label":"Systemically unwell","change":"Fever, vomiting and poor hydration appear.","action":"Assess upper UTI/sepsis and escalate according to illness and ability to take treatment.","phrase":"These features mean we need a more urgent assessment."},{"label":"Recurrent infection","change":"Further confirmed episodes occur.","action":"Review cultures, bladder/bowel factors and the imaging/referral pathway for recurrence.","phrase":"We should look at the pattern, not just treat another episode."}],"safetyNet":["Severe illness, poor responsiveness or circulatory/respiratory compromise: emergency help.","Persistent vomiting, worsening fever, poor urine output or failure to improve on treatment: prompt reassessment.","Explain who reviews the culture and will contact the family if treatment needs changing."],"followUp":"Track culture results and clinical response; reassess promptly for failure to improve, with imaging/referral follow-up based on the actual age and infection pattern.","pitfalls":["Do not treat every child with upper UTI as the same admission scenario regardless of assessment.","Do not automatically prescribe long-term prophylaxis or image every first infection."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG224 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng224/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["childhood","uti","and","recurrent","infection"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Refer immediately to paediatrics for assessment and treatment; do not use a routine community oral-antibiotic plan. At this age, suspected infection needs specialist assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-bedwetting","title":"Bedwetting, embarrassment and a school trip","summary":"An 8-year-old wets at night and is anxious about a residential school visit.","presentation":"An 8-year-old wets at night and is anxious about a residential school visit. The parent has been limiting drinks all day.","priorities":["Clarify primary/secondary wetting, daytime symptoms, constipation and fluid/toilet pattern","Ask about thirst, polyuria, weight loss, illness and new stressors"],"assessment":["Clarify primary/secondary wetting, daytime symptoms, constipation and fluid/toilet pattern","Ask about thirst, polyuria, weight loss, illness and new stressors","Explore the child’s goals, privacy and family response","Assess whether an alarm or medication plan would be feasible"],"management":[{"step":"1. Normalise without dismissing","detail":"Explain that bedwetting is not deliberate and avoid punishment. Reward agreed behaviours rather than dry nights alone."},{"step":"2. Address contributors","detail":"Treat constipation and assess relevant urinary or medical causes. Support adequate daytime fluid intake and regular toileting."},{"step":"3. Choose treatment","detail":"An alarm is often the initial treatment after appropriate advice when acceptable and feasible. Desmopressin may suit a need for rapid/short-term improvement or when alarm use is unsuitable, following age/suitability guidance."},{"step":"4. Explain safety","detail":"If desmopressin is prescribed, give clear fluid-restriction instructions and when to withhold it; check understanding and use current product guidance."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Should we stop drinks all day?","response":"No. Adequate daytime drinking matters. If medicine is used, there are specific night-time fluid rules which I will explain separately.","why":"Separate general hydration from medicine safety."},{"cue":"They are being lazy.","response":"This is not something to punish. We can help them with a practical plan and protect their privacy.","why":"Remove shame."},{"cue":"Can the trip still happen?","response":"Let us discuss options for the trip, including a discreet plan and whether short-term treatment is suitable.","why":"Use the child’s meaningful goal."}],"variations":[{"label":"Diabetes symptoms","change":"New thirst, weight loss and frequent urination develop.","action":"Arrange urgent assessment for diabetes; do not treat as uncomplicated enuresis.","phrase":"These symptoms need checking promptly."},{"label":"Constipation","change":"Painful infrequent stools and soiling coexist.","action":"Treat bowel dysfunction alongside the wetting plan.","phrase":"The bowel problem can contribute to wetting."},{"label":"Cannot follow fluid rules","change":"The child cannot safely comply with night-time fluid restriction.","action":"Do not use desmopressin without a safe plan; consider alternatives and specialist advice.","phrase":"The medicine is only suitable if its safety instructions can be followed."}],"safetyNet":["New illness, significant thirst/weight loss or urinary pain needs medical assessment.","For desmopressin, explain warning symptoms such as significant headache, vomiting or confusion and the urgent action required.","Give a contact route for treatment difficulties or family distress."],"followUp":"Review alarm engagement and progress early according to the recommended pathway; desmopressin needs review of response and safety rather than unsupervised indefinite use.","pitfalls":["Do not punish wetting or impose dehydration throughout the day.","Do not prescribe desmopressin without discussing safe fluid intake."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG111 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg111/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["bedwetting,","embarrassment","and","a","school"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent assessment for diabetes; do not treat as uncomplicated enuresis. These symptoms need checking promptly.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-eczema","title":"Childhood eczema and fear of topical steroids","summary":"A child scratches through the night.","presentation":"A child scratches through the night. The parent stopped the prescribed cream after reading that steroids always damage skin.","priorities":["Assess severity, extent, sites, infection signs and effect on sleep/growth","Clarify what products and potencies have actually been used"],"assessment":["Assess severity, extent, sites, infection signs and effect on sleep/growth","Clarify what products and potencies have actually been used","Check quantity, technique, adherence barriers and skin irritation","Assess painful rapidly worsening eczema or eye involvement"],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain the condition","detail":"Describe inflammation and skin-barrier problems. Regular emollients support the barrier; flares may need anti-inflammatory treatment."},{"step":"2. Treat proportionately","detail":"Match topical corticosteroid potency to severity, body site and age, following the actual prescription and current guidance. Explain where, how much and how long."},{"step":"3. Teach practically","detail":"Demonstrate application, suitable washing and a written flare plan. Explain emollient residue fire risk and appropriate precautions."},{"step":"4. Escalate complications","detail":"Suspected eczema herpeticum needs immediate treatment and same-day specialist advice; eye involvement needs ophthalmological as well as dermatological advice."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Will the cream thin their skin?","response":"Using the right strength in the right place for the prescribed time has a different risk from using a strong cream everywhere indefinitely. Let me show you the plan for your child.","why":"Address the specific fear."},{"cue":"Should we stop all treatment if the skin weeps?","response":"We need to assess whether there is infection and choose treatment accordingly. Do not assume every flare needs antibiotics or stop all eczema care without advice.","why":"Avoid an automatic rule."},{"cue":"How much should I put on?","response":"I will show you the amount for each area and write down which product goes where.","why":"Make instructions usable."}],"variations":[{"label":"Painful punched-out lesions","change":"The eczema becomes rapidly painful with uniform blisters/erosions and illness.","action":"Consider eczema herpeticum, start indicated systemic treatment and arrange same-day specialist advice.","phrase":"This may need urgent treatment beyond the usual eczema creams."},{"label":"Eye area affected","change":"Suspected herpetic lesions involve skin near the eyes.","action":"Arrange same-day ophthalmological and dermatological advice.","phrase":"The eye area makes urgent specialist assessment important."},{"label":"Treatment ineffective","change":"Symptoms persist despite the plan.","action":"Review diagnosis, actual technique, potency, infection and adherence, and refer if indicated.","phrase":"Let us check why the plan is not controlling the symptoms."}],"safetyNet":["Rapidly worsening painful eczema, blisters or fever: urgent same-day assessment.","Eye symptoms or involvement in suspected eczema herpeticum: same-day specialist care.","Seek advice for significant treatment adverse effects, poor growth or uncontrolled sleep disturbance."],"followUp":"Set an explicit flare review and ongoing skin-care plan; review sooner for worsening, infection concerns or poor response.","pitfalls":["Do not call all topical steroids dangerous or harmless regardless of use.","Do not treat every weeping flare with antibiotics without assessment."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG57 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg57/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["childhood","eczema","and","fear","of"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Consider eczema herpeticum, start indicated systemic treatment and arrange same-day specialist advice. This may need urgent treatment beyond the usual eczema creams.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-not-brought","title":"Child not brought: access barriers and safeguarding","summary":"A child has missed essential follow-up twice.","presentation":"A child has missed essential follow-up twice. A parent says they could not understand the messages and had no transport.","priorities":["Establish the child’s current health and consequences of delay","Review the pattern of missed care and other safeguarding information"],"assessment":["Establish the child’s current health and consequences of delay","Review the pattern of missed care and other safeguarding information","Clarify language, transport, disability, finances and communication needs","Listen without assuming either neglect or no risk"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess current need","detail":"Determine whether delay creates urgent clinical harm and arrange appropriate assessment."},{"step":"2. Remove barriers","detail":"Use professional interpretation, accessible communication and feasible appointment arrangements; confirm the parent understands the reason for care."},{"step":"3. Assess safeguarding","detail":"Repeated failure to bring a child to essential follow-up can raise neglect concerns. Consider the whole pattern, seek safeguarding advice and act proportionately."},{"step":"4. Close the loop","detail":"Name who will track the appointment and the agreed action if it is missed or contact fails; document facts and reasoning."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"We did not understand the letter.","response":"Thank you for telling me. Let us explain the appointment in your preferred language and check what is needed for your child now.","why":"Repair communication."},{"cue":"Are you saying I am a bad parent?","response":"I want to understand what prevented the care and make sure your child gets what they need. I also have a duty to consider their safety.","why":"Combine empathy and responsibility."},{"cue":"What happens if transport fails again?","response":"Let us agree a backup contact and who will help arrange the next step, rather than leave the appointment untracked.","why":"Make a reliable plan."}],"variations":[{"label":"Acute clinical risk","change":"The missed treatment has left the child unwell.","action":"Arrange urgent assessment now and safeguarding action if indicated.","phrase":"The child needs care today."},{"label":"Persistent missed essential care","change":"Missed appointments continue despite support and safety concerns rise.","action":"Use safeguarding advice and local multi-agency pathways; do not endlessly rebook without review.","phrase":"We need additional help to protect your child’s health."},{"label":"Communication solved","change":"An interpreter reveals a genuine misunderstanding.","action":"Correct access and understanding, but still assess the actual clinical consequences and wider information.","phrase":"We can fix the communication and check the health needs."}],"safetyNet":["An acutely unwell child needs urgent/emergency care according to symptoms.","No response to attempts at contact must trigger the agreed clinical and safeguarding escalation.","Explain how the family can contact the team if arrangements fail."],"followUp":"Document a named appointment/results owner and contact-back deadline based on the child’s clinical risk; verify essential care happens.","pitfalls":["Do not label non-attendance as informed refusal by the child.","Do not declare no safeguarding concern solely because one practical barrier is identified."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG89 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg89/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"NICE NG76 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng76/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["child","not","brought:","access","barriers"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent assessment now and safeguarding action if indicated. The child needs care today.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-neonatal-eye","title":"A red sticky eye in a newborn","summary":"A 12-day-old has a red sticky eye.","presentation":"A 12-day-old has a red sticky eye. The parent requests ordinary conjunctivitis drops from the pharmacy.","priorities":["Establish age, redness, discharge, swelling and ability to open the eye","Ask about fever, feeding, responsiveness and other illness"],"assessment":["Establish age, redness, discharge, swelling and ability to open the eye","Ask about fever, feeding, responsiveness and other illness","Review onset and relevant birth/maternal history sensitively","Assess urgency and access to paediatric/ophthalmic care"],"management":[{"step":"1. Recognise neonatal risk","detail":"A red sticky eye in a newborn requires urgent assessment rather than routine treatment as older-child conjunctivitis."},{"step":"2. Assess complications","detail":"Examine for significant infection or eye involvement and arrange appropriate sampling and specialist advice."},{"step":"3. Treat the cause","detail":"Some neonatal infections need systemic treatment and assessment of mother/partners, guided by specialists; topical drops alone may be insufficient."},{"step":"4. Communicate sensitively","detail":"Explain urgency without blame or assumptions about infection source. Agree prompt attendance and follow-up."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can I just buy eye drops?","response":"Because your baby is so young, we need an urgent assessment before treating this like ordinary conjunctivitis.","why":"Give the reason for urgency."},{"cue":"Is this my fault?","response":"Our priority is to assess and treat the baby. We will explain any tests and any care needed for you without making assumptions.","why":"Reduce blame."},{"cue":"Can it wait until next week?","response":"No, a newborn’s red sticky eye should be checked urgently.","why":"Use clear timing."}],"variations":[{"label":"Systemic illness","change":"The baby has fever, poor feeding or lethargy.","action":"Arrange urgent/emergency paediatric assessment according to severity.","phrase":"The baby needs medical assessment now."},{"label":"Marked swelling","change":"The eye cannot open or swelling is significant.","action":"Escalate urgent eye/paediatric assessment for serious infection.","phrase":"This needs urgent specialist review."},{"label":"Discharge without redness","change":"A well infant has mild stickiness without a red eye.","action":"Assess whether tear-duct obstruction is likely, but retain a low threshold for review and clear red-eye/illness advice.","phrase":"We need to distinguish simple stickiness from infection."}],"safetyNet":["Newborn redness with discharge: urgent assessment.","Fever, poor feeding, reduced responsiveness or significant swelling: urgent/emergency care.","Explain who follows samples and specialist treatment."],"followUp":"Confirm urgent assessment and appropriate specialist/result follow-up; do not provide only a distant routine review.","pitfalls":["Do not use the older-child conjunctivitis pathway without considering age.","Do not assume topical drops alone treat all neonatal eye infections."],"links":[{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/red-eye/","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"},{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.moorfields.nhs.uk/for-health-professionals/childrens-eye-conditions-management/infective-conjunctivitis","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["a","red","sticky","eye","in"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent/emergency paediatric assessment according to severity. The baby needs medical assessment now.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"pd-head-lice","title":"Head lice, recurrent treatment and school anxiety","summary":"A parent finds nits and wants the whole household treated with an old insecticide shampoo.","presentation":"A parent finds nits and wants the whole household treated with an old insecticide shampoo. They worry the child must miss school.","priorities":["Confirm live lice rather than eggs alone","Review prior treatment, timing, technique and close contacts"],"assessment":["Confirm live lice rather than eggs alone","Review prior treatment, timing, technique and close contacts","Check age, pregnancy/asthma considerations for product suitability","Assess scalp injury or secondary infection"],"management":[{"step":"1. Confirm infestation","detail":"Treatment is based on live lice. Lice are not proof of poor hygiene."},{"step":"2. Choose an effective approach","detail":"Explain wet combing on days 1, 5, 9 and 13, then check on day 17. If unsuitable or unsuccessful, discuss a suitable recommended lotion/spray with the pharmacist and follow its instructions."},{"step":"3. Treat affected contacts","detail":"Check close contacts and treat those with live lice on the same day; do not automatically treat everyone or use prevention insecticides."},{"step":"4. Avoid unnecessary exclusion","detail":"Children do not need to stay off school solely for head lice. Explain practical checking and adherence without excessive household cleaning demands."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does this mean their hair is dirty?","response":"No. Head lice spread through close head contact and are not a sign of poor hygiene.","why":"Remove stigma."},{"cue":"Should everyone use the shampoo?","response":"Check everyone, then treat the people who have live lice on the same day.","why":"Make the advice specific."},{"cue":"Can they go to school?","response":"They do not need to miss school just because of head lice. Let us make sure the treatment plan is followed properly.","why":"Prevent unnecessary disruption."}],"variations":[{"label":"Eggs only","change":"No live lice are found.","action":"Recheck carefully rather than automatically use treatment.","phrase":"We treat live infestation, not every remaining egg case."},{"label":"Treatment failure","change":"Live lice persist after an apparently complete course.","action":"Check technique, correct repeat timing, reinfestation and product suitability; seek pharmacist/clinical advice.","phrase":"Let us work out why it has not cleared before repeating the same product."},{"label":"Scalp infection","change":"Painful crusting or spreading redness develops.","action":"Assess and treat the skin complication as indicated.","phrase":"The scalp symptoms need a separate assessment."}],"safetyNet":["Spreading redness, swelling, significant pain or systemic illness needs medical review.","Persistent live lice despite correctly completed treatment needs reassessment of technique and choice.","Avoid applying more product than directed or using incompatible products together."],"followUp":"Use the complete combing schedule and day-17 check, or the actual product’s repeat/check schedule.","pitfalls":["Do not recommend permethrin as a routine current UK head-lice treatment.","Do not diagnose active infestation from nits alone or require school exclusion."],"links":[{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/head-lice-and-nits/","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["head","lice,","recurrent","treatment","and"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Recheck carefully rather than automatically use treatment. We treat live infestation, not every remaining egg case.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}}]},{"id":"older-palliative","title":"Older-person & palliative care","description":"Cognition, falls, symptom relief, support and planning care around the person’s wishes.","icon":"03","reviewed":"2026-11-11","cases":[{"id":"op-delirium","title":"Sudden confusion: delirium until assessed","summary":"A care worker reports that an older person has become muddled over a day.","presentation":"A care worker reports that an older person has become muddled over a day. The person is quieter than usual, takes several medicines and lives alone. The caller asks for antibiotics for a urine infection.","priorities":["Assess responsiveness, breathing, circulation, focal neurological signs and immediate safety","Establish acute onset, fluctuation and baseline cognition/function"],"assessment":["Assess responsiveness, breathing, circulation, focal neurological signs and immediate safety","Establish acute onset, fluctuation and baseline cognition/function","Check physical illness, pain, hydration, constipation, retention, medicines and glucose when relevant","Obtain collateral information and determine whether safe urgent community assessment is achievable"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess urgently","detail":"New confusion needs prompt clinical assessment for potentially serious causes; emergency features require emergency care. Quiet withdrawal can be hypoactive delirium, not reassuring calmness."},{"step":"2. Search for causes","detail":"Assess observations, examination and targeted tests. A 4AT can support delirium assessment in relevant settings; no score replaces assessment of the cause."},{"step":"3. Avoid diagnostic shortcuts","detail":"In older adults, do not use urine dipsticks to diagnose UTI or treat asymptomatic bacteriuria. Consider urinary symptoms and the overall clinical picture; do not assume confusion equals UTI."},{"step":"4. Support and coordinate","detail":"Provide reassurance, orientation, sensory aids and familiar support. Arrange safe supervision and a definite review; community care is appropriate only if the assessment and support can meet the need."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"It must be a urine infection.","response":"An infection is one possibility, but dehydration, medicines, constipation and other illnesses can also cause sudden confusion. We need to assess the person rather than choose treatment from a urine dipstick.","why":"Broaden the differential."},{"cue":"They are just sleepy, not agitated.","response":"A sudden quieter or sleepier state can still be important. It needs the same attention to possible causes.","why":"Recognise hypoactive delirium."},{"cue":"Can they stay at home?","response":"That depends on the illness and whether safe assessment, treatment and support can be provided. We need a plan for today, not simply leave them alone.","why":"Connect setting to actual support."}],"variations":[{"label":"Stroke signs","change":"New weakness or speech change appears.","action":"Activate the acute stroke/emergency pathway immediately.","phrase":"These new symptoms need emergency help."},{"label":"Medicine error","change":"An accidental extra dose or sedating medicine is identified.","action":"Assess toxicity and seek urgent medicines/toxicology advice or emergency care according to effects.","phrase":"We need to check the dose and effects urgently."},{"label":"Home unsafe","change":"The person cannot safely be left without supervision.","action":"Arrange an appropriate urgent care setting or reliable support; do not promise an unavailable home package.","phrase":"We need a safe arrangement while this is assessed."}],"safetyNet":["Reduced consciousness, severe illness, stroke signs, respiratory compromise or suspected serious overdose: emergency help.","Worsening confusion or inability to maintain intake/safety: urgent reassessment.","Give carers an actual same-day contact and backup route."],"followUp":"Confirm same-day assessment and handover, with close reassessment according to cause and response; do not defer review of unresolved delirium for a routine week.","pitfalls":["Do not diagnose UTI in an older adult from a positive dipstick alone.","Do not equate quietness with recovery or presume a new dementia diagnosis during acute confusion."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG103 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg103/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/urinary-tract-infection-diagnosis/diagnosis-of-urinary-tract-infections-quick-reference-tools-for-primary-care","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["sudden","confusion:","delirium","until","assessed"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Activate the acute stroke/emergency pathway immediately. These new symptoms need emergency help.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-memory","title":"Progressive memory difficulties and possible dementia","summary":"An adult and their partner describe gradually worsening memory over months.","presentation":"An adult and their partner describe gradually worsening memory over months. The person fears losing independence and asks for an immediate diagnosis.","priorities":["Distinguish gradual decline from acute delirium","Assess cognition, daily function, mood, sensory impairment and medicines"],"assessment":["Distinguish gradual decline from acute delirium","Assess cognition, daily function, mood, sensory impairment and medicines","Hear the patient directly and obtain relevant collateral information with appropriate consent","Assess driving, work, home safety and support needs"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess broadly","detail":"Evaluate the cognitive and functional pattern and potentially reversible contributors using examination and appropriate tests. Use an appropriate cognitive instrument with attention to language, education and sensory needs."},{"step":"2. Refer when indicated","detail":"Arrange specialist dementia assessment after appropriate initial evaluation. A normal brief score does not alone exclude dementia; do not diagnose subtype from one score."},{"step":"3. Support independence","detail":"Agree practical memory aids and support, medicine safety and meaningful goals. Discuss family involvement according to the patient’s wishes."},{"step":"4. Discuss responsibilities","detail":"Address driving and other safety concerns, including notification duties after diagnosis using the relevant licensing authority. Future planning is supported and decision-specific."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does this mean I have dementia?","response":"There are several possible explanations for memory difficulties. We need to assess the pattern, check contributing causes and arrange specialist assessment if needed.","why":"Explain uncertainty."},{"cue":"Will everyone else make decisions for me?","response":"A diagnosis does not automatically take away your ability to decide. We will support you with each decision and involve people you choose where appropriate.","why":"Protect autonomy."},{"cue":"Can tablets cure it?","response":"Treatments may help some symptoms in particular types of dementia, but they are not a cure. The specialist assessment helps identify suitable options.","why":"Avoid an exaggerated promise."}],"variations":[{"label":"Sudden worsening","change":"Confusion develops over hours or days.","action":"Assess delirium and urgent causes rather than label progression.","phrase":"The sudden change needs assessment now."},{"label":"Unsafe driving","change":"A credible report describes dangerous driving.","action":"Assess promptly, advise against unsafe driving and follow professional duties and licensing guidance if concerns persist.","phrase":"We must address the driving risk directly."},{"label":"Brief test normal","change":"A screening score is normal despite concerning functional decline.","action":"Do not dismiss the history; consider further assessment and referral.","phrase":"The score is one piece of the assessment."}],"safetyNet":["Acute confusion, new neurological symptoms or major loss of function needs urgent assessment.","Immediate hazards at home or inability to meet basic needs require a timely safety plan.","Provide a contact for referral delays and carer difficulties."],"followUp":"Name who tracks tests/referral and reviews support needs; cognitive and functional review should be tailored to risk and specialist advice.","pitfalls":["Do not assume incapacity from a diagnosis or score.","Do not promise a drug will stop disease progression or use an acute delirium score to diagnose dementia."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG97 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng97/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["progressive","memory","difficulties","and","possible"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Assess delirium and urgent causes rather than label progression. The sudden change needs assessment now.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-dementia-distress","title":"Distress and aggression in dementia","summary":"A family asks for medication because a person with dementia shouts during personal care.","presentation":"A family asks for medication because a person with dementia shouts during personal care. The pattern has recently changed, and carers are exhausted.","priorities":["Assess immediate danger and whether symptoms are new or fluctuating","Look for pain, delirium, constipation, retention, infection, sensory problems and medicine effects"],"assessment":["Assess immediate danger and whether symptoms are new or fluctuating","Look for pain, delirium, constipation, retention, infection, sensory problems and medicine effects","Ask what happens before and during distress and how care is provided","Assess the person’s preferences, communication and carer safety"],"management":[{"step":"1. Identify the need","detail":"Use a structured assessment of clinical and environmental causes before treating distress. Do not assume aggression is simply inevitable dementia."},{"step":"2. Adapt care","detail":"Use calm communication, familiar routines, reduced overstimulation, appropriate pain relief and person-centred activities. Avoid coercive or humiliating approaches."},{"step":"3. Use medicines selectively","detail":"Antipsychotics require an appropriate indication such as risk of harm or severe distress, a benefit–harm discussion, lowest effective dose and shortest duration. Follow relevant prescribing and specialist advice."},{"step":"4. Review and support","detail":"Address carer needs and a safe care plan. If an antipsychotic is used, reassess at least every 6 weeks and stop if there is no clear continuing benefit, with appropriate clinical planning."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can you give something to calm them?","response":"I understand how difficult this is. First we need to check whether pain, illness or the way care is happening is causing distress.","why":"Explain why assessment precedes sedation."},{"cue":"They will not cooperate.","response":"Their behaviour may be telling us something is frightening or uncomfortable. Let us look at what triggers it and how we can change the care.","why":"Use non-blaming interpretation."},{"cue":"Are the medicines harmless?","response":"They can sometimes help, but they also carry important risks. We need a specific reason to use them and a clear review plan.","why":"Be honest about harms."}],"variations":[{"label":"Lewy body dementia","change":"There is Lewy body or Parkinson’s disease dementia.","action":"Antipsychotic sensitivity and worsening motor symptoms are important risks; obtain appropriate specialist advice.","phrase":"This type of dementia changes the medicine risks."},{"label":"New pain","change":"Distress occurs during movement or care.","action":"Assess and treat pain, adapting handling and communication.","phrase":"Relieving pain may be more useful than sedation."},{"label":"Immediate violence","change":"Someone faces immediate serious harm.","action":"Arrange emergency help and a safe response without expecting carers to physically manage danger alone.","phrase":"We need help to keep everyone safe now."}],"safetyNet":["Immediate serious danger needs emergency help.","Acute confusion, fever, new pain or marked deterioration needs urgent assessment.","Give a route for carers who cannot safely provide care or if medicine adverse effects appear."],"followUp":"Arrange an early review of the care changes; if antipsychotics are prescribed, review effectiveness and need at least every 6 weeks, sooner for adverse effects.","pitfalls":["Do not prescribe sedation for convenience.","Do not overlook severe antipsychotic sensitivity in Lewy body/Parkinson’s disease dementia."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG97 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng97/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"NICE CG103 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg103/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["distress","and","aggression","in","dementia"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Antipsychotic sensitivity and worsening motor symptoms are important risks; obtain appropriate specialist advice. This type of dementia changes the medicine risks.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-falls","title":"Falls, dizziness and a recent medicine change","summary":"An older adult has fallen twice after a treatment change.","presentation":"An older adult has fallen twice after a treatment change. They are frightened of losing independence and want to stop every medicine.","priorities":["Assess injury, head trauma, loss of consciousness and acute illness","Ask about dizziness, posture, heart symptoms and neurological symptoms"],"assessment":["Assess injury, head trauma, loss of consciousness and acute illness","Ask about dizziness, posture, heart symptoms and neurological symptoms","Review actual medicines, vision, gait, balance, footwear and home hazards","Explore function, confidence, osteoporosis/fracture risk and support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Address immediate harm","detail":"Arrange urgent injury or acute-cause assessment when indicated; do not label every fall mechanical."},{"step":"2. Assess comprehensively","detail":"Use a falls assessment matched to history and risk, including relevant examination, lying/standing BP, gait/balance and medication review."},{"step":"3. Make targeted changes","detail":"Reconcile recent changes and drug-related risks. Adjust according to findings rather than stop all medicines or prescribe a general tonic."},{"step":"4. Restore confidence","detail":"Discuss appropriate strength/balance interventions, home hazard work, vision care and support. Agree a meaningful mobility goal and ownership across services."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does this mean I must stop living alone?","response":"Not automatically. We need to understand the falls and what would make your home and movement safer.","why":"Avoid a premature loss-of-independence conclusion."},{"cue":"I will stop all the tablets.","response":"Some may contribute, but stopping everything could also cause harm. Let us check what you take and make a targeted plan.","why":"Support a safe review."},{"cue":"I am afraid to walk now.","response":"That fear matters. We can plan safe support and rehabilitation while we address the causes.","why":"Connect reassurance to action."}],"variations":[{"label":"Head injury","change":"The fall included a head strike and anticoagulant use.","action":"Use the current head-injury pathway for urgent assessment rather than routine falls follow-up.","phrase":"The head injury and medicine change the urgency."},{"label":"Blackout","change":"There was transient loss of consciousness.","action":"Assess a blackout/cardiac cause through the appropriate pathway.","phrase":"We need to understand why consciousness was lost."},{"label":"Multiple falls and frailty","change":"There are recurrent falls with impaired gait.","action":"Arrange comprehensive assessment and individualised intervention rather than a single leaflet.","phrase":"Several factors may be interacting, so the plan needs to address them together."}],"safetyNet":["Head injury with concerning symptoms, new neurological signs, collapse or severe injury: urgent/emergency assessment.","Recurrent falls, inability to mobilise safely or worsening dizziness require timely review.","Specify the backup plan if home support or rehabilitation cannot be arranged."],"followUp":"Arrange follow-up based on injury/acute risk and the interventions, including who reviews medicine changes and whether mobility is improving.","pitfalls":["Do not use a prediction score as a substitute for assessing individual risks.","Do not treat falls with blanket medicine stopping."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG249 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng249/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["falls,","dizziness","and","a","recent"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Use the current head-injury pathway for urgent assessment rather than routine falls follow-up. The head injury and medicine change the urgency.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-opioid-constipation","title":"Opioid-related constipation in a frail adult","summary":"A care-home resident taking an opioid for painful arthritis has constipation and increasing distress.","presentation":"A care-home resident taking an opioid for painful arthritis has constipation and increasing distress. A laxative is already prescribed, but actual use is unclear.","priorities":["Check obstruction features, pain, vomiting, distension and passage of flatus","Assess stool pattern, overflow, impaction, intake and mobility"],"assessment":["Check obstruction features, pain, vomiting, distension and passage of flatus","Assess stool pattern, overflow, impaction, intake and mobility","Review opioid dose, actual laxative administration and other constipating medicines","Assess new confusion, urinary retention and safe care needs"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess before escalating laxatives","detail":"Obstruction, severe illness or marked impaction needs appropriate examination and urgent management rather than remote dose escalation."},{"step":"2. Review the regimen","detail":"Verify what is actually administered and why the opioid is used. Maintain adequate pain care while considering the medicine’s benefits and harms."},{"step":"3. Treat appropriately","detail":"Choose a suitable bowel regimen using current prescribing guidance; opioid constipation often needs regular laxative treatment and may require an osmotic/stimulant approach. Avoid inappropriate bulk loading or rectal treatment without assessment."},{"step":"4. Make care practical","detail":"Agree a bowel chart, fluids and mobility as appropriate to clinical status, who can administer treatment and when response will be reviewed."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can we stop the painkiller?","response":"We should review whether it is still the best treatment, but I also want to keep the pain controlled. Let us assess the constipation and balance both needs.","why":"Avoid replacing one untreated problem with another."},{"cue":"Should we give extra fibre?","response":"That depends on the situation. We first need to check for retained stool or obstruction and choose a suitable treatment.","why":"Avoid generic advice."},{"cue":"They are more agitated.","response":"Pain, constipation, retention or delirium may contribute. The change needs assessment rather than just a stronger sedative.","why":"Recognise interacting problems."}],"variations":[{"label":"Obstruction signs","change":"Vomiting, marked distension and no flatus develop.","action":"Arrange urgent assessment rather than add oral laxatives without review.","phrase":"These symptoms need an urgent medical assessment."},{"label":"Overflow","change":"Loose leakage occurs around retained stool.","action":"Assess impaction; do not assume laxative-induced diarrhoea or stop all treatment automatically.","phrase":"Leakage can occur despite constipation."},{"label":"Renal impairment","change":"Significant kidney impairment limits treatment choices.","action":"Check laxative and opioid suitability and seek specialist/pharmacy advice as needed.","phrase":"The kidney function changes what is safest."}],"safetyNet":["Severe pain, vomiting, distension, inability to pass wind or significant bleeding: urgent assessment.","New confusion, poor intake or rapid deterioration: reassess promptly.","Give a route for non-response or inability to administer the actual regimen."],"followUp":"Set a definite short-interval bowel and symptom review, commonly within 24–48 hours when symptomatic, sooner for warning signs; reassess unresolved symptoms and suspected impaction.","pitfalls":["Do not assume leakage excludes impaction.","Do not give a generic laxative escalation or enema without checking obstruction, frailty and renal considerations."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG140 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg140/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["opioid-related","constipation","in","a","frail"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange urgent assessment rather than add oral laxatives without review. These symptoms need an urgent medical assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-palliative-pain","title":"Palliative pain and an opioid patch problem","summary":"A person with advanced cancer has worsening pain and says the opioid patch keeps coming loose.","presentation":"A person with advanced cancer has worsening pain and says the opioid patch keeps coming loose. A relative asks for a direct conversion to tablets over the phone.","priorities":["Assess pain severity, pattern, breakthrough use and urgent causes","Establish exact drug, strength, timing, patch number, adhesion and removal"],"assessment":["Assess pain severity, pattern, breakthrough use and urgent causes","Establish exact drug, strength, timing, patch number, adhesion and removal","Assess sedation, breathing, cognition, renal/hepatic function and other medicines","Ask about swallowing, goals, available support and specialist plan"],"management":[{"step":"1. Check immediate risks","detail":"Assess possible toxicity as well as undertreatment. New neurological deficit or suspected cancer emergency needs urgent escalation consistent with the person’s care plan."},{"step":"2. Review delivery","detail":"Check the product and application instructions, skin and adhesion, heat exposure and accidental transfer. Do not cut patches or improvise extra patches."},{"step":"3. Change safely","detail":"Opioid conversions are not a single universal ratio. Seek specialist/pharmacist advice for switching, residual absorption, incomplete cross-tolerance, organ impairment and breakthrough treatment."},{"step":"4. Coordinate","detail":"Provide the actual written prescription/administration plan, bowel and nausea care, monitoring, safe storage/disposal and a named contact."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can we just replace it with morphine?","response":"We may be able to change treatment, but the dose and timing need checking carefully because the patch can keep releasing medicine after removal.","why":"Explain why conversion needs care."},{"cue":"Will this mean stopping pain relief?","response":"No. We need a safe pain-relief plan, including what to do while any change is arranged.","why":"Avoid abandonment."},{"cue":"Can a hot-water bottle help over the patch?","response":"Do not put heat over or near the patch. Heat can increase absorption and cause harm.","why":"Use a clear safety boundary."}],"variations":[{"label":"Toxicity","change":"The person is difficult to wake with slow breathing.","action":"Arrange emergency care and follow opioid-toxicity guidance; patch removal does not end the need for assessment/monitoring.","phrase":"This needs emergency help now."},{"label":"Renal impairment","change":"Kidney function has significantly deteriorated.","action":"Seek specialist advice on opioid choice/dose and assess accumulation.","phrase":"The kidney change affects how the medicine is handled."},{"label":"Spinal symptoms","change":"New weakness or bladder/bowel changes appear with spinal pain.","action":"Arrange urgent assessment for possible cord/cauda equina compression rather than simply increase opioid.","phrase":"The new nerve symptoms change the urgency."}],"safetyNet":["Very slow breathing, unresponsiveness or suspected overdose: emergency help.","New neurological symptoms or severe rapidly worsening pain: urgent assessment.","Clarify who to call for uncontrolled pain, confusion or accidental patch exposure."],"followUp":"Agree prompt review after a change, with observation frequency matched to risk and opioid kinetics; confirm specialist/community team handover and access to breakthrough care.","pitfalls":["Do not use a universal opioid conversion or fixed start time without patient-specific checking.","Do not assume a removed patch has immediately stopped working."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG140 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg140/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/fentanyl/how-and-when-to-use-fentanyl/","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["palliative","pain","and","an","opioid"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Arrange emergency care and follow opioid-toxicity guidance; patch removal does not end the need for assessment/monitoring. This needs emergency help now.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-palliative-nausea","title":"Nausea and vomiting after an opioid change","summary":"A patient receiving palliative care develops nausea after an opioid increase.","presentation":"A patient receiving palliative care develops nausea after an opioid increase. The family assumes the opioid must be stopped, but constipation and other causes have not been checked.","priorities":["Assess hydration, vomiting frequency, intake and medicine retention","Check constipation/obstruction, metabolic causes, infection and intracranial symptoms"],"assessment":["Assess hydration, vomiting frequency, intake and medicine retention","Check constipation/obstruction, metabolic causes, infection and intracranial symptoms","Review all medicines, timing, organ function and prior antiemetic response","Clarify route needs, symptom goals and home support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Assess cause","detail":"Do not assume the opioid is the only explanation. Obstruction, dehydration or other urgent pathology may change management."},{"step":"2. Treat symptoms","detail":"When opioid-related nausea is likely, explain that it can occur and may improve; prescribe and optimise a suitable antiemetic when indicated, checking interactions and contraindications."},{"step":"3. Preserve pain care","detail":"Review the balance of analgesia and adverse effects. Persistent symptoms despite appropriate antiemetic treatment may require a specialist opioid/route review."},{"step":"4. Coordinate review","detail":"Arrange a route the patient can use, a hydration/symptom plan consistent with goals and clear contact for ongoing vomiting."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Must we stop the pain medicine?","response":"Not automatically. We need to check the cause of the sickness and treat it while keeping the pain under control.","why":"Balance competing needs."},{"cue":"Can I take any anti-sickness tablet?","response":"The best choice depends on the cause, other medicines and whether you can keep tablets down.","why":"Explain individualisation."},{"cue":"What if the medicine comes back up?","response":"Let us agree what to do if you cannot retain it and whether another route is needed. Do not keep repeating doses without advice.","why":"Prevent accidental extra dosing."}],"variations":[{"label":"Obstruction suspected","change":"Vomiting occurs with colic, distension and no flatus.","action":"Seek urgent/specialist assessment and an obstruction-specific plan; avoid prokinetics in complete obstruction.","phrase":"This pattern needs a different assessment and treatment."},{"label":"Cannot swallow","change":"Oral medicines cannot be taken.","action":"Review formulation and route with the team; do not crush modified-release preparations or assume every drug can be given through a tube.","phrase":"We need a route that is safe and effective."},{"label":"New severe headache","change":"Vomiting accompanies new neurological symptoms.","action":"Assess an urgent intracranial or other acute cause, in line with the clinical situation and care goals.","phrase":"The new symptoms need urgent review."}],"safetyNet":["Blood in vomit, severe pain/distension, marked dehydration or new neurological signs: urgent assessment.","Persistent inability to retain fluids/medicines or worsening confusion: prompt clinical review.","Explain the day/night contact and what to do if the antiemetic does not help."],"followUp":"Arrange early review of response, hydration and medicine retention, sooner for deterioration; persistent symptoms need a revised cause/route assessment.","pitfalls":["Do not attribute every vomiting episode to opioids.","Do not give a cause-blind antiemetic or repeat vomited doses without a plan."],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG140 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg140/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"NICE NG31 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng31/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["nausea","and","vomiting","after","an"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Seek urgent/specialist assessment and an obstruction-specific plan; avoid prokinetics in complete obstruction. This pattern needs a different assessment and treatment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-breathlessness","title":"Breathlessness in advanced illness: physical causes and anxiety","summary":"A person with advanced cancer becomes breathless when dressing and is afraid to leave home.","presentation":"A person with advanced cancer becomes breathless when dressing and is afraid to leave home. A recent assessment was reassuring, but today’s symptoms must still be clarified.","priorities":["Assess current severity, onset, change and emergency features","Consider pulmonary, cardiac, thromboembolic, infective and medication causes"],"assessment":["Assess current severity, onset, change and emergency features","Consider pulmonary, cardiac, thromboembolic, infective and medication causes","Explore panic, activity limitation and the patient’s goals","Review baseline condition, prescribed symptom plan and available support"],"management":[{"step":"1. Reassess change","detail":"A previous normal assessment does not exclude new illness. Arrange examination/investigation or escalation according to current symptoms and care goals."},{"step":"2. Offer practical relief","detail":"Positioning, a handheld fan if suitable, calm support and paced activity may help. Avoid exhausting instructions to breathe deeply repeatedly."},{"step":"3. Individualise medicines","detail":"Persistent distress may need a palliative symptom plan with specialist support and attention to opioids, sedatives, organ function and toxicity. Do not use a blanket sedative for presumed anxiety."},{"step":"4. Coordinate","detail":"Clarify expected response, review and out-of-hours help. Oxygen is not a universal treatment for breathlessness; in the last days of life, use it when symptomatic hypoxaemia is known or suspected."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"They checked me last week, so it must be anxiety.","response":"Anxiety may contribute, but I need to assess what is happening today before assuming there is no physical cause.","why":"Avoid diagnostic overshadowing."},{"cue":"Can I have oxygen?","response":"We need to assess whether oxygen would help your situation. There are other ways to ease breathlessness too.","why":"Avoid promising a treatment without indication."},{"cue":"I just want to get outside.","response":"That goal matters. Let us first check safety, then build a symptom and activity plan that supports it.","why":"Connect care to function."}],"variations":[{"label":"Acute chest pain","change":"New sudden breathlessness and chest pain occur.","action":"Assess for a possible emergency such as PE or cardiac illness, with urgent action appropriate to findings/goals.","phrase":"This new pattern needs urgent assessment."},{"label":"Last days of life","change":"The person is recognised as dying and is distressed.","action":"Use an individualised end-of-life symptom plan, checking reversible causes and preferences; do not apply a chronic rehabilitation plan mechanically.","phrase":"We will focus on relief that fits your wishes."},{"label":"Medicine sedation","change":"Extra sedating doses have been taken.","action":"Assess respiratory depression/toxicity urgently and review the prescribed plan.","phrase":"We need to check whether medicine is contributing."}],"safetyNet":["Severe new breathlessness, chest pain, collapse, blue colour or reduced responsiveness: emergency/urgent care appropriate to the agreed plan and clinical assessment.","Worsening despite the symptom plan needs prompt contact with the treating team.","Give a usable day/night route rather than vague advice to wait for a nurse."],"followUp":"Agree prompt review of current symptoms and any medicine changes; reassess physical causes when the pattern changes.","pitfalls":["Do not conclude there is no physical cause from a remote account or old normal observations.","Do not prescribe oxygen or a benzodiazepine automatically for all breathlessness."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG31 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng31/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["breathlessness","in","advanced","illness:","physical"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Assess for a possible emergency such as PE or cardiac illness, with urgent action appropriate to findings/goals. This new pattern needs urgent assessment.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-mnd","title":"MND, swallowing difficulties and carer exhaustion","summary":"A person with MND uses a gastrostomy and communicates with an aid.","presentation":"A person with MND uses a gastrostomy and communicates with an aid. Their partner asks about anticipatory medicines and says care at home is becoming unsafe.","priorities":["Hear the patient using suitable communication support","Assess current respiratory, swallowing, nutrition and symptom needs"],"assessment":["Hear the patient using suitable communication support","Assess current respiratory, swallowing, nutrition and symptom needs","Review tube problems, medicines, care plan and what carers can actually provide","Clarify wishes, decision-specific capacity and any valid legal authority"],"management":[{"step":"1. Support communication","detail":"Allow time and communication aids. Speech difficulty does not imply impaired understanding or capacity."},{"step":"2. Coordinate specialist care","detail":"Use the MND multidisciplinary team, dietetics, speech and language therapy, respiratory and palliative services according to need."},{"step":"3. Make medicines/routes safe","detail":"Check each medicine’s formulation and suitability for tube or other route with pharmacy/nursing input. Do not crush modified-release medicines or give every liquid through a tube without checking."},{"step":"4. Plan care realistically","detail":"Discuss carer assessment, respite, equipment, hospice/community options and who will respond today. Revisit preferences as needs change; a past wish for home care does not require unsafe care."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does a syringe pump mean death is being caused?","response":"It is a way of giving prescribed medicines steadily when that route is needed. We should explain which symptoms each medicine treats and how we monitor its effects.","why":"Explain purpose rather than guarantee an outcome."},{"cue":"I cannot manage another night.","response":"Thank you for saying that. We need a safe plan for tonight as well as longer-term support.","why":"Act on the immediate burden."},{"cue":"Can I decide everything because I am next of kin?","response":"We need to involve them as fully as possible and check any actual legal authority for decisions they cannot make.","why":"Avoid assumed proxy authority."}],"variations":[{"label":"Tube problem","change":"The gastrostomy leaks, blocks or is displaced.","action":"Use the relevant urgent tube-care pathway and specialist advice; do not force feeds or medicines through an unsafe tube.","phrase":"We need to check the tube before using it."},{"label":"Respiratory deterioration","change":"New distress or signs of ventilatory failure develop.","action":"Arrange urgent respiratory/palliative assessment and escalation in line with the agreed plan.","phrase":"The breathing change needs help now."},{"label":"Placement requested","change":"The partner requests a hospice or care setting.","action":"Explore the patient’s current wishes and feasible support, and follow the appropriate decision process if capacity is lacking.","phrase":"Let us discuss the safest achievable setting with the person and team."}],"safetyNet":["Acute respiratory distress, choking, reduced responsiveness or tube displacement needs urgent help through the relevant pathway.","Uncontrolled symptoms or inability to provide safe care tonight requires an urgent practical response.","Give named day/night contacts and a backup if the usual team cannot respond."],"followUp":"Confirm who will assess current needs and when; coordinate ongoing MDT review and explicit follow-up of carer support and symptom/route changes.","pitfalls":["Do not infer incapacity from communication impairment.","Do not promise instant hospice admission or send carers away with an unconfirmed referral."],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG42 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng42/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"},{"label":"NICE NG31 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng31/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["mnd,","swallowing","difficulties","and","carer"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Use the relevant urgent tube-care pathway and specialist advice; do not force feeds or medicines through an unsafe tube. We need to check the tube before using it.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}},{"id":"op-advance-care","title":"Advance care, CPR and treatment preferences","summary":"A patient with advanced disease wants to plan future care.","presentation":"A patient with advanced disease wants to plan future care. A family member believes a DNACPR recommendation means all treatment will stop.","priorities":["Check current symptoms and whether a decision is needed now","Explore understanding, values, fears and desired involvement"],"assessment":["Check current symptoms and whether a decision is needed now","Explore understanding, values, fears and desired involvement","Support communication and assess capacity for each relevant decision when indicated","Check existing documents, legal authority and the UK nation’s framework"],"management":[{"step":"1. Start with the person","detail":"Ask permission to discuss future care and what matters most. Avoid imposing a decision based solely on age, disability or diagnosis."},{"step":"2. Separate decisions","detail":"Explain that CPR recommendations concern CPR, while other treatments and escalation require their own discussion. A DNACPR recommendation is distinct from a valid applicable advance refusal of treatment."},{"step":"3. Plan practically","detail":"Discuss preferred care, likely scenarios, benefits/burdens and who will coordinate. Explain uncertainty and when the plan should be reviewed."},{"step":"4. Record and share","detail":"Document agreed preferences/decisions and share with appropriate teams, including out-of-hours services, respecting confidentiality and the applicable legal framework."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does DNACPR mean no antibiotics?","response":"No. It concerns CPR if the heart or breathing stops. Other appropriate treatments need their own plan.","why":"Correct the central misunderstanding."},{"cue":"Can I change my mind?","response":"We can review preferences and the plan as your wishes or circumstances change. The exact effect depends on the type of decision or document.","why":"Avoid conflating documents."},{"cue":"Will you make my family decide everything?","response":"We should involve you as fully as possible. Family views can help, but we need to check any actual legal authority if you cannot make a particular decision.","why":"Explain roles."}],"variations":[{"label":"Currently capable refusal","change":"The patient makes an informed refusal of a treatment.","action":"Respect the decision within the applicable law, explain consequences and record clearly; do not equate disagreement with incapacity.","phrase":"I want to make sure you understand the options and that we record your wishes accurately."},{"label":"Capacity uncertain","change":"The person cannot engage because of acute confusion.","action":"Assess/treat the acute cause, support decision-making and follow the relevant legal process for decisions that cannot wait.","phrase":"We need to support the decision and check what can safely wait."},{"label":"Valid prior refusal","change":"An advance refusal may apply to the current treatment.","action":"Check validity, applicability and legal requirements; seek timely advice if uncertain rather than assume a CPR form settles it.","phrase":"We need to check exactly what this document covers."}],"safetyNet":["Acute deterioration still needs assessment and treatment consistent with valid applicable decisions and the clinical situation.","Clarify which service to contact for a crisis and how the care plan will be available.","Explain how to request a review when wishes or circumstances change."],"followUp":"Name the coordinating clinician and triggers for review, such as deterioration, admission, changed preferences or a change in decision-making ability.","pitfalls":["Do not equate DNACPR with comfort-only care or automatic refusal of all treatment.","Do not assume next of kin has unrestricted authority or apply one UK nation’s legal rules universally."],"links":[{"label":"Official clinical and patient guidance","url":"https://www.gmc-uk.org/professional-standards/the-professional-standards/treatment-and-care-towards-the-end-of-life/cardiopulmonary-resuscitation-cpr","use":"Relevant clinical safety and patient information"},{"label":"NICE NG31 — recommendations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng31/chapter/recommendations","use":"Assessment, treatment or referral recommendations relevant to this case"}],"reviewed":"2026-11-11","tags":["advance","care,","cpr","and","treatment"],"retrieval":{"question":"What changes in the first permutation, and what action would you explain to the patient or family?","answer":"Respect the decision within the applicable law, explain consequences and record clearly; do not equate disagreement with incapacity. I want to make sure you understand the options and that we record your wishes accurately.","checks":["Identified the change in risk or need","Specified action, urgency and ownership","Used clear language and checked practical understanding"]}}]},{"id":"cardiovascular","title":"Cardiovascular","description":"Raised blood pressure, atrial fibrillation and acute chest-pain triage, each with management discussion and three changed circumstances.","icon":"04","reviewed":"2026-11-12","cases":[{"id":"cv-hypertension","title":"Raised blood pressure: confirmation, risk and a shared plan","summary":"Confirm the pattern, assess urgency and discuss prevention in terms the person can use.","presentation":"A 52-year-old has repeat clinic readings around 158/96 mmHg at a health check. They feel well and ask whether tablets are really necessary. There are no supplied symptoms of acute target-organ injury; these must still be assessed.","assessment":["Repeat and interpret measurements using an appropriate validated device, cuff and technique; check pulse regularity and relevant postural symptoms.","Ask about chest pain, breathlessness, confusion, neurological or visual symptoms and other findings that change urgency.","Review previous readings, current medicines and substances, kidney or cardiovascular disease, diabetes, pregnancy possibility where relevant, family history and cardiovascular risk.","Assess for target-organ damage and relevant baseline investigations using NG136; explore daily routines, beliefs, treatment burden and what the patient wants to understand."],"priorities":["Separate a routine confirmation pathway from severe hypertension with acute symptoms or signs.","Explain why a person who feels well may still benefit from assessment and prevention."],"management":[{"step":"1. Establish the appropriate urgency","detail":"Suspected acute organ injury or other time-critical illness requires immediate/same-day specialist assessment according to the clinical picture. Severe clinic BP of 180/120 mmHg or higher needs the specific NG136 pathway, not an automatic long wait for home readings."},{"step":"2. Confirm rather than label from one reading","detail":"For clinic BP in the non-severe diagnostic range, offer ABPM; use HBPM if ABPM is unsuitable or not tolerated. Hypertension is confirmed with clinic BP at least 140/90 and an ABPM daytime or HBPM average at least 135/85 mmHg. Use the correct monitoring protocol."},{"step":"3. Assess the wider risk","detail":"Check for target-organ damage and calculate cardiovascular risk using the appropriate current tool. Explain the findings and any relevant kidney, diabetic or cardiovascular conditions that affect the plan."},{"step":"4. Discuss prevention and treatment","detail":"Offer practical lifestyle support. Discuss antihypertensive treatment according to confirmed stage, age, risk, organ damage and comorbidities. Do not imply that lifestyle change and indicated medication are mutually exclusive."},{"step":"5. Choose and monitor treatment safely","detail":"If prescribing, use the current NICE step algorithm and prescribing reference, considering age, relevant comorbidities, contraindications, interactions, pregnancy potential and monitoring. Agree an individual BP target; frailty and postural symptoms may change the approach."},{"step":"6. Make monitoring workable","detail":"Demonstrate home technique if used, check access to an appropriate monitor and agree who reviews the readings. Set a definite confirmation/result discussion and earlier checks after any medicine change as appropriate to the drug and patient."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"I feel fine. Why should we do anything?","response":"High blood pressure often has no symptoms. We check it carefully because, over time, it can increase the chance of a stroke, heart or kidney problems. Our next step is to confirm the pattern and discuss what would help you.","why":"Link prevention to a clear first action without using fear or diagnosing from one value."},{"cue":"Is it just because I get nervous here?","response":"That can affect a reading. A monitor during your usual day, or a properly recorded set of home readings, helps us see whether the pressure is also raised outside the surgery.","why":"Explain why the confirmation process changes the decision."},{"cue":"I don’t want to be on tablets forever.","response":"Could you tell me what concerns you most about taking them? We can discuss the likely benefit, possible side effects, what changes you want to make and how we would review the plan.","why":"Explore the reason for reluctance and keep indicated treatment open for discussion."},{"cue":"Which number am I aiming for?","response":"We will agree a target that fits your age, overall health and any dizziness on standing. Home and surgery targets are different, so I will write down the one we want you to use.","why":"Make the target explicit for the individual rather than apply one number to every setting."},{"cue":"I can’t afford a home machine.","response":"Let’s check whether ambulatory monitoring or another available practice arrangement would work. We need a plan you can actually use, rather than asking you to buy something you cannot afford.","why":"Identify access barriers and confirm actual service availability."}],"variations":[{"label":"Severe BP with acute symptoms","change":"The reading is 192/124 mmHg and new chest pain or confusion is present.","action":"Arrange emergency/same-day acute specialist assessment according to the suspected condition. Do not wait for routine monitoring or attempt an unsupported rapid outpatient reduction.","phrase":"The new symptoms change the urgency. I’m concerned this needs emergency assessment now, and I will help arrange it."},{"label":"Clinic and out-of-office readings differ","change":"The daytime ABPM average is below the diagnostic threshold despite raised clinic readings.","action":"Review the quality of readings and the whole clinical picture. Do not automatically label sustained hypertension; consider a white-coat effect and agree risk management and subsequent monitoring.","phrase":"The readings outside the surgery are different. Let’s check what that means for your risk and agree how we will keep an eye on it."},{"label":"Frailty and postural symptoms","change":"An older frail person reports dizziness and falls after a recent treatment change.","action":"Check lying/standing BP, actual medicines, falls and other causes. Individualise targets and treatment burden, using standing measurements where indicated and arranging clinically appropriate review.","phrase":"We want to reduce future risk while keeping you steady and safe now. Let’s review the standing readings and the medicines together."}],"pitfalls":["Do not diagnose sustained hypertension from a single clinic value or dismiss a severely raised reading without assessment.","Do not automatically send every asymptomatic severe reading through the same pathway as severe BP with acute organ injury; follow the defined NG136 severe-hypertension pathway.","Do not use one universal target or medication regimen for pregnancy, CKD, frailty and all other adults."],"safetyNet":["New chest pain, severe breathlessness, focal neurological symptoms, confusion or severe acute illness needs urgent/emergency assessment according to severity.","With BP at least 180/120 and no same-day-referral features, assess for target-organ damage promptly and follow NG136: if no target-organ damage is found, ensure repeat/confirmatory assessment and clinical review within 7 days.","If monitoring, tests or the review cannot be arranged, contact the named team promptly rather than leave the confirmation process open."],"followUp":"Book a definite review of confirmation and baseline results, then drug-specific safety/response checks if treatment starts. Provide at least annual hypertension review thereafter, with earlier review for symptoms, poor control or treatment problems.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["blood pressure","hypertension","ABPM","HBPM","prevention","adherence"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG136: hypertension","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng136","use":"Measurement, confirmation, severe-hypertension pathways, targets and treatment."},{"label":"NHS: high blood pressure","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/high-blood-pressure/","use":"Plain-language explanation and patient information."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the need for out-of-office confirmation, and how does 192/124 with new chest pain change the plan?","answer":"Explain that out-of-office readings help establish whether pressure remains raised outside the surgery. New chest pain with severe BP changes the priority to acute specialist/emergency assessment; do not wait for routine confirmation.","checks":["Explained confirmation and what it changes","Recognised acute symptoms and severe BP","Specified an actual assessment and follow-up owner"]}},{"id":"cv-atrial-fibrillation","title":"New atrial fibrillation: symptoms, stroke prevention and bleeding concerns","summary":"Separate stability, rhythm-related symptoms and prevention of stroke, then make an individual plan.","presentation":"A clinically stable 72-year-old man with hypertension has AF confirmed on a 12-lead ECG after palpitations. He asks whether an irregular heartbeat means an immediate stroke and is worried about bleeding if a new medicine is offered.","assessment":["Check haemodynamic stability, chest pain, breathlessness, syncope, acute heart failure and neurological symptoms; instability or suspected stroke changes urgency.","Clarify onset, duration, symptom burden, previous episodes, comorbidities, current medicines and possible contributors; arrange appropriate examination and investigations.","Assess stroke risk with CHA₂DS₂-VASc and bleeding risk with ORBIT using verified clinical information. Review renal function, interactions, previous bleeding, valve disease and other factors that affect safe treatment.","Explore the patient’s goals, understanding, actual medicine use, falls, alcohol and ability to follow a monitoring and treatment plan."],"priorities":["Treat acute instability or suspected stroke as urgent/emergency care.","Explain that symptom control and prevention of stroke are related but separate decisions."],"management":[{"step":"1. Establish and document the diagnosis","detail":"Use the actual ECG and assessment. If episodes are intermittent and a resting ECG does not capture AF, arrange appropriate ambulatory ECG monitoring; a normal tracing between episodes does not exclude paroxysmal AF."},{"step":"2. Agree symptom-management assessment","detail":"Review rate/rhythm needs and possible causes with the patient. Select treatment and referral through current guidance, considering heart failure, duration, symptoms, comorbidities and suitability rather than giving one regimen to every patient."},{"step":"3. Assess stroke and bleeding risk explicitly","detail":"In this supplied example, age 65–74 and hypertension each contribute to the stroke-risk assessment. Calculate the actual score and use ORBIT to identify bleeding risk and modifiable factors. Explain the estimates and their limits."},{"step":"4. Discuss appropriate anticoagulation","detail":"NICE offers a DOAC for AF with CHA₂DS₂-VASc at least 2 and considers it for men with a score of 1, taking bleeding risk into account. Check the specific agent’s indication, renal function, interactions, dose criteria and contraindications; some valve conditions need a different pathway."},{"step":"5. Reduce avoidable harm","detail":"Address uncontrolled BP, interacting medicines such as inappropriate NSAIDs, alcohol and treatable causes of anaemia where relevant. Do not withhold anticoagulation solely because of age or falls, and do not use aspirin alone as AF stroke prevention."},{"step":"6. Close the practical plan","detail":"Give clear medicine and missed-dose advice for the actual agent, accessible written information, monitoring arrangements and an agreed review. Record a shared decision and reassess if anticoagulation is declined, unsuitable or later reconsidered."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does this mean I am having a stroke?","response":"An irregular rhythm can increase the risk of a clot forming over time, but it does not mean you are having a stroke now. New stroke symptoms need emergency help. Let’s discuss both the rhythm symptoms and how we can reduce future risk.","why":"Distinguish current symptoms from longer-term risk without ignoring an acute neurological presentation."},{"cue":"Why are there two parts to the treatment?","response":"One part of the plan helps control the heart rhythm or rate and how you feel. Another considers reducing the chance of a clot causing a stroke. We should discuss each clearly.","why":"Avoid implying that symptom improvement removes the need to assess stroke prevention."},{"cue":"A blood thinner sounds dangerous.","response":"Bleeding is an important risk to discuss. We can look at your stroke risk, the bleeding risk and what we can change to make treatment safer, then explain why a particular option is recommended for you.","why":"Balance benefit and harm using the person’s actual risk and preferences."},{"cue":"I have fallen before. Does that rule it out?","response":"The falls matter and we should assess why they happen. A history of falls alone does not automatically rule out stroke-prevention treatment. Let’s review the risks and practical ways to make the plan safer.","why":"Address falls without treating them as a blanket contraindication."},{"cue":"What if I miss a dose?","response":"The advice depends on the medicine and timing. I will give you the instructions for your actual prescription, including missed doses, bleeding warnings and who to contact. Please check rather than guess or double a dose.","why":"Make medicine instructions specific to the selected agent."}],"variations":[{"label":"New instability","change":"The person develops collapse, ongoing chest pain, severe breathlessness or other signs of haemodynamic compromise.","action":"Arrange immediate emergency assessment and handover. Do not delay for a routine stroke score or outpatient rate-control discussion.","phrase":"The symptoms now need emergency care. I will arrange help and pass on the ECG and what has changed."},{"label":"Anticoagulation declined because of falls","change":"The patient fears that a single fall would inevitably cause a fatal bleed.","action":"Explore the fear, assess the falls and actual bleeding risks, explain benefit/harm and address modifiable factors. Avoid withholding solely on falls; document the shared decision and agree reassessment.","phrase":"Let’s look at what has caused the falls and what your individual treatment risks are, rather than assume that one fact decides everything."},{"label":"Symptoms not captured on today’s ECG","change":"Episodes are intermittent and the resting ECG is normal.","action":"Do not label the symptoms harmless from that tracing. Arrange monitoring of suitable duration to capture suspected paroxysmal AF, guided by episode frequency and the relevant pathway; assess other causes and current urgency.","phrase":"The tracing shows what the heart was doing at that moment. A monitor can help us find out what happens during an episode."}],"pitfalls":["Do not confuse anticoagulation with symptom treatment or assume a normal resting ECG excludes intermittent AF.","Do not make a universal DOAC choice or dose without checking valve disease, renal function, interactions and the indication.","Do not prescribe aspirin alone for AF stroke prevention or withhold anticoagulation solely for age/falls."],"safetyNet":["New facial weakness, arm weakness, speech difficulty or other sudden neurological symptoms needs emergency assessment.","Collapse, severe chest pain or severe breathlessness needs emergency help rather than waiting for routine AF review.","For prescribed anticoagulation, provide agent-specific advice about significant/recurrent bleeding and emergency features; ensure a named route for questions and monitoring problems."],"followUp":"Arrange timely review of symptoms, treatment choice, safety tests and ability to follow the plan. Reassess stroke/bleeding risk when circumstances change and at guideline-recommended intervals; actively review if treatment is declined or stopped.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["AF","atrial fibrillation","palpitations","anticoagulation","stroke prevention","ORBIT"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG196: atrial fibrillation","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng196","use":"Diagnosis, risk assessment, anticoagulation and rate/rhythm management."},{"label":"NHS: atrial fibrillation","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/atrial-fibrillation/","use":"Patient explanation and urgent symptom advice."},{"label":"NHS: anticoagulant adverse effects","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/anticoagulants/side-effects/","use":"Bleeding and other medicine safety information."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the separate goals of AF treatment to someone worried about bleeding, and what does a history of falls change?","answer":"Distinguish symptom control from stroke prevention. Review verified stroke and bleeding risks, explain relevant benefits/harms and address falls and other modifiable factors. Falls alone should not determine withholding anticoagulation.","checks":["Separated current urgency, symptoms and prevention","Used individual stroke/bleeding assessment","Addressed the concern and monitoring plan without a blanket rule"]}},{"id":"cv-chest-pain","title":"Chest pain by telephone: recognise possible ACS and arrange help","summary":"Use a brief focused assessment to choose urgency, then communicate and hand over clearly.","presentation":"A 58-year-old describes twenty minutes of central chest heaviness with sweating and discomfort into the arm. The pain is still present during a telephone call. They ask whether it could simply be indigestion and do not want to leave a dependent partner alone.","assessment":["Establish exact location, a callback number and whether the person is alone; arrange emergency help without delay when the supplied symptoms suggest ACS.","Clarify current pain, onset, duration, radiation, breathlessness, sweating, nausea, collapse and relevant history and medicines without allowing questions to delay escalation.","Consider other time-critical causes of chest pain and clinical instability. Do not use a normal resting ECG or response to indigestion treatment/GTN to rule out ACS.","Assess the practical barrier to emergency care, the person’s understanding and decision-making ability if there is a concern; identify safe arrangements for the dependent partner."],"priorities":["The supplied current symptoms need emergency hospital assessment, not routine outpatient testing.","Make the next action explicit and confirm an accepted handover and practical support."],"management":[{"step":"1. State the concern and arrange emergency care","detail":"For suspected ACS with current pain, arrange emergency hospital referral/ambulance through the local pathway. Confirm location and contact details and do not ask the person to drive themselves."},{"step":"2. Keep initial care proportionate","detail":"Follow the acute chest-pain/ACS pathway while help is arranged. An ECG, if available, must not delay transfer. NICE recommends a single 300 mg aspirin loading dose as soon as possible unless there is clear aspirin allergy; check prior dosing and relevant clinical circumstances and communicate any treatment to the receiving team."},{"step":"3. Avoid false exclusion","detail":"Do not rule out ACS because pain resolves, an ECG is normal, the person is younger than expected or a previous episode was attributed to reflux. Referral urgency depends on current symptoms, timing and the relevant ECG/clinical findings."},{"step":"4. Address refusal and the actual barrier","detail":"Acknowledge the concern about the partner, explain the risk and recommended urgency, and seek a feasible support arrangement. If the person continues to decline, assess understanding and any capacity concern, seek appropriate senior/emergency advice and document the discussion and plan under the relevant framework."},{"step":"5. Make and document the handover","detail":"Pass the symptom history and timing, observations/ECG if obtained, treatment given, allergies and relevant risks. Confirm that the receiving service has accepted the handover; keep contact as appropriate while assistance is arranged."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Could it just be indigestion?","response":"It could have more than one cause, but the pressure, sweating and arm discomfort make me concerned about a heart-related emergency. I recommend emergency assessment now. I will help arrange the ambulance.","why":"Explain the reason for urgency without claiming a confirmed diagnosis."},{"cue":"Can I drive myself in?","response":"Please do not drive yourself. Your symptoms could worsen on the way. Let’s confirm where you are and arrange the safest emergency help.","why":"Link the recommendation to practical transport rather than only giving a destination."},{"cue":"I cannot leave my partner alone.","response":"I hear that you are worried about your partner. Your symptoms still need emergency assessment. Let’s work out who can support them while the ambulance is arranged, rather than leave you both without help.","why":"Acknowledge the barrier while retaining the urgent recommendation."},{"cue":"The pain has eased. Do I still need to go?","response":"The improvement is useful to know, but it does not by itself rule out a heart problem. Because of what has happened and how recently it occurred, we need to keep the urgent assessment plan.","why":"Do not use symptom resolution as a standalone safety test."},{"cue":"What if this turns out to be nothing serious?","response":"That would be reassuring. The safest way to find out is the appropriate emergency assessment, because waiting could be harmful if this is a heart problem.","why":"Support the action without promising the outcome."}],"variations":[{"label":"Pain stops during the call","change":"The same recent concerning episode has resolved but no ECG is available.","action":"Do not cancel escalation solely because symptoms stop. Follow CG95’s recent-pain pathway; suspected ACS with pain in the last 12 hours and no available ECG still requires emergency referral.","phrase":"I’m glad the pain has eased, but that alone does not make it safe to wait. We still need the emergency assessment."},{"label":"Patient continues to decline hospital","change":"The person understands the recommendation but fears leaving a dependent partner.","action":"Explore the barrier, offer practical arrangements, explain the risk and options and assess capacity if there is a concern. Respect the relevant decision-making framework, seek appropriate advice and record a concrete safety plan; disagreement alone is not incapacity.","phrase":"Could you tell me what you understand about the risk of waiting? I want to work with you on the safest plan and the help your partner needs."},{"label":"A different stable exertional pattern","change":"In a separate scenario, a completed assessment identifies predictable exertional discomfort without acute or recent ACS features.","action":"Use the stable chest-pain diagnostic pathway, with appropriate timely assessment and CT coronary angiography when indicated. Give clear advice for prolonged, rest or changing pain. Do not relabel the original current-pain case as stable just to avoid referral.","phrase":"This pattern needs a different assessment pathway. Let’s arrange it and agree what change would mean you should seek emergency help."}],"pitfalls":["Do not delay an emergency referral while completing a full routine history, arranging outpatient troponin or waiting for an ECG.","Do not exclude ACS from a normal ECG or improved pain alone.","Do not treat a care barrier as an informed refusal without exploring it, and do not assume disagreement automatically means incapacity."],"safetyNet":["For the supplied ongoing possible-ACS presentation, the immediate action is emergency care now; safety-net wording is not a substitute for escalation.","Confirm who is calling/arranging emergency help and what happens if the call disconnects; avoid an ambiguous assumption that someone else has acted.","In a genuinely stable outpatient scenario, new persistent/rest pain, collapse, severe breathlessness or other acute change requires emergency reassessment."],"followUp":"Confirm the actual emergency handover and document action. After discharge, reconcile the diagnosis, treatment and follow-up arrangements, including rehabilitation and secondary prevention if indicated; an emergency referral should not disappear from continuity of care.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["chest pain","ACS","heart attack","urgent triage","refusal","carer"],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG95: recent-onset suspected cardiac chest pain","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg95","use":"Current and recent suspected-ACS referral pathways and diagnostic assessment."},{"label":"NICE NG185: acute coronary syndromes","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng185","use":"Acute treatment and subsequent management."},{"label":"NHS: heart attack","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/heart-attack/","use":"Patient-facing emergency advice."}],"retrieval":{"question":"What would you say when the pain eases but the patient still does not want hospital because of a dependent partner?","answer":"Acknowledge the improvement and the care barrier, retain the appropriate recent-ACS emergency pathway, explain why symptom relief does not exclude danger and arrange practical support and an accepted handover.","checks":["Kept urgency linked to the clinical history and timing","Addressed the barrier without blame or false reassurance","Named an actual action, transport and handover owner"]}}]},{"id":"respiratory","title":"Respiratory","description":"Adult asthma and COPD: diagnosis and control, inhaler discussion, breathlessness and exacerbation decisions, each with three changed circumstances.","icon":"05","reviewed":"2026-11-12","cases":[{"id":"rs-adult-asthma","title":"Adult asthma: increasing reliever use and night symptoms","summary":"Assess current severity, actual inhaler use and technique, then agree an anti-inflammatory treatment plan.","presentation":"A 30-year-old with confirmed asthma requests another blue reliever inhaler. They report needing it most days and waking with symptoms. They are comfortable speaking now but stopped the preventer because of fears about steroids.","assessment":["Assess current symptoms and severity, including ability to speak, breathlessness at rest, exhaustion, response to reliever and relevant objective measures when feasible; do not delay emergency action to obtain a reading.","Explore day/night symptoms, activity limitation, reliever use, previous attacks/admissions, risk factors and actual treatment use.","Check the recorded basis for diagnosis and reconsider alternatives if uncertain. Objective testing follows the appropriate pathway; previous ICS treatment can affect test results.","Watch inhaler technique using the actual device, review adherence without blame, triggers, smoking/exposures and comorbidities, and explore the patient’s concerns and functional goals."],"priorities":["Separate an acute attack from a chronic-control review.","Address the reason for poor control before automatically increasing doses."],"management":[{"step":"1. Act on acute severity","detail":"Arrange emergency or urgent assessment when indicated by the current symptoms and local acute-asthma pathway. Do not wait for routine spirometry or a control-review appointment during a serious attack."},{"step":"2. Identify what is happening in real use","detail":"Check reliever frequency, anti-inflammatory treatment use, device technique, supply and barriers. Demonstrate technique and ask the patient to show it back. Review contributory exposures and comorbidities."},{"step":"3. Use the current age-appropriate pathway","detail":"For people aged 12 and over with newly diagnosed asthma, NG245 offers low-dose ICS/formoterol as-needed AIR therapy. Existing uncontrolled asthma may need an appropriate MART or other pathway step after review. Use the correct formulation, dose and licence; not every ICS/LABA combination is a reliever."},{"step":"4. Avoid SABA-only management in asthma","detail":"Do not prescribe a SABA without an accompanying ICS prescription. Discuss the role of anti-inflammatory treatment, relevant benefits/harms and the patient’s concerns rather than repeatedly issuing a reliever alone."},{"step":"5. Agree an achievable written action plan","detail":"Explain the actual regimen, routine/reliever roles, maximum use and deterioration actions for the selected device. Check understanding and access; plans for SABA and AIR/MART regimens are not interchangeable."},{"step":"6. Review response and safety","detail":"After starting or adjusting asthma medicines, review response in 8–12 weeks, or earlier for clinical need, side effects or deterioration. An attack needs early follow-up; NHS advice is GP review within 2 days even after improvement. Maintain ongoing control and risk review."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Why is using the blue inhaler a problem if it works?","response":"It is useful that you get relief, but needing it frequently and waking at night suggest the asthma is not well controlled. We need to review the inflammation treatment and how the inhalers fit together.","why":"Acknowledge relief while explaining why frequency matters."},{"cue":"I’m scared of steroids.","response":"What have you heard that worries you most? The inhaled treatment aims to reduce airway inflammation and the chance of attacks. We can discuss the risks of the actual product and dose and how to use it safely.","why":"Explore a specific concern instead of dismissing it or claiming there are no harms."},{"cue":"Are you saying I use it wrongly?","response":"I check technique with everyone because the devices can be tricky and instructions are not always clear. Could you show me how you usually use yours, then we can work through it together?","why":"Make the review routine and collaborative, not a criticism."},{"cue":"Does this inhaler do both jobs?","response":"Some specific inhalers are used in a plan that covers both anti-inflammatory treatment and relief. We will make sure you know exactly which device you have and how your written plan says to use it.","why":"Avoid transferring AIR/MART instructions to a different device or combination."},{"cue":"I cannot remember extra doses during shifts.","response":"Let’s look at your actual routine and choose a suitable regimen you can follow. We can practise the device, write down the instructions and agree what to do if you miss a dose or need more relief.","why":"Address the barrier while keeping advice product-specific."}],"variations":[{"label":"Attack not responding to reliever","change":"The patient is worsening, struggling to speak or not improving with their reliever.","action":"Arrange emergency help and follow the acute-asthma action pathway. Confirm location, actual reliever/regimen and handover; do not continue a routine chronic review.","phrase":"Your breathing needs emergency help now. Let’s confirm where you are and arrange it, while following the emergency instructions for your actual inhaler."},{"label":"New diagnosis after objective assessment","change":"A symptomatic adult has asthma newly confirmed and no acute attack requiring a different pathway.","action":"Use NG245’s age-appropriate initial pharmacological pathway, including as-needed low-dose ICS/formoterol AIR therapy for ages 12 and over; check the actual product, licensing and instructions, teach technique and provide an action plan.","phrase":"We can choose a suitable treatment that includes anti-inflammatory medicine and relief, and I will show you exactly how your prescribed device should be used."},{"label":"Regimen seems ineffective despite reported use","change":"Control remains poor after a change, but the patient is using the device incorrectly or cannot obtain it reliably.","action":"Watch actual technique and establish access and use before reflex escalation. Correct barriers, reassess diagnosis and risks, and arrange a definite review or specialist advice if control remains inadequate.","phrase":"Before adding more medicine, let’s make sure this device and plan are actually working for you in day-to-day use."}],"pitfalls":["Do not use normal speech at one moment or a single reassuring measurement to dismiss a concerning attack history.","Do not continue SABA-only management for asthma or assume all ICS/LABA inhalers can be used as relievers.","Do not automatically apply adult/12-and-over pathways to younger children or delay acute treatment for diagnostic testing."],"safetyNet":["An attack with worsening symptoms, poor reliever response, severe breathing difficulty, exhaustion or altered consciousness requires emergency help through the actual action plan.","More frequent relief, night waking, reduced activity or concern about treatment needs prompt clinical review; do not wait for a planned routine date if worsening.","Give written device-specific instructions and a contact route if the inhaler cannot be obtained, instructions are unclear or the plan is not working."],"followUp":"Agree a named review and assess symptoms, function, reliever use, attacks, technique, actual use and adverse effects. Usual medication-response review is 8–12 weeks, with earlier action when needed; review promptly after an attack and continue scheduled asthma reviews.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["adult asthma","AIR","MART","ICS","reliever","inhaler technique"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG245: BTS/NICE/SIGN asthma guidance","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng245","use":"Objective assessment, age-specific treatment, monitoring and action plans."},{"label":"NHS: asthma","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/asthma/","use":"Patient explanation, attacks and early review."}],"retrieval":{"question":"What would you say to a patient who gets relief from frequent SABA use but stopped anti-inflammatory treatment because of fear?","answer":"Acknowledge relief, explain that frequency and night symptoms suggest poor control, explore the specific treatment concern and review technique/actual use. Agree an appropriate ICS-containing plan, device-specific instructions and review; escalate any current attack.","checks":["Assessed current severity","Explored the barrier and actual use","Recommended an appropriate anti-inflammatory plan and clear follow-up"]}},{"id":"rs-copd","title":"COPD: breathlessness, inhaler review and a request for oxygen","summary":"Confirm the diagnosis and current stability, address function and use an individual management plan.","presentation":"A 67-year-old with previously confirmed COPD reports gradually limiting breathlessness and asks for home oxygen. They use inhalers inconsistently and have had several courses from rescue packs. There is no supplied acute deterioration; assess this rather than assume stability.","assessment":["Check whether breathlessness is new or acutely worse, including chest pain, fever, haemoptysis, severe hypoxia, confusion or other findings suggesting urgent illness or another diagnosis.","Review the basis for COPD diagnosis, symptoms and exposures; use quality post-bronchodilator spirometry in the appropriate clinical context rather than a smoking history alone.","Assess functional limitation, exacerbations/admissions, sputum pattern, oxygen saturation where appropriate, weight/nutrition, comorbidities and possible asthma/steroid-responsive features.","Watch device technique, establish actual medicines and rescue-pack use, smoking status and support needs, and ask what the patient most wants to be able to do."],"priorities":["Separate stable limitation from an acute exacerbation or a different serious cause.","Treat breathlessness and prevention needs rather than prescribe oxygen from symptoms alone."],"management":[{"step":"1. Confirm the clinical picture","detail":"Review the diagnostic evidence and current assessment. Consider alternative or additional causes of breathlessness and investigate/refer when the pattern, examination or warning features require it."},{"step":"2. Optimise practical treatment","detail":"Check device suitability, technique, actual use and benefit before changing inhalers. Select bronchodilator/other maintenance treatment using NG115, accounting for asthma/steroid-responsive features, exacerbations, symptoms, adverse effects and patient preferences."},{"step":"3. Offer broader support","detail":"Offer smoking-cessation treatment and support where relevant, appropriate vaccinations, activity/nutrition advice and pulmonary rehabilitation when breathlessness limits function and the person is suitable. Address anxiety, social needs and access."},{"step":"4. Assess oxygen properly","detail":"Home oxygen requires assessment against clinical hypoxaemia criteria and a formal safety/risk process; breathlessness alone is not an indication. Smoking and household risks must be addressed through the appropriate specialist pathway."},{"step":"5. Make an exacerbation plan specific","detail":"Explain how to recognise a sustained change from the usual state and when to seek help. Rescue medicines are for appropriate patients who understand their use, with reporting and review; repeated courses need reassessment."},{"step":"6. Treat an actual exacerbation when indicated","detail":"Assess severity, differential diagnosis and setting. For appropriate COPD exacerbations requiring oral corticosteroids, NICE recommends oral prednisolone 30 mg daily for 5 days, with suitability and existing therapy checked. Consider antibiotics using NG114, including sputum colour/volume/thickness changes, severity, complications and previous cultures; not every exacerbation needs them."},{"step":"7. Agree response and continuity checks","detail":"Confirm the actual inhaler and self-management instructions, arrange review of any change and consider specialist input for persistent symptoms, diagnostic uncertainty or complex oxygen/exacerbation needs. Give an annual or clinically appropriate more frequent review plan."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Would oxygen make walking easier?","response":"We should check why you are breathless and whether your blood oxygen is low. Oxygen helps in particular circumstances and needs a proper assessment and safety plan. We can also look at treatments and rehabilitation that may help your day-to-day activity.","why":"Validate the goal while explaining why oxygen is not prescribed from the symptom alone."},{"cue":"There are too many inhalers.","response":"Could you show me what you actually use on a usual day? Let’s check what each one is for, whether the device suits you and how we can make a safe plan you can follow.","why":"Explore treatment burden and actual use before changing doses or devices."},{"cue":"I’ve used another rescue pack. Can I have a replacement?","response":"Let’s review what changed, what you took and whether you improved. Repeated courses can mean we need to reassess the cause and the prevention plan rather than only replace the pack.","why":"Make rescue-pack use visible and clinically reviewed."},{"cue":"Are you just going to tell me to stop smoking?","response":"Stopping can make an important difference, and we can offer practical treatment and support if you would like it. We will also assess and treat the breathing problems and discuss what matters to you now.","why":"Offer help without blame or making all other care conditional on cessation."},{"cue":"Exercise feels impossible.","response":"That is exactly the sort of difficulty a suitable rehabilitation programme can help us work on. Let’s discuss what it involves, whether it is appropriate for you and how you could attend.","why":"Describe structured rehabilitation rather than simply telling the person to exercise more."}],"variations":[{"label":"Severe acute deterioration","change":"The person is now very breathless at rest, confused, cyanosed or otherwise seriously unwell.","action":"Arrange emergency assessment and appropriate acute treatment/oxygen management through the clinical pathway. Do not assume a routine COPD flare or issue only a rescue pack.","phrase":"The change in your breathing needs emergency assessment now. I will help arrange it and pass on your usual treatment and what has changed."},{"label":"Repeated antibiotics without a clear review","change":"Several packs have been used for any increase in cough, without documenting sputum change, response or complications.","action":"Reassess the episode and diagnosis, antibiotic indication, cultures where appropriate and prevention. Check for other causes and harms of repeated treatment, and agree when/how to use future rescue medicines.","phrase":"Let’s go through what happened with each course and make the instructions clearer, so treatment matches the change in your symptoms."},{"label":"Breathlessness with acceptable oxygen levels","change":"The person is stable and oxygen assessment does not show an indication for home oxygen.","action":"Explain the result and address other treatable contributors, inhalers, rehabilitation, anxiety/function and specialist needs. Do not give oxygen solely because the person remains breathless.","phrase":"The oxygen result helps us choose the treatment. Let’s focus on the other things that can improve your breathing and daily activity."}],"pitfalls":["Do not diagnose COPD from smoking alone or attribute new chest pain, haemoptysis, weight loss or sudden breathlessness to established COPD without assessment.","Do not assume asthma and COPD use identical inhaler pathways or that every person with COPD needs an inhaled steroid.","Do not prescribe home oxygen for breathlessness alone or use repeated oral steroids/antibiotics as routine stable management without reviewing indications and harms."],"safetyNet":["Severe or rapidly worsening breathlessness, difficulty speaking, confusion, blue/grey colour, collapse or other serious acute symptoms require emergency help.","For a sustained increase in usual symptoms, follow the individual action plan and seek clinical advice according to severity; do not wait through deterioration because a rescue pack has been started.","Report rescue-pack courses and any treatment failure, significant adverse effect or inability to use the prescribed device through the named clinical team."],"followUp":"Arrange an early clinically appropriate review after exacerbation or treatment change, checking recovery and prevention needs. In stable care review symptoms, function, attacks, technique, actual use, smoking support and comorbidities at least annually, or more often when clinically indicated.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["COPD","breathlessness","oxygen","rescue pack","pulmonary rehabilitation","inhalers"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG115: COPD diagnosis and management","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng115","use":"Assessment, stable management, oxygen, rehabilitation and exacerbations."},{"label":"NICE NG114: antibiotics for COPD exacerbations","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng114","use":"Antibiotic decision-making and current prescribing tables."},{"label":"NHS: COPD","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/chronic-obstructive-pulmonary-disease-copd/","use":"Patient explanation and treatment overview."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you respond to a request for oxygen and another rescue pack without making either the automatic answer to breathlessness?","answer":"Assess current severity and possible alternative causes, review actual inhalers and recent courses, and explain that oxygen needs formal hypoxaemia/safety assessment. Agree appropriate treatment, prevention, rehabilitation and a clear exacerbation/review plan.","checks":["Assessed stability and possible additional diagnoses","Explained oxygen and rescue-treatment decisions accurately","Addressed the person’s goal, practical use and follow-up"]}}]},{"id":"endocrine-metabolic","title":"Endocrine & metabolic","description":"Current type 2 diabetes treatment discussions, underactive-thyroid care and possible type 1/DKA recognition, each with three changed circumstances.","icon":"06","reviewed":"2026-11-12","cases":[{"id":"em-type2-diabetes","title":"New type 2 diabetes: explain the diagnosis and current treatment choices","summary":"Connect confirmation, the 2026 medicine pathway, self-management and ongoing checks in a practical plan.","presentation":"A 57-year-old has diabetes confirmed after repeat testing and appropriate clinical assessment. There are no acute hyperglycaemic symptoms and no relevant cardiovascular/renal comorbidity or significant frailty identified in this example. The person feels blamed and asks whether one tablet is the whole treatment.","assessment":["Confirm the diagnostic evidence and clinical classification; review symptoms, speed of onset, weight change and possible type 1 or secondary diabetes rather than decide type from age or BMI alone.","Assess current illness and features of ketosis/DKA or marked symptomatic hyperglycaemia; an acute picture changes treatment and urgency.","Assess cardiovascular and renal status, frailty, current medicines, interactions, weight/nutrition, symptoms of complications and other comorbidities before selecting a treatment pathway.","Explore the person’s understanding, concerns, daily routine, health literacy, access and goals; establish baseline checks and structured education/support needs."],"priorities":["Separate a stable confirmed type 2 diagnosis from an insulin-deficient or acutely unwell presentation.","Use current NG28 and an individual plan, including the 2026 medicine changes and comorbidity-specific pathways."],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain the diagnosis without blame","detail":"Use the actual test/clinical findings, acknowledge the effect of the diagnosis and explain the plan for glucose, broader health and prevention. Offer structured diabetes education and appropriate ongoing support."},{"step":"2. Select the correct current pathway","detail":"The current NG28 summary recommends modified-release metformin and an SGLT-2 inhibitor for adults with no relevant comorbidities, with different details for other groups. Assess renal/cardiovascular disease, obesity, early-onset disease and frailty and use the appropriate current table, licensing and local implementation."},{"step":"3. Introduce medicines safely","detail":"The current visual summary advises starting medicines sequentially, not simultaneously, and moving through treatment after confirming the maximum tolerable current dose. Choose the actual agent/dose with renal function, contraindications, interactions and patient preferences checked; do not interpret a combination recommendation as an automatic same-day double prescription."},{"step":"4. Explain benefits and harms clearly","detail":"Discuss what each medicine aims to achieve, relevant adverse effects and what to do if problems occur. With SGLT-2 inhibitors discuss dehydration/genital-infection concerns, ketoacidosis risk, appropriate sick-day guidance and when to seek help; risks and suitability are individual."},{"step":"5. Agree achievable self-management and targets","detail":"Discuss food, activity, weight goals and support in the person’s circumstances. Agree an individual glycaemic target and monitoring plan rather than a universal target or a promise of rapid normalisation. Include BP, lipid, kidney, foot and eye assessment through the relevant pathways."},{"step":"6. Make monitoring and sick-day advice concrete","detail":"Write down the introduction/titration plan, safety checks, who reviews results and when to seek help. Use an individual sick-day plan: acute illness, dehydration or surgery can change medicines and monitoring. Suspected DKA on an SGLT-2 inhibitor needs urgent assessment even without very high glucose."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Have I caused this?","response":"This result is not a judgement about you. Several factors can contribute. Let’s focus on what the results mean for your health and the steps we can take together.","why":"Reduce shame and make the next actions understandable."},{"cue":"Is the treatment just one tablet?","response":"We will choose the plan that fits your health and the current guidance. It often includes more than one medicine working in different ways, alongside practical support and checks. We introduce treatment carefully and review how you are getting on.","why":"Explain the current individual plan without using an outdated universal sequence or promising a fixed regimen."},{"cue":"Why start medicines one at a time?","response":"Introducing them in sequence helps us check how you manage each one, adjust the dose safely and understand any side effects. I will write down when the next step and review should happen.","why":"Turn the sequencing instruction into a practical schedule with ownership."},{"cue":"How will I know whether a side effect is serious?","response":"We will go through the warnings for the actual medicines. If you develop vomiting, abdominal pain, unusual sleepiness or deep breathing, seek urgent emergency assessment rather than assume it is a normal tablet side effect.","why":"Name relevant urgent symptoms and avoid false reassurance about DKA."},{"cue":"I work shifts and eat at different times.","response":"Let’s look at a typical working day and choose instructions you can follow safely. We can also discuss support with meals, activity and attending checks, rather than give you a plan that does not fit your life.","why":"Link treatment to the person’s circumstances and feasible support."},{"cue":"Do two medicines mean I have failed?","response":"The choice considers your health and what each medicine can help with, not a judgement about your effort. We will explain why the recommended plan fits you and introduce it with the right support and checks.","why":"Address shame and explain the reason for the actual individual recommendation."}],"variations":[{"label":"Rapid symptoms or possible insulin deficiency","change":"The person has rapid weight loss, thirst, frequent urination and becomes unwell or ketotic.","action":"Reassess classification and urgency; possible type 1/insulin deficiency and DKA need appropriate same-day specialist or emergency assessment. Do not simply add routine type 2 tablets or wait for HbA1c/antibody confirmation.","phrase":"The speed of this change means we need a different assessment today. If you are unwell or have ketones, we need urgent hospital help."},{"label":"Kidney disease changes eligibility","change":"Renal assessment shows significant CKD, including a level where metformin is contraindicated.","action":"Use NG28’s current eGFR-specific pathway and the actual agent’s licence/dose criteria; the no-comorbidity plan cannot simply be copied. Seek appropriate renal/diabetes input where needed.","phrase":"Your kidney result changes which medicines and doses are suitable. Let’s choose the plan using that information."},{"label":"Frailty, dizziness and treatment burden","change":"A frail person has postural symptoms, multiple medicines and risk of adverse events.","action":"Use the frailty pathway, individualise targets and minimise burden. An SGLT-2 inhibitor is not automatic if frailty places the person at adverse-event risk; check the current alternatives and shared priorities.","phrase":"We need a plan that helps you while keeping you steady and making the medicine routine manageable."}],"pitfalls":["Do not use an old universal “metformin alone first for everyone” rule without checking the current NG28 pathway.","Do not assume all age/BMI patterns imply type 2 disease or delay assessment of ketosis, weight loss or acute illness.","Do not start medicines simultaneously merely because both appear in the initial recommendation, or ignore renal function, frailty and sick-day education."],"safetyNet":["Vomiting, abdominal pain, deep breathing, drowsiness or confusion with possible diabetes requires urgent assessment; severe illness or suspected DKA needs emergency hospital care.","SGLT-2-associated DKA may occur with near-normal glucose. If DKA is suspected, stop the SGLT-2 inhibitor and obtain urgent medical assessment. After DKA, do not restart an SGLT-2 inhibitor unless another cause has been identified and resolved and appropriate clinical review supports restarting.","Agree whom to contact for intolerance, dehydration, inability to eat/drink or a missed monitoring/review arrangement; provide clear medicine-specific advice."],"followUp":"Book medicine-introduction/tolerance and safety reviews and a named review of glycaemic and broader health targets. Continue structured diabetes reviews, education and complication checks, with earlier reassessment if symptoms, risks or treatment suitability change.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["type 2 diabetes","metformin","SGLT2","2026 guidance","shared decisions","sick day rules"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG28: type 2 diabetes management","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng28","use":"Current treatment, individualisation and review recommendations."},{"label":"NICE: current diabetes medicine visual summary","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng28/resources/visual-summary-choosing-reviewing-and-changing-medicines-pdf-10956472093","use":"Current initial and further medicine pathways, including the September 2026 clarification."},{"label":"MHRA: SGLT2 inhibitors and ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/sglt2-inhibitors-updated-advice-on-the-risk-of-diabetic-ketoacidosis","use":"Recognising DKA, including cases without marked hyperglycaemia."},{"label":"NHS: type 2 diabetes","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/type-2-diabetes/","use":"Plain-language diagnosis and ongoing care information."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the current initial plan and why CKD, frailty or acute symptoms could change it?","answer":"Use the current NG28 subgroup pathway, explain the role of the medicines and sequential introduction, and agree support and monitoring. Renal disease and frailty change suitability; possible insulin deficiency or DKA changes urgency and may replace routine oral treatment with an urgent specialist/emergency pathway.","checks":["Used the current subgroup pathway","Explained sequencing and patient-specific safety","Recognised an acute or classification-changing presentation"]}},{"id":"em-hypothyroidism","title":"Underactive thyroid: treatment, taking the tablet and follow-up","summary":"Distinguish overt from subclinical disease and agree replacement treatment and monitoring that fits the person.","presentation":"An adult reports fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation. Thyroid tests show raised TSH with FT4 below the laboratory reference range, and the clinical assessment supports primary hypothyroidism. They ask whether they need lifelong treatment and how the tablet fits with other medicines.","assessment":["Interpret TSH and FT4 in the clinical context, including medicines, supplements/biotin, recent illness and possible pituitary disease when relevant.","Assess symptom severity, other causes of fatigue, age, body weight, cardiovascular history and current medicines before choosing a starting dose.","Ask about pregnancy or plans for pregnancy where relevant, and consider autoimmune context and other appropriate investigations.","Explore the person’s expectations, usual meal/medicine routine, practical barriers and concerns about long-term treatment."],"priorities":["Confirm the actual biochemical and clinical pattern instead of treating fatigue alone or a single mildly raised TSH as overt disease.","Choose replacement and monitoring safely, with a different approach for older people, cardiovascular disease and pregnancy."],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain and agree treatment","detail":"Levothyroxine is the usual first-line replacement for primary hypothyroidism. Explain what it replaces and expected gradual improvement. Do not routinely offer liothyronine or natural thyroid extract as a superior standard option."},{"step":"2. Choose a clinically appropriate initial dose","detail":"NG145 considers 1.6 micrograms/kg/day, rounded to the nearest 25 micrograms, for adults under 65 with no cardiovascular disease. For adults 65 or over, or with cardiovascular disease, consider 25–50 micrograms/day with titration. Check the actual prescription, product and patient circumstances; these are not universal doses."},{"step":"3. Teach the actual administration routine","detail":"Use the prescribed product instructions. Current NHS advice describes taking it on an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before the first meal, caffeine or other medicine. Check interacting medicines/supplements and required separation with the pharmacist, and agree a sustainable consistent routine."},{"step":"4. Arrange monitoring rather than guess from symptoms alone","detail":"NICE considers TSH every 3 months until stable (two similar in-range results 3 months apart), then annually. Arrange appropriate checks after changes and consider FT4 if needed for persistent symptoms; avoid excessive replacement or repeated premature adjustments."},{"step":"5. Reassess context and continuing symptoms","detail":"If symptoms persist, review actual use, interactions, results and alternative causes rather than automatically increase the dose. Pregnancy/planning pregnancy needs its own timely review and monitoring pathway."},{"step":"6. Close prescription and results ownership","detail":"Confirm the daily dose in micrograms, repeat-prescription arrangements, test/review dates, who reviews results and what to do for adverse symptoms or practical problems."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"What does an underactive thyroid mean?","response":"The thyroid is making less hormone than your body needs. Levothyroxine replaces that hormone. We use your symptoms and blood tests to find a suitable dose.","why":"Explain replacement before giving a medicine name alone."},{"cue":"Will I feel normal tomorrow?","response":"Improvement is usually gradual, and we need follow-up to check the dose and how you are feeling. If symptoms continue, we will review the results, how the medicine is taken and other possible causes.","why":"Set a realistic expectation without promising a fixed recovery time."},{"cue":"Can I take it with breakfast and my other tablets?","response":"Let’s go through your usual morning. It is taken on an empty stomach before food or caffeine, and some other medicines need separation. I will give you the instructions for your actual prescription and check the other tablets with you.","why":"Make administration workable and avoid an unreviewed instruction to take every medicine together."},{"cue":"Would a higher dose give me more energy?","response":"Too much replacement can cause problems such as palpitations. We should adjust it using the assessment and blood results, rather than keep increasing it whenever you feel tired.","why":"Explain why monitoring matters without dismissing ongoing symptoms."},{"cue":"What if I become pregnant?","response":"Please contact us promptly so we can review the dose and arrange the right blood-test and pregnancy support plan. Thyroid treatment and monitoring often need to change in pregnancy.","why":"Make the need for early review explicit without giving a generic pregnancy dose."},{"cue":"Will I need it for life?","response":"That depends on the cause. Many people need long-term replacement, and we will explain what is likely in your case and review the blood results and treatment. Please discuss any wish to change or stop it with us so we can make a safe plan.","why":"Answer the original long-term-treatment concern without promising a fixed duration."}],"variations":[{"label":"Subclinical rather than overt disease","change":"TSH is mildly raised but FT4 is normal.","action":"Do not copy the overt-hypothyroidism plan automatically. Follow NG145’s repeat-testing and treatment-consideration criteria, including age, symptoms and persistence; discuss uncertainty and an appropriate review.","phrase":"This is a different blood-test pattern. Let’s confirm how persistent it is and discuss whether treatment or monitoring is the right next step."},{"label":"Pregnancy or pregnancy planning","change":"The person becomes pregnant or is planning pregnancy.","action":"Arrange timely thyroid/pregnancy review, dose assessment and closer monitoring through the appropriate pathway. Do not leave the usual annual review unchanged or stop prescribed replacement without clinical advice.","phrase":"This changes the monitoring and may change the dose. Let’s arrange the appropriate review promptly."},{"label":"Palpitations after a dose change","change":"The person develops palpitations or other possible over-replacement symptoms.","action":"Assess severity, dose/actual use, interactions and thyroid/cardiac findings. Acute chest pain, collapse or serious illness needs urgent/emergency assessment; do not assume every symptom is a harmless adjustment effect.","phrase":"We should check the new symptoms and the actual dose rather than assume they are expected. If there are serious symptoms, we need urgent assessment."}],"pitfalls":["Do not confuse micrograms and milligrams, or use the young/no-cardiovascular-disease dose in every older/cardiac patient.","Do not ignore interactions, administration routine, biotin/assay context or other causes of continuing symptoms.","Do not treat pregnancy, possible central thyroid disease or severe illness as a routine unchanged primary-hypothyroidism follow-up."],"safetyNet":["Acute chest pain, collapse, severe breathlessness or serious new illness needs urgent/emergency assessment, including when it follows a medicine change.","Report persistent palpitations, marked tremor, treatment intolerance or inability to follow the prescription for clinician review.","Contact the team promptly for pregnancy/planning pregnancy, an unresolved prescription or missed monitoring so the plan can be adjusted."],"followUp":"Confirm the actual initial and titration prescription and review arrangements. Use appropriate TSH monitoring until stable, then at least annual review, with earlier reassessment for symptoms, dose changes, interactions or pregnancy.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["hypothyroidism","thyroid","levothyroxine","TSH","FT4","medicine timing"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG145: thyroid disease","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng145","use":"Testing, primary/subclinical hypothyroidism, replacement and monitoring."},{"label":"NHS: underactive thyroid","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/underactive-thyroid-hypothyroidism/","use":"Patient explanation and pregnancy considerations."},{"label":"NHS: levothyroxine","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/levothyroxine/","use":"Current administration instructions and medicine information."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the tablet routine and why an older person with heart disease needs a different starting approach?","answer":"Explain hormone replacement, verify the exact dose/product and agree a consistent empty-stomach routine with interactions checked. Older age/cardiovascular disease changes starting-dose and titration considerations; blood results and clinical review guide adjustment.","checks":["Explained replacement and practical administration","Recognised the patient-specific starting approach","Specified monitoring and earlier-review triggers"]}},{"id":"em-new-type1","title":"Thirst, weight loss and vomiting: possible type 1 diabetes or DKA","summary":"Recognise insulin deficiency and acute danger, explain urgency and arrange a real same-day/emergency handover.","presentation":"A 28-year-old reports several weeks of thirst, frequent urination and weight loss, followed by vomiting, abdominal pain and deep breathing. They have not been diagnosed with diabetes. The current symptoms raise concern for an acute metabolic emergency.","assessment":["Assess immediate illness, responsiveness, breathing, circulation/dehydration and other acute symptoms. Confirm location and a callback route for a remote consultation.","Obtain appropriate immediate glucose and ketone assessment if feasible without delaying emergency care; hospital assessment establishes the biochemical diagnosis and treatment needs.","Clarify the symptom course, weight change, current/previous diabetes treatment, medicines including SGLT-2 inhibitors, intercurrent illness and relevant history while arranging the appropriate help.","Consider insulin deficiency regardless of age or BMI, other causes of hyperglycaemia/illness and the person’s ability to access urgent care safely."],"priorities":["The supplied vomiting, abdominal pain and deep breathing with diabetic symptoms needs emergency hospital assessment.","Do not wait for routine fasting tests, HbA1c, autoantibodies or an outpatient review before acting."],"management":[{"step":"1. Arrange emergency care for suspected DKA","detail":"Use the emergency pathway for the current unwell presentation. Confirm location, safe transport and an accepted handover. DKA is potentially life-threatening and requires urgent hospital treatment and monitoring."},{"step":"2. Use immediate tests without creating delay","detail":"Check glucose/ketones when available and appropriate, but do not delay escalation for testing. A less striking glucose result does not exclude DKA in relevant settings, including SGLT-2 inhibitor use."},{"step":"3. Avoid a routine type 2 prescription pathway","detail":"Do not rely on age/body size to exclude type 1 diabetes or start a routine oral-treatment trial in place of assessing insulin deficiency. Classification and specialist treatment should be coordinated through the urgent diabetes pathway."},{"step":"4. Match a stable suspected-type-1 scenario to urgent specialist care","detail":"If a person is clinically well without acute-DKA features but type 1 diabetes is suspected, arrange urgent specialist assessment, usually the same day under the relevant service. Confirm the actual arrangement and a safe alternative if that route cannot respond."},{"step":"5. Explain what will happen and address barriers","detail":"Explain that urgent care is needed to assess glucose/ketones, fluid balance and treatment. Resolve transport, caring responsibilities and communication support where possible; do not leave the person waiting alone with an unconfirmed referral."},{"step":"6. Reconnect after the acute episode","detail":"Check the confirmed diagnosis, treatment and discharge plan. Coordinate specialist diabetes education, insulin/monitoring support when relevant, sick-day advice, supply, follow-up and the person’s concerns."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can I just do the blood test tomorrow?","response":"The thirst and weight loss, together with vomiting and deep breathing, make me concerned about a diabetes emergency. I recommend hospital assessment now rather than waiting for tomorrow’s test. I will help arrange it.","why":"Connect symptoms with the action without pretending a remote assessment confirms the full diagnosis."},{"cue":"Why would this make me sick?","response":"If the body does not have enough insulin, chemicals called ketones can build up and make someone seriously unwell. We need urgent tests and treatment to check what is happening and correct it safely.","why":"Use a simple mechanism to support the urgency, not a long biochemical lecture."},{"cue":"I’m too old for type 1, aren’t I?","response":"Type 1 diabetes can develop in adults. Age alone does not tell us which type this is. Your current symptoms and assessment are what determine the next step.","why":"Correct a common misconception without diagnosing solely from age or symptoms."},{"cue":"The glucose number is not as high as I expected.","response":"The number is only part of the picture. Some diabetes emergencies can occur without a very high glucose reading, especially with certain medicines. We need to assess the symptoms and ketones appropriately.","why":"Avoid reassurance from one measurement when the clinical picture is concerning."},{"cue":"I’m frightened of what happens after hospital.","response":"That is understandable. First we need to make you safe and establish the diagnosis. Then the diabetes team and your practice can explain treatment, show you how to use it and agree support and follow-up.","why":"Acknowledge fear and sequence immediate care and longer-term learning."}],"variations":[{"label":"Clinically well but suspected type 1","change":"The person has new diabetic symptoms but no vomiting, deep breathing or other acute illness after assessment.","action":"Arrange urgent specialist assessment, usually the same day, through the appropriate diabetes pathway. Do not defer to a routine later appointment or await all classification tests before arranging care.","phrase":"You do not have the emergency features we checked for now, but this still needs urgent diabetes assessment today. Let’s confirm that arrangement."},{"label":"SGLT-2 inhibitor and modest glucose","change":"An adult taking an SGLT-2 inhibitor is unwell with DKA symptoms despite a glucose reading that is not markedly high.","action":"Assess for DKA urgently, including blood ketones where appropriate, and obtain emergency care when indicated. Stop the SGLT-2 inhibitor if DKA is suspected and follow MHRA/specialist guidance on subsequent treatment; do not simply restart after symptoms settle.","phrase":"This medicine can be associated with ketoacidosis even without very high glucose. We need urgent assessment rather than reassurance from that number."},{"label":"Different scenario: known type 1 during illness","change":"Someone already treated with insulin stops taking it because they are vomiting and not eating.","action":"Assess glucose, ketones, hydration and current danger urgently. Use the individual sick-day/diabetes-team plan; do not advise unsupported cessation of basal insulin. DKA symptoms or significant clinical deterioration require emergency care.","phrase":"Illness can change how much insulin and monitoring you need. Let’s get urgent advice and assess for ketones rather than stop the usual plan without support."}],"pitfalls":["Do not exclude type 1 diabetes from age or BMI, or attribute weight loss and thirst to lifestyle without assessing diabetes.","Do not delay emergency care while waiting for HbA1c or reassure from a near-normal glucose result in someone with possible DKA.","Do not issue a generic insulin correction dose or advise stopping insulin from an incomplete remote assessment."],"safetyNet":["For the supplied acutely unwell presentation, act now through emergency care; a future warning list is not a substitute for escalation.","If a clinically well person is awaiting the confirmed same-day specialist arrangement, vomiting, abdominal pain, deep breathing, drowsiness, confusion or deterioration requires immediate reassessment/emergency help.","Give a clear callback/contact plan if the call disconnects or the receiving service cannot respond; make sure a handover is accepted."],"followUp":"Confirm arrival/acceptance through the appropriate handover process. After acute care, reconcile the diagnosis and medicine/monitoring plan, ensure access to supplies and structured education and agree practice/specialist follow-up.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["type 1 diabetes","DKA","ketones","thirst","weight loss","urgent referral"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG17: type 1 diabetes in adults","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng17","use":"Assessment, classification and ongoing specialist diabetes care."},{"label":"NHS: type 1 symptoms and diagnosis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/type-1-diabetes/symptoms/","use":"Urgent assessment and usual same-day specialist care."},{"label":"NHS: diabetic ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/diabetic-ketoacidosis/","use":"Symptoms and emergency action."},{"label":"MHRA: SGLT2 and ketoacidosis","url":"https://www.gov.uk/drug-safety-update/sglt2-inhibitors-updated-advice-on-the-risk-of-diabetic-ketoacidosis","use":"Important near-normal-glucose presentations and medicine safety."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How does the plan differ between a clinically well person with suspected type 1 and someone with vomiting and deep breathing?","answer":"Suspected type 1 still needs urgent specialist assessment, usually the same day, with an actual confirmed route. Vomiting/deep breathing and possible DKA require emergency hospital assessment now. Testing and classification must not delay the appropriate care.","checks":["Matched setting/urgency to the current illness","Avoided waiting for routine or classification tests","Explained the reason and confirmed an accepted handover"]}}]},{"id":"gastrointestinal","title":"Gastrointestinal","description":"IBS-pattern symptoms, rectal bleeding/FIT and dyspepsia: assessment, test discussions and changed-risk pathways, each with three variations.","icon":"07","reviewed":"2026-11-12","cases":[{"id":"gi-ibs","title":"IBS-pattern symptoms: a positive explanation and an individual plan","summary":"Recognise the pattern, assess warning features and offer practical symptom-led care without dismissing the patient.","presentation":"A 29-year-old has eight months of intermittent abdominal pain associated with changes in stool pattern and bloating. Symptoms affect work and social life. Appropriate assessment and initial tests have not identified warning features or another cause in this ordinary scenario.","assessment":["Clarify the pain–stool relationship, duration, stool form/frequency, bloating, triggers, effect on life and the person’s explanation and concerns.","Assess for warning features and alternative diagnoses, including bleeding, weight loss, anaemia, inflammatory symptoms, masses and relevant family history. A new or changed pattern needs reassessment.","Use the appropriate examination and initial tests, including blood count, inflammatory markers and coeliac serology according to CG61. Interpret tests in the clinical context; coeliac testing needs an adequate gluten-containing diet during the diagnostic process.","When IBD versus IBS remains a relevant differential and specialist assessment is being considered, faecal calprotectin can support assessment if cancer is not suspected and a locally agreed pathway is in place (HTG320). Interpret it in context; it is not a test to exclude bowel cancer.","Review medicines, actual diet and any exclusions, nutrition, mental health/context and constipation/diarrhoea predominance without attributing all symptoms to stress."],"priorities":["Make an assessment-led positive explanation rather than label unexplained symptoms automatically as IBS.","Keep warning symptoms visible and avoid restrictive advice or unnecessary testing that does not answer the clinical question."],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain the working diagnosis","detail":"Relate the typical symptom pattern and assessed findings to IBS. Explain that symptoms are real and that gut sensitivity/function can contribute; do not imply the person is imagining them or guarantee that future symptoms need no assessment."},{"step":"2. Agree a small practical starting plan","detail":"Discuss regular meals, suitable fluids, activity and symptom/food patterns. Review fibre type and tolerance rather than simply advise unlimited bran. Identify what is most disruptive and agree a feasible first step."},{"step":"3. Tailor symptom treatment","detail":"Consider appropriate antispasmodic and constipation/diarrhoea options according to the pattern, contraindications and preferences. Avoid one drug for every phenotype; use the current prescribing reference and CG61."},{"step":"4. Use dietary expertise appropriately","detail":"If general advice is insufficient, consider further dietary management, including low-FODMAP/exclusion approaches, with a suitably trained professional. Avoid broad indefinite food restriction or starting a gluten-free diet before adequate coeliac assessment."},{"step":"5. Review persistent symptoms and next options","detail":"Check response, diagnosis, actual use and harms. For persistent symptoms consider the appropriate further treatment or specialist input; low-dose neuromodulator/psychological options require an explanation of purpose, suitability and review rather than imply the symptoms are psychiatric."},{"step":"6. Set a definite review and change plan","detail":"Agree what improvement would look like, a review date and specific earlier-help triggers. New warning features require investigation/referral rather than simply another IBS prescription."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Are you saying it is all in my head?","response":"No. The pain and bowel changes are real. In IBS, the way the gut works and senses signals can contribute to symptoms. We can discuss treatments aimed at the symptoms that are affecting you most.","why":"Validate the experience and explain a mechanism without a false mind/body split."},{"cue":"How can you diagnose it if there is no single test?","response":"We use the pattern of symptoms, examination and appropriate tests to check for other important causes. Your assessment fits the working diagnosis today, and we will review it if the pattern changes or new warning signs appear.","why":"Explain a positive clinical assessment and the limits of certainty."},{"cue":"Should I stop eating lots of different foods?","response":"Let’s first look at what you eat and what seems to affect symptoms. If a more detailed dietary approach is needed, we can arrange appropriate advice so your diet stays balanced and the changes have a clear purpose.","why":"Avoid a poorly supported restrictive diet."},{"cue":"Why would a medicine used for mood help bowel pain?","response":"Some medicines can affect how pain signals are processed and may be used at a low dose for that purpose. If we consider one, I will explain why, the possible side effects and how we would review it.","why":"Explain a possible treatment honestly without assuming the person has depression."},{"cue":"What is the first thing I should change?","response":"Of the pain, bloating and bowel changes, which is getting in the way most? Let’s choose one practical treatment step, agree what you will try and review the effect before changing several things together.","why":"Reduce cognitive burden and make response to the plan interpretable."}],"variations":[{"label":"New warning features","change":"Rectal bleeding, unexplained weight loss, anaemia or another concerning change develops.","action":"Reassess the diagnosis and follow the appropriate investigation/referral pathway. Do not attribute new warning findings to the previous IBS label.","phrase":"The new symptoms change what we need to check. We should reassess rather than assume this is the same problem."},{"label":"Diet becomes very restricted","change":"The person has removed multiple foods and is losing weight or struggling to eat adequately.","action":"Assess nutrition, weight change and the reason for restriction; reconsider other causes and arrange appropriate dietetic/clinical support. Avoid adding more exclusions without a safe plan.","phrase":"I’m concerned the current restrictions may be making it harder to stay well. Let’s review the symptoms and rebuild a safe, balanced plan with the right support."},{"label":"New IBS-type symptoms after age 50","change":"A person with female reproductive organs develops new IBS-type symptoms within the last 12 months at age 50 or over.","action":"Follow NG12’s appropriate ovarian-cancer assessment/testing pathway alongside the wider differential rather than assume a new functional-bowel diagnosis. Use the current age-/symptom-specific recommendations.","phrase":"The new pattern and your circumstances mean we should check other possible causes, including the relevant pelvic/ovarian pathway, rather than assume this is IBS."}],"pitfalls":["Do not dismiss symptoms as stress or diagnose IBS without considering warning features and appropriate initial assessment.","Do not use a previous IBS label or a single reassuring test to explain every new symptom.","Do not recommend a broadly restrictive or gluten-free diet without considering nutrition and the effect on coeliac testing."],"safetyNet":["New significant bleeding, black stools, persistent vomiting, severe/worsening pain, collapse or serious illness needs prompt urgent/emergency assessment according to severity.","New weight loss, anaemia, masses or an unexplained changed pattern requires reassessment and appropriate investigation/referral.","Agree when and who to contact if the trial fails or treatment causes problems; do not leave follow-up only as a vague “if worse”."],"followUp":"Review the specific symptom/function goals and treatment trial after an agreed interval. Further medicine options have their own safety/response reviews; new warning features prompt earlier investigation.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["IBS","abdominal pain","bloating","bowel change","diet","coeliac"],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG61: IBS in adults","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg61","use":"Assessment, initial tests, diet and symptom-directed treatment."},{"label":"NICE NG20: coeliac disease","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng20","use":"Appropriate testing and the effect of gluten exclusion."},{"label":"NHS: IBS","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/irritable-bowel-syndrome-ibs/","use":"Patient explanations and self-management information."},{"label":"NICE NG12: new and concerning symptom pathways","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12","use":"GI warning findings and the appropriate ovarian assessment for new IBS-type symptoms."},{"label":"NICE HTG320: faecal calprotectin","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/htg320","use":"Conditional use to support the IBD/IBS differential when cancer is not suspected and an appropriate local pathway is in place."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain IBS as a real, assessment-led diagnosis and still make room for new warning symptoms?","answer":"Explain the typical pattern and assessed findings, validate the symptoms and choose a focused practical trial. Explicitly reassess bleeding, weight loss or a changed pattern rather than assume all future symptoms are IBS.","checks":["Used a positive, respectful explanation","Matched treatment to the person and symptom pattern","Specified new-warning and planned-review actions"]}},{"id":"gi-rectal-bleeding","title":"Rectal bleeding and bowel change: explain FIT and close the referral loop","summary":"Assess urgency, use the current symptomatic pathway and explain a test result without false reassurance.","presentation":"A 56-year-old reports several weeks of rectal bleeding and a changed bowel habit. They assume it is piles and mention a previous negative screening test. They are currently stable in this scenario but need appropriate assessment and the symptomatic investigation/referral pathway.","assessment":["Assess current bleeding volume, haemodynamic symptoms, severe pain or other acute illness; significant ongoing bleeding, collapse or instability changes urgency.","Clarify duration, bowel pattern, pain, weight loss, anaemia symptoms, family/personal history and relevant medicines, including anticoagulants.","Offer an appropriate abdominal and anorectal assessment, explaining purpose, consent, dignity and chaperone arrangements; a mass or other concerning finding changes the pathway.","Distinguish symptomatic FIT from previous screening and explore the person’s fears, sample-collection barriers and ability to attend investigations."],"priorities":["Do not diagnose the cause as haemorrhoids from a brief account or a history of piles alone.","Make investigation, results review, referral and non-returned samples explicit and owned."],"management":[{"step":"1. Choose the right urgency","detail":"Manage significant acute bleeding or instability through the urgent/emergency pathway. For stable persistent symptoms, use current NG12 and local symptomatic lower-GI guidance."},{"step":"2. Arrange appropriate examination and tests","detail":"Assess the actual findings and relevant blood tests. Offer quantitative FIT when indicated by the symptomatic pathway, even if screening was previously negative. A rectal mass, unexplained anal mass or anal ulceration does not need FIT before referral is considered."},{"step":"3. Act on the FIT in context","detail":"NICE recommends suspected-cancer-pathway referral for adults with FIT at least 10 micrograms haemoglobin per gram of faeces. A result below that threshold is not an automatic dismissal of ongoing unexplained symptoms or strong clinical concern."},{"step":"4. Explain the purpose and next steps","detail":"Explain that FIT detects small amounts of blood, not the cause by itself. Discuss why further assessment/referral is recommended and how uncertainty will be resolved; do not announce a cancer diagnosis from FIT alone."},{"step":"5. Support completion and ownership","detail":"Check sample instructions, practical/cultural/privacy needs and return arrangements. Name who checks the result and acts on it; follow up non-returned samples or unresolved concerns rather than assume lack of interest."},{"step":"6. Confirm the referral and review plan","detail":"Check the referral is sent/received and provide an agreed contact/backup plan if an appointment or result is not received. Persistent concern may require referral despite a low/absent FIT; safety netting does not replace that decision."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"It is probably just piles, isn’t it?","response":"That is one possible cause, but the bleeding and changed bowel habit need a proper assessment. I do not want to assume the explanation before we have checked the relevant findings and tests.","why":"Acknowledge a possibility without prematurely closing the diagnosis."},{"cue":"My screening test was negative. Why repeat it?","response":"Screening looked at risk when that sample was taken. New symptoms need their own assessment. The test and examination we arrange now help us decide what should happen next.","why":"Distinguish screening from the current symptomatic pathway."},{"cue":"Does a positive FIT mean cancer?","response":"It means the test found blood at or above the level used for further investigation. It does not tell us the cause by itself. We should explain the result and arrange the appropriate next assessment.","why":"Explain “positive” without treating it as either good news or a confirmed diagnosis."},{"cue":"If the result is low, can I forget about the symptoms?","response":"A low result can be helpful, but it does not answer every question. If symptoms continue, change or remain concerning, we still need to review the whole picture and decide on further assessment.","why":"Avoid false reassurance and retain clinician responsibility."},{"cue":"I find collecting the sample embarrassing.","response":"Many people find it awkward. Could you tell me which part feels difficult? We can go through the practical steps and check what would make it manageable for you.","why":"Address the actual barrier without shame or assumptions."}],"variations":[{"label":"FIT is at least 10","change":"The quantitative symptomatic FIT reaches the referral threshold.","action":"Arrange the suspected-cancer pathway, explain what the result does and does not establish, and confirm referral ownership and the contact plan.","phrase":"The result means we should arrange further assessment through the urgent pathway. It does not by itself tell us the diagnosis."},{"label":"FIT is low but concern persists","change":"Symptoms continue, or examination/history raises strong concern despite a result below threshold.","action":"Reassess and do not delay appropriate secondary-care referral when clinical concern remains. A low result does not negate a mass, persistent unexplained symptoms or another important finding.","phrase":"The test is one part of the assessment. Your continuing symptoms mean we still need to act on the wider picture."},{"label":"The sample is not returned","change":"The patient has not completed FIT because of unclear instructions, disability, language or embarrassment.","action":"Contact and clarify the barrier, support completion and assess ongoing urgency. If concern is strong, do not let the missing sample delay an appropriate referral.","phrase":"It sounds as though the test arrangements were difficult. Let’s work out what would help and make sure your symptoms are still being assessed safely."}],"pitfalls":["Do not treat a previous screening result, a low symptomatic FIT or an anticoagulant prescription as a complete explanation for persistent bleeding.","Do not wait for FIT before considering referral for a rectal/unexplained anal mass or ulceration.","Do not send a test or referral without named follow-up and a plan for non-return or missed communication."],"safetyNet":["Heavy ongoing bleeding, collapse, marked faintness, severe pain or serious acute illness needs urgent/emergency assessment.","Persistent or worsening unexplained symptoms need review even after a low FIT; do not simply close the case because the result is below threshold.","Agree when results and referral information should be available and how to contact the team if not; the practice remains responsible for checking significant unresolved findings."],"followUp":"Track sample completion, result review and referral/action. Confirm how the patient will receive information and what to do for deterioration or missed contact. Continue assessment of persistent concern until a clinically appropriate explanation and plan are established.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["rectal bleeding","FIT","bowel change","colorectal referral","results ownership"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG12: suspected cancer","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12","use":"Current symptomatic lower-GI investigation and referral recommendations."},{"label":"NICE HTG690: FIT in primary care","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/htg690","use":"Thresholds, referral and safety-netting principles."},{"label":"NHS: bleeding from the bottom","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/symptoms/bleeding-from-the-bottom-rectal-bleeding/","use":"Patient explanation and urgent symptom advice."}],"retrieval":{"question":"What would you say after a low FIT if symptoms or examination remain concerning?","answer":"Explain that the result is one part of the assessment, review the continuing findings and arrange the appropriate referral/investigation when concern remains. Do not let a low or unreturned FIT delay action on strong clinical concern.","checks":["Interpreted the result in the clinical context","Specified actual referral or reassessment","Retained results/referral ownership and a contingency"]}},{"id":"gi-dyspepsia","title":"Dyspepsia: explain testing, a treatment trial and changed symptoms","summary":"Assess warning features, choose a proportionate test/treatment sequence and give a review plan.","presentation":"A 36-year-old describes recurrent upper abdominal discomfort and reflux symptoms. Appropriate assessment has not identified alarm features in this ordinary scenario. They buy acid-suppressing medicine intermittently and ask whether they need a scope or antibiotics.","assessment":["Clarify the symptom pattern, duration, swallowing difficulty, vomiting, weight loss, bleeding/black stools, anaemia symptoms and other concerning findings; examine as appropriate.","Consider cardiac, biliary and other causes rather than label every upper abdominal/chest symptom as acid reflux.","Review medicines, including NSAIDs/aspirin and acid suppression, prior H pylori testing/treatment, allergies, previous antibiotics, alcohol/smoking and other relevant context.","Explore what the person thinks is causing symptoms, their concern about cancer/testing and the effect on eating, sleep and daily activities."],"priorities":["Separate uncomplicated dyspepsia from bleeding, obstruction, possible ACS or a suspected-cancer pathway.","Make the test/treatment sequence, result ownership and reassessment triggers clear."],"management":[{"step":"1. Choose urgency from the actual assessment","detail":"Acute serious illness, suspected GI bleeding or possible ACS needs appropriate urgent/emergency care. Use current NG12 criteria for concerning swallowing/weight-loss and other findings rather than a routine empirical trial when referral is indicated."},{"step":"2. Agree a proportionate initial strategy","detail":"For uncomplicated dyspepsia, discuss H pylori test-and-treat and/or an appropriate empirical full-dose PPI trial (CG184 describes 4 weeks), selecting the sequence based on the clinical context and previous treatment."},{"step":"3. Prepare testing correctly","detail":"For a breath or stool antigen H pylori test, leave the recommended 2-week PPI washout when safe and clinically appropriate. Check recent antibiotics and the actual laboratory/test preparation requirements. Agree symptom support and timing rather than give an unsupported stop instruction."},{"step":"4. Act on a positive result using an appropriate regimen","detail":"If eradication is indicated, choose the current appropriate regimen considering allergy, previous antibiotic exposure, interactions and local resistance/prescribing guidance. Explain the actual course, adherence, harms and what to do if treatment is not tolerated."},{"step":"5. Review response and ongoing need","detail":"Review after the planned trial/result. Check actual use and reconsider diagnosis if symptoms persist or change. If ongoing acid suppression is appropriate, agree the lowest effective/appropriate regimen and review its indication, including any required gastroprotection."},{"step":"6. Close tests, referral and safety loops","detail":"Name who reviews the test, when the person should hear and what happens if symptoms continue. Retesting/further assessment depends on the indication, response and guideline pathway; do not leave treatment repeating indefinitely without review."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Does this mean I need an endoscopy?","response":"That depends on the symptoms, examination and any warning findings. Your assessment today allows us to discuss an initial test or treatment plan, but a change such as difficulty swallowing or concerning weight loss would change what we arrange.","why":"Explain why the decision is assessment-led and what would change it."},{"cue":"What is the H pylori test looking for?","response":"It looks for a bacterium that can contribute to some stomach problems, including ulcers. A positive result helps us decide whether eradication treatment is appropriate; it is not a cancer diagnosis.","why":"Explain purpose and limits without overclaiming the cause of every symptom."},{"cue":"Why do I need to pause the acid medicine for a test?","response":"It can affect how accurate the test is. We need the correct preparation period and a plan to manage symptoms safely while you prepare. Let’s check the instructions for the actual test and your other medicines.","why":"Make preparation practical and preserve clinical safety."},{"cue":"Can you give antibiotics now to be safe?","response":"Antibiotics help when there is an appropriate infection-related reason and the right regimen is selected. Let’s confirm the test and assessment plan rather than give a course that may not help and can cause side effects.","why":"Retain a clinically grounded decision while answering the request."},{"cue":"Will I be taking acid tablets forever?","response":"We’ll review how the planned course works and whether you still need treatment. Some people need ongoing medicine for a clear reason, and some can reduce it. We will decide from your response, risks and other medicines.","why":"Avoid promising either indefinite treatment or automatic stopping."}],"variations":[{"label":"Alarm features develop","change":"The person develops dysphagia, concerning weight loss, bleeding or another significant change.","action":"Reassess urgency and use the appropriate NG12/acute pathway. Do not simply increase the PPI or repeat antibiotics while delaying needed investigation.","phrase":"The new symptom changes what we need to check. We should arrange the appropriate assessment rather than only adjust the acid medicine."},{"label":"Test performed without adequate preparation","change":"A breath/stool test was taken during PPI use or too soon after a medicine that can affect the result.","action":"Interpret the result with the actual test conditions and arrange the correct preparation/retest plan when needed, keeping clinical warning features and urgency separate.","phrase":"The medicine can affect this result. Let’s check how the sample was taken and agree the correct next step."},{"label":"Symptoms persist after a reasonable initial trial","change":"There is incomplete response despite appropriate actual use and no new emergency feature.","action":"Review the diagnosis, treatment use, previous H pylori pathway and other causes; consider further investigation/referral according to guidance. Do not continue indefinite empirical escalation without reassessment.","phrase":"Let’s review what changed during the trial and whether we need a different explanation or investigation."}],"pitfalls":["Do not use an empirical PPI trial to delay action on dysphagia, bleeding, concerning weight loss or possible cardiac symptoms.","Do not interpret H pylori testing without the relevant medicine preparation or prescribe a universal eradication regimen regardless of allergy/exposure.","Do not stop a clinically needed protective medicine or continue long-term acid suppression without reviewing the actual indication."],"safetyNet":["Vomiting blood, black/tarry stools with illness, collapse, severe/worsening pain or possible cardiac symptoms needs appropriate urgent/emergency assessment.","New swallowing difficulty, unintentional weight loss, persistent vomiting or another significant change requires prompt reassessment and the relevant investigation/referral pathway.","Agree a definite result/treatment review and how to contact the team for intolerance, preparation problems or treatment failure."],"followUp":"Book review of the initial treatment/test and confirm ownership of results. Reassess persistent or changed symptoms and the ongoing medicine indication; arrange retesting/investigation/referral where indicated rather than leave open-ended repeats.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["dyspepsia","reflux","H pylori","PPI","endoscopy","test preparation"],"links":[{"label":"NICE CG184: dyspepsia and reflux","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg184","use":"Initial strategies, H pylori testing/treatment and review."},{"label":"NICE NG12: suspected cancer","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12","use":"Current upper-GI alarm/referral criteria."},{"label":"NHS: indigestion","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/indigestion/","use":"Patient explanation and concerning symptoms."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the H pylori test and PPI preparation while making it clear that warning symptoms change the plan?","answer":"Explain the bacterium/test purpose, check current medicines and arrange safe preparation with symptom support. Assess warning features separately and act through the appropriate acute/cancer pathway rather than wait for a routine test or empirical treatment response.","checks":["Explained test purpose and preparation","Kept urgent/referral findings separate","Named results/treatment review and a contingency"]}}]},{"id":"musculoskeletal","title":"Musculoskeletal","description":"Back pain/sciatica, gout and knee osteoarthritis: risk assessment, treatment explanations, function and changed circumstances, each with three variations.","icon":"08","reviewed":"2026-11-12","cases":[{"id":"msk-back-pain","title":"Low back pain: assess risk and build an active plan","summary":"Explain a positive plan without routine imaging or unsupported medicines, and recognise symptoms that change urgency.","presentation":"A 42-year-old has two weeks of low back pain after lifting. They are worried that movement will cause damage and ask for an MRI and stronger tablets. Appropriate history and examination have not identified a serious specific cause or neurological deficit in this ordinary scenario.","assessment":["Clarify onset, trauma, pain distribution, leg symptoms, neurological function and progression, previous episodes and the impact on movement, sleep, work and caring.","Assess for serious causes, including cauda equina/cord compression, infection, cancer, fracture and inflammatory disease. New or changed findings need reconsideration rather than automatic reuse of a previous mechanical label.","Ask clearly about bladder/bowel changes, altered saddle/genital sensation, sexual-function changes and leg weakness/numbness where relevant, and perform the appropriate examination. A remote account does not replace needed in-person assessment.","Review medical and medicine risks, actual analgesic use, fear of movement, expectations about scans and the practical support needed to keep active safely."],"priorities":["Choose the urgency before discussing routine back-pain care.","Offer a clear explanation, achievable activity and a review plan rather than a scan or prescription alone."],"management":[{"step":"1. Act on concerning findings","detail":"Arrange the appropriate urgent/emergency pathway when a serious cause is suspected. New bladder/bowel dysfunction, saddle/genital sensory loss, relevant new sexual-function changes or significant bilateral neurological symptoms need immediate assessment; do not wait for routine imaging or physiotherapy."},{"step":"2. Explain the working assessment","detail":"Relate the actual findings to the current working diagnosis and acknowledge the pain. Do not say that pain proves damage, or guarantee that every future symptom is harmless. Explain why the current assessment supports a non-emergency plan."},{"step":"3. Agree activity and support","detail":"Encourage continued normal activities as appropriate, avoiding prolonged bed rest. Agree manageable movement and return-to-work/daily-activity goals; tailor exercise support to needs, preferences and capabilities. If manual therapy is considered, it should be part of a package including exercise."},{"step":"4. Explain imaging appropriately","detail":"Do not routinely offer imaging in non-specialist care for low back pain with or without sciatica. Specialist imaging is considered when it is likely to change management. A patient request deserves an explanation and a positive plan, not a dismissive refusal."},{"step":"5. Choose medicines by indication and risk","detail":"If an oral NSAID is appropriate for low back pain, check gastrointestinal, renal, liver and cardiovascular risks, interactions and gastroprotection/monitoring needs; use the lowest effective dose for the shortest period. Do not offer paracetamol alone for low back pain or routine opioids; chronic low back pain should not be treated with opioids. Do not offer gabapentinoids for low back pain."},{"step":"6. Keep sciatica and existing medicine use distinct","detail":"For sciatica, NG59 advises against gabapentinoids/other antiepileptics, oral corticosteroids and benzodiazepines, and against opioids for chronic sciatica. Do not abruptly stop an existing dependence-forming medicine; review indication, risks and a suitable withdrawal plan separately."},{"step":"7. Agree review and earlier-help triggers","detail":"Set a review according to severity, function and the treatment trial. Reassess persistent or changed symptoms, barriers and the diagnosis. Make clear how to get urgent help and who reviews any medicine or referral."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Can I have an MRI to make sure nothing is damaged?","response":"A scan is useful when it is likely to change what we do. Based on the assessment today, a routine scan is not the next helpful step. Let’s go through the treatment and review plan, and the changes that would mean we need a different assessment.","why":"Explain the reason and offer an active plan instead of only refusing a request."},{"cue":"Should I stay in bed until the pain is completely gone?","response":"Long periods in bed can make it harder to get moving again. Let’s choose manageable activities and build up in a way that fits your symptoms and the assessment. We will also agree what would need review sooner.","why":"Support function without telling the person to push through every symptom."},{"cue":"Does pain mean I am damaging my back?","response":"Pain can be very real and limiting without telling us exactly how much tissue damage there is. We use the history and examination to assess what needs checking and which activities are appropriate.","why":"Avoid both catastrophic language and unsupported reassurance."},{"cue":"My friend was given pregabalin for sciatica. Why not me?","response":"I understand why you ask. Current guidance does not recommend pregabalin for sciatica because the expected benefit does not outweigh the harms. I would rather agree a treatment and support plan that is appropriate for your assessment.","why":"Explain an evidence-led recommendation and a constructive alternative."},{"cue":"I cannot go straight back to lifting at work.","response":"Let’s look at what your job involves and what you can manage safely now. We can discuss suitable temporary adjustments and a review rather than assume the only choices are full lifting or complete rest.","why":"Turn return to activity into a feasible individual arrangement."}],"variations":[{"label":"New bladder or saddle symptoms","change":"Back/leg symptoms are joined by new difficulty passing urine, loss of bladder/bowel control, altered genital/saddle sensation or significant bilateral neurological symptoms.","action":"Arrange immediate emergency assessment through the appropriate pathway for possible serious neurological compression. Do not wait for routine MRI, a GP follow-up slot or physiotherapy.","phrase":"The new symptoms change the urgency. You need emergency assessment now rather than waiting for the routine back-pain plan."},{"label":"NSAID risk is high","change":"The person has CKD, previous GI bleeding, anticoagulation or another important medicine risk.","action":"Review the individual risks and actual medicines before prescribing. Do not automatically add an oral NSAID; use an appropriate alternative/support plan and seek prescribing advice when needed.","phrase":"Before we choose pain medicine, I need to check what is safe with your kidneys, other conditions and current treatment."},{"label":"Already taking long-term opioids or gabapentinoids","change":"The patient is using a dependence-forming medicine long term with little benefit and asks whether to stop today.","action":"Review the indication, benefit, harm and dependence/withdrawal risk. Explain the current recommendation and agree an individual review/withdrawal plan rather than abrupt unsupervised stopping.","phrase":"Let’s review what the medicine is doing for you and how to make any change safely. Please do not make a sudden change without an agreed plan."}],"pitfalls":["Do not use a previous mechanical diagnosis or a routine scan request to distract from new neurological or systemic warning findings.","Do not equate a positive plan with bed rest, escalating opioids or gabapentinoids.","Do not promise imaging or a specific recovery deadline without the actual clinical reason; do not abruptly stop dependence-forming medicines."],"safetyNet":["New bladder/bowel or genital/saddle sensory changes, serious bilateral leg neurological symptoms or other suspected compression needs immediate emergency assessment; do not drive yourself if emergency transfer is needed.","Serious trauma, acute systemic illness or suspected cancer/infection/fracture needs the appropriate urgent pathway.","Persistent deterioration, new weakness or a changed pattern needs reassessment; agree a contact route and review instead of only “if worse”."],"followUp":"Agree a review based on function, severity and any medicine trial, including adverse-effect checks. If not improving, revisit the diagnosis, examination and support rather than automatically repeat the same prescription. Track any referral and maintain an earlier-help plan.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["back pain","sciatica","MRI","activity","cauda equina","NSAID","medicine review"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG59: low back pain and sciatica","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng59","use":"Current assessment, imaging, activity and medicine recommendations, including the 2026 amendments."},{"label":"NHS: back pain","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/back-pain/","use":"Patient explanations, activity and emergency warning symptoms."},{"label":"NICE NG215: dependence and withdrawal","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng215","use":"Safe individual review of existing dependence-forming medicines."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain why a routine MRI is not the next step while still offering a clear, safe back-pain plan?","answer":"Connect the actual assessment to the recommendation, explain that imaging is useful when it changes management, agree activity/support and appropriate medicine checks, and name review and warning symptoms. A new neurological or systemic warning finding changes the pathway immediately.","checks":["Linked the recommendation to assessment rather than dismissal","Offered practical activity/support and medicine-risk checks","Specified review ownership and emergency changes"]}},{"id":"msk-gout","title":"Gout: treat the flare and explain long-term prevention","summary":"Separate acute relief from urate lowering, consider comorbidity and make treat-to-target follow-up understandable.","presentation":"A 58-year-old has recurrent clinically assessed gout affecting the big toe. The current presentation has been assessed without evidence of joint infection in this fictional ordinary scenario. They want rapid relief and ask whether preventive tablets can be stopped once the pain settles.","assessment":["Assess the red, swollen joint and overall illness; consider septic arthritis, CPPD and other inflammatory causes rather than assume every hot joint is gout. Suspected septic arthritis requires immediate referral via the local pathway.","Clarify the pattern, number and severity of flares, tophi, kidney disease/stones, medicines including diuretics, cardiovascular disease, previous treatment and functional impact.","Use serum urate in the clinical context. If below 360 micromol/litre during a flare but gout remains strongly suspected, repeat at least two weeks after the flare settles; consider aspiration or appropriate imaging when diagnosis remains uncertain.","Check renal function, co-prescriptions, allergy and other treatment risks before choosing flare or urate-lowering medicines. Explore beliefs, barriers and what the person means by “prevention”."],"priorities":["Exclude a hot-joint emergency before routine gout treatment.","Explain the different jobs of flare treatment and long-term urate lowering, with a monitored plan for each."],"management":[{"step":"1. Confirm urgency and the diagnostic basis","detail":"Assess for joint infection and other important causes. Arrange immediate referral if septic arthritis is suspected; do not delay for routine urate testing or treat presumed gout while infection remains unaddressed."},{"step":"2. Treat the assessed flare appropriately","detail":"Choose an NSAID, colchicine or a short course of oral corticosteroid according to comorbidities, co-prescriptions and preference. Check the actual dose/product, renal function, contraindications and interactions; consider gastroprotection when indicated. Do not offer the same regimen to everyone."},{"step":"3. Explain and offer prevention when indicated","detail":"Offer treat-to-target urate-lowering therapy for multiple/troublesome flares, CKD stages 3–5, diuretic therapy, tophi or chronic gouty arthritis. Discuss the option after other first/subsequent flares too. Explain that ULT is usually continued after the target is reached and is typically lifelong."},{"step":"4. Choose the start and medicine safely","detail":"NG219 recommends starting ULT at least 2–4 weeks after a flare has settled; it can start during a flare when flares are more frequent, with appropriate prevention discussion. Allopurinol or febuxostat can be first-line depending on context; use allopurinol first-line for major cardiovascular disease. Check the full prescribing reference and patient-specific risks."},{"step":"5. Titrate to a measured target","detail":"Start ULT at a low dose and use monthly serum urate to guide tolerated increases until target. Aim below 360 micromol/litre; consider below 300 for tophi/chronic gouty arthritis or continuing frequent flares despite reaching below 360. Explain that the blood test guides dose rather than pain alone."},{"step":"6. Discuss flare prevention during titration","detail":"Discuss benefits/harms of flare prophylaxis. If chosen, offer colchicine while reaching target, with suitable alternatives when contraindicated, not tolerated or ineffective. Check interactions and renal safety; do not mistake prophylaxis for the long-term urate-lowering medicine itself."},{"step":"7. Review after the flare and maintain follow-up","detail":"Review response, serum urate when appropriate, lifestyle and comorbidity, medicines and the ULT decision. Consider annual urate monitoring once target is reached on ongoing ULT, with earlier assessment for problems. Advice should support health without blaming the person or promising diet alone will prevent every flare."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Why take another tablet when the pain has gone?","response":"The flare medicine treats the current inflammation and pain. Urate-lowering treatment has a different job: reducing urate over time to lower the chance of future attacks and joint problems. We adjust it using blood tests, not just whether today’s toe hurts.","why":"Separate acute relief from the preventive treatment purpose."},{"cue":"Can I stop the preventive medicine once the blood result is normal?","response":"Reaching the target tells us the treatment is doing its job. It usually needs to continue to keep urate down. Let’s discuss the long-term plan, monitoring and any concerns before making a change.","why":"Explain why a good result is not an automatic stop signal."},{"cue":"Could the preventive tablet make attacks happen at first?","response":"Flares can occur while urate-lowering treatment is being started or adjusted. We should discuss whether suitable temporary flare-prevention treatment would help, what side effects to watch for and how to contact us.","why":"Prepare the person for a recognised barrier to adherence without promising a flare-free start."},{"cue":"Is this all my fault because of food?","response":"Gout has several contributing factors, including how the body handles urate and other health conditions. We can discuss helpful lifestyle changes without blaming you, and decide whether medicine is needed to prevent further problems.","why":"Reduce shame and avoid claiming that a restrictive diet alone replaces indicated treatment."},{"cue":"I have kidney problems. Can I take the usual tablets?","response":"That affects what is safe and how we prescribe it. I need to check your kidney function, other medicines and the specific treatment before agreeing the dose and monitoring.","why":"Make comorbidity part of the actual prescribing decision."}],"variations":[{"label":"Hot joint with possible infection","change":"The presentation includes a rapidly worsening hot swollen joint and systemic illness, or infection remains a meaningful concern.","action":"Refer immediately through the local septic-arthritis pathway. Do not use a gout history, raised urate or a presumed flare response to rule infection out.","phrase":"A joint infection is one of the important possibilities here. We need urgent specialist assessment now rather than assume this is another gout flare."},{"label":"Urate is low during an apparent flare","change":"The serum urate is below 360 micromol/litre during the acute episode but the clinical suspicion remains strong.","action":"Keep the differential open. Repeat serum urate at least two weeks after the flare settles; use aspiration or appropriate further assessment if the diagnosis is uncertain.","phrase":"This result during an attack does not settle the question on its own. We should review the whole picture and arrange the right repeat or further test."},{"label":"CKD and major cardiovascular disease","change":"Recurrent gout occurs with kidney disease and a history of myocardial infarction.","action":"Individualise flare medicines, ULT starting dose/titration and prophylaxis. NG219 recommends allopurinol first-line when starting ULT in major cardiovascular disease. Monitor to target and check the full prescribing guidance rather than reuse a standard regimen.","phrase":"Your kidney and heart history affect which treatment is suitable. We can choose a careful starting plan and use monitoring to adjust it safely."}],"pitfalls":["Do not assume every hot joint is gout or wait for routine blood results if infection is suspected.","Do not confuse flare treatment, flare prophylaxis and urate lowering, or prescribe without renal/interaction checks.","Do not stop preventive treatment just because pain settles or target urate is reached; review with the person."],"safetyNet":["A worsening hot swollen joint, systemic illness or suspected infection needs immediate reassessment/referral according to severity.","Discuss the actual medicine’s important adverse effects and the action/contact route, including urgent help for serious reactions.","Agree what to do for continuing pain, treatment intolerance or a flare during titration; do not leave the person to abandon or change medicines without advice."],"followUp":"Review after the flare and agree prevention. When starting/titrating ULT, use monthly urate-guided dose review until target, with safety checks and suitable flare prophylaxis discussion. Consider annual serum urate once target is reached on continuing ULT; review earlier when clinically needed.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["gout","hot joint","urate","allopurinol","CKD","flare","septic arthritis"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG219: gout diagnosis and management","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng219","use":"Flare assessment, treatment choices, ULT indications, targets and monitoring."},{"label":"NHS: gout","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/gout/","use":"Patient explanations and help-seeking advice."},{"label":"NHS: septic arthritis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/septic-arthritis/","use":"The importance of urgent assessment of possible joint infection."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain the three different roles of flare treatment, temporary flare prevention and long-term urate lowering?","answer":"Treat the assessed flare with a suitable anti-inflammatory option. Discuss temporary prophylaxis while starting/titrating ULT when chosen. Explain that long-term ULT lowers urate to a measured target and usually continues after symptoms and blood results improve. Check the diagnostic basis, comorbidity and monitoring.","checks":["Distinguished the three medicine purposes","Included infection, renal and interaction checks","Explained target-guided titration and ongoing review"]}},{"id":"msk-knee-oa","title":"Knee osteoarthritis: movement, pain support and referral","summary":"Make a clinical diagnosis where appropriate, explain exercise and medicine choices, and consider referral from symptoms and function.","presentation":"A 67-year-old describes gradual activity-related knee pain, brief morning stiffness and difficulty walking to the shops. The assessment fits knee osteoarthritis without atypical or acute inflammatory features in this ordinary scenario. They fear that exercise will wear the knee out faster and ask whether their BMI rules out referral.","assessment":["Clarify pain pattern, duration, morning stiffness, swelling, injury, locking/instability and the effect on walking, sleep, work and valued activities. Examine appropriately.","Consider alternatives when symptoms or examination are atypical, including a hot swollen joint, marked/prolonged morning stiffness, rapid deterioration, trauma or systemic illness. Do not force every older person’s knee pain into an OA label.","NG226 supports clinical diagnosis without imaging in people aged at least 45 with activity-related pain and no morning stiffness or stiffness lasting no longer than 30 minutes. Routine imaging is not needed unless atypical findings suggest another/additional diagnosis.","Review previous exercise/support, treatment actually used, medical/medicine risks, weight-management needs if relevant and the person’s goals and beliefs about movement and surgery."],"priorities":["Base treatment on symptoms, function and preference, not an X-ray grade or a dismissive “wear and tear” label.","Offer practical therapeutic exercise and support, then appropriate medicine/referral choices with review."],"management":[{"step":"1. Explain the assessment and goals","detail":"Describe OA in accessible language, acknowledge pain and variable symptoms, and agree a meaningful function goal. Routine imaging does not improve a typical clinical diagnosis; atypical features need a different assessment."},{"step":"2. Offer tailored therapeutic exercise","detail":"Offer suitable local strengthening and general aerobic activity; consider supervised sessions. Explain that some initial discomfort can occur and regular, consistent exercise can improve pain/function. Tailor the starting level and support; a new hot joint or major change requires reassessment rather than “push through”."},{"step":"3. Discuss weight support when appropriate","detail":"If overweight/obesity is relevant, explain how supported weight management may improve symptoms and health and agree a respectful, feasible plan. Avoid blame or make weight loss a prerequisite for all care."},{"step":"4. Choose pain support alongside exercise","detail":"Offer a topical NSAID for knee OA when pharmacological treatment is needed, checking suitability. If ineffective or unsuitable, consider an oral NSAID only after GI, renal, liver, cardiovascular and co-prescription risk checks; offer gastroprotection with an oral NSAID. Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time."},{"step":"5. Explain limits of other medicines and injections","detail":"Do not routinely offer paracetamol or weak opioids; NG226 limits their use to infrequent short-term situations when other pharmacological options are unsuitable/ineffective. Do not offer strong opioids or glucosamine. Consider an intra-articular corticosteroid when other medicines are ineffective/unsuitable or to support exercise, explaining short-term relief of about 2–10 weeks."},{"step":"6. Consider referral from the clinical picture","detail":"Consider joint-replacement referral when pain, stiffness, reduced function or deformity substantially affects quality of life and non-surgical care is ineffective or unsuitable. Do not exclude referral solely because of age, sex/gender, smoking, comorbidity or BMI; discuss how individual risks affect surgical decisions."},{"step":"7. Agree review that fits the need","detail":"Patient-initiated follow-up suits many people, while a planned review may be needed for treatment monitoring, severe limitation, support needs or preference. Name what improvement should look like, adverse-effect checks and how to return if the plan is insufficient."}],"discussion":[{"cue":"Isn’t exercise wearing the knee out even more?","response":"It is understandable to worry about that. Appropriate exercise can help the muscles support the joint and improve function. We can choose a manageable starting level and build it up, with advice about what discomfort to expect and what would need review.","why":"Challenge the damage assumption without denying pain or ignoring a changed presentation."},{"cue":"If you do not X-ray it, how can you know?","response":"A typical pattern of symptoms and examination can be enough to make the diagnosis. We use imaging when the findings are unusual or it would help answer a different question, rather than order it routinely for everyone.","why":"Explain the clinical basis and when the imaging decision changes."},{"cue":"Could an injection fix it permanently?","response":"A steroid injection may give short-term relief in an appropriate situation, sometimes to help you engage with exercise. It does not permanently repair the joint, so we should discuss its purpose, risks and what comes afterwards.","why":"Avoid promising a cure and place the intervention in the overall plan."},{"cue":"I was told my weight means I can never be referred.","response":"Weight can affect symptoms and surgical risks, and we can offer support. It should not by itself rule out referral. We should consider how much the knee affects your life and whether suitable non-surgical care has helped, then discuss the options and individual risks.","why":"Separate risk discussion from an automatic access barrier."},{"cue":"What is a realistic first goal?","response":"What activity would you most like to do more comfortably? Let’s choose one goal, agree the exercise and pain-support plan, and decide how we will judge whether it is helping.","why":"Use the patient’s valued function rather than an abstract pain score alone."}],"variations":[{"label":"The knee becomes hot and acutely swollen","change":"New marked warmth/swelling, severe restriction or systemic illness appears.","action":"Reassess urgently for infection and other acute/inflammatory causes. Suspected septic arthritis needs immediate referral; do not treat this as an ordinary OA flare or inject before infection is addressed.","phrase":"This new swelling changes the assessment. We need to check for an acute cause rather than assume it is the same osteoarthritis pain."},{"label":"Comorbidity makes oral NSAIDs risky","change":"There is CKD, anticoagulation, previous GI bleeding or significant cardiovascular risk.","action":"Review suitability of all treatments and the actual co-prescriptions. Do not add an oral NSAID automatically or assume topical medicines have no precautions; optimise appropriate non-drug/topical support and consider further options/referral.","phrase":"We need pain support that fits safely with your other treatment. Let’s check the risks before adding another medicine."},{"label":"Quality of life remains substantially affected","change":"Suitable non-surgical options are ineffective or unsuitable and walking, sleep or daily life remains severely limited.","action":"Discuss joint-replacement referral using clinical assessment and shared decisions. Do not apply an automatic age/BMI/comorbidity exclusion; explain individual risks and what the specialist assessment involves.","phrase":"The knee is still having a major effect on your life despite the care we have tried. It is reasonable to discuss a specialist opinion about the next options."}],"pitfalls":["Do not dismiss pain as inevitable ageing or use a scan grade as the whole management decision.","Do not prescribe escalating opioids, routine paracetamol or glucosamine instead of evidence-based support.","Do not promise permanent benefit from a steroid injection or deny referral solely because of BMI, age or another listed factor."],"safetyNet":["An acutely hot swollen knee, systemic illness or suspected infection needs urgent assessment, with immediate referral for suspected septic arthritis.","New trauma, rapid deterioration or atypical features requires reassessment rather than automatic repeat OA care.","Explain adverse effects and contact arrangements for the actual medicine/injection; review an ineffective or intolerable plan."],"followUp":"Agree patient-initiated or planned review according to individual needs, treatment monitoring and preference. Review function goals, exercise access, pain support, adverse effects and whether referral is now appropriate. Track any referral and agree a contact plan.","reviewed":"2026-11-12","tags":["osteoarthritis","knee pain","exercise","NSAID","joint replacement","BMI","steroid injection"],"links":[{"label":"NICE NG226: osteoarthritis","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng226","use":"Clinical diagnosis, therapeutic exercise, medicines, injections and referral criteria."},{"label":"NHS: osteoarthritis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/osteoarthritis/","use":"Accessible patient information about symptoms and treatment."},{"label":"NHS: septic arthritis","url":"https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/septic-arthritis/","use":"Urgent assessment of a possible joint infection."}],"retrieval":{"question":"How would you explain an exercise-led knee OA plan while answering concerns about imaging, pain relief and possible referral?","answer":"Link the typical assessed pattern to a clinical diagnosis, offer tailored therapeutic exercise and appropriate support, choose medicines after risk checks and explain their limits. Use symptoms/function and response to suitable non-surgical care to discuss referral, without an automatic BMI/age exclusion. Reassess an acutely hot joint.","checks":["Explained diagnosis and a practical exercise/function goal","Included medicine risk and realistic intervention benefit","Specified review, changed symptoms and fair referral discussion"]}}]}];
